const QUESTIONS = [{"id":1,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A major European airline wants to identify distinct groups of frequent flyers based on booking patterns, flight routes, spending, and loyalty tier usage — without any predefined segment definitions. The marketing VP asks a junior data scientist to build a logistic regression model for this task. The VP's request reveals a fundamental misunderstanding. The specific paradigm mismatch AND the correct approach category are:","options":["A) Logistic regression can only handle numeric features, but booking patterns and flight routes contain categorical data that cannot be encoded numerically without losing essential information about the segments","B) Logistic regression requires a minimum sample size of 10 observations per feature to produce stable coefficient estimates, and the airline's feature count likely exceeds this threshold given the number of route combinations","C) Logistic regression produces linear boundaries between groups, which cannot capture the complex nonlinear patterns in multi-dimensional booking behavior that the airline needs to identify meaningful customer segments","D) Logistic regression requires predefined labeled categories as target output, but this task has no predefined segments, making it an unsupervised problem; clustering methods like K-means or DBSCAN are appropriate instead","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The core issue is paradigm mismatch: discovering groups WITHOUT predefined labels is unsupervised learning. Logistic regression is supervised — it needs labeled target categories. K-means, DBSCAN, or hierarchical clustering are appropriate. B is wrong: logistic regression handles categorical features via encoding. C describes a real limitation but misses the fundamental issue. D invents a sample size rule."},
{"id":2,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A hospital has labeled patient records with diagnoses and wants to (1) predict disease risk and (2) discover hidden symptom patterns without using labels. A colleague says both tasks are supervised since labels exist in the dataset. Why is this flawed?","options":["A) The hospital should use semi-supervised learning which automatically determines when to use labels and when to ignore them based on the statistical properties of each feature","B) Having labels in the dataset does not force every task to use them — task two deliberately ignores the labels to discover hidden structure, making it unsupervised learning","C) Disease prediction should use reinforcement learning where sequential patient outcomes serve as the reward signal, not supervised learning with static diagnostic labels","D) Unsupervised methods require datasets that contain absolutely no label columns in the data storage format, so the labels must be physically deleted before running task two","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The presence of labels doesn’t force all tasks to be supervised. Task 1 uses labels (supervised), task 2 deliberately ignores them (unsupervised). B mischaracterizes reinforcement learning. C is wrong — unsupervised methods can run on datasets containing label columns. D describes a real technique but doesn’t address the logical flaw."},
{"id":3,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"Per the scikit-learn algorithm cheat sheet from the course: 100,000+ labeled samples, predicting a category, Linear SVC did not work. What does the flowchart recommend next?","options":["A) Abandon the classification framing entirely and reframe the problem as a regression task, predicting a continuous probability score for each possible category independently","B) Switch directly to a deep neural network architecture since the dataset is large enough to support the parameter count needed by modern deep learning model architectures","C) Revert to the KNeighbors classifier which makes no distributional assumptions about the data and typically works well when linear methods fail on complex classification tasks","D) Apply kernel approximation techniques first, and if those also underperform, move to ensemble classifiers that can capture more complex nonlinear decision boundary patterns","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The scikit-learn cheat sheet flow: for >100K samples, try SGD/Linear SVC first, then kernel approximation, then ensemble classifiers. B skips intermediate steps. C (KNN) is recommended for smaller datasets. D is a misunderstanding — predicting class probabilities is still classification."},
{"id":4,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A company detects fraud using well-defined, stable rules that rarely change. An executive pushes for an ML replacement. A senior engineer argues against it. Who is making the stronger technical argument?","options":["A) The executive is right: ML always outperforms rule-based systems because it can discover additional hidden patterns that hand-crafted rules will inevitably miss over time","B) The engineer is right: when patterns are well-defined and stable, rule-based systems are simpler, more transparent, and easier to maintain without adding ML complexity","C) The executive is right: current regulatory requirements mandate that all financial fraud detection systems must incorporate machine learning components for compliance purposes","D) The engineer is right: ML fundamentally cannot be applied to fraud detection because the severe class imbalance between fraudulent and legitimate transactions prevents learning","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"When rules are clear and stable, ML adds unnecessary complexity, opacity, and maintenance burden without benefit. B is false as a universal claim. C is wrong — ML handles class imbalance through various techniques. D is fabricated — no such regulation exists."},
{"id":5,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"A real estate model: price = θ0 + 350·area − 15000·bedrooms. A colleague claims the negative bedroom coefficient proves that adding bedrooms decreases home value. Is this interpretation correct?","options":["A) Yes: the coefficient directly tells us that each additional bedroom reduces the property value by exactly fifteen thousand dollars regardless of all other model features","B) No: negative coefficients in multiple regression always indicate measurement error in the corresponding feature rather than reflecting any genuine causal or associative relationship","C) No: the negative sign is an artifact of the gradient descent optimization algorithm converging to a local minimum rather than reflecting the true underlying data relationship","D) No: area and bedrooms are correlated, so the coefficient shows the effect of adding bedrooms while holding area constant — same area but more bedrooms means smaller rooms","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In multiple regression, coefficients represent marginal effects holding other variables constant. Since area and bedrooms are correlated, more bedrooms at the same area = smaller rooms, which may decrease value. B is wrong — negative coefficients can be meaningful. C ignores the ceteris paribus interpretation. D is wrong — MSE is convex for linear regression."},
{"id":6,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"You have 500 samples and 10,000 features. A colleague suggests the Normal Equation for linear regression. What is the primary computational concern with this specific dataset?","options":["A) The Normal Equation requires loading all feature columns into memory simultaneously, which is infeasible when there are 10,000 columns regardless of available system resources","B) The Normal Equation assumes all features are uncorrelated with each other, which is extremely unlikely to hold with 10,000 features, causing numerically unstable coefficient estimates","C) The Normal Equation requires inverting a 10,000×10,000 matrix with O(N³) complexity, making the computation prohibitively expensive when the feature count is this large","D) The Normal Equation can only handle datasets where the sample count exceeds the feature count, so it would fail entirely and produce undefined results on this dataset","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The Normal Equation computes θ = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy, requiring inversion of a features×features matrix. At 10,000 features, that’s O(10¹²) — computationally prohibitive. B is partially true (XᵀX may be singular) but pseudoinverse handles it. C conflates assumptions. D is misleading — the bottleneck is matrix inversion, not loading."},
{"id":7,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"A degree-15 polynomial is fit to 50 data points. Training MSE is 0.001 while test MSE is 45,000. A colleague says polynomial regression is inherently nonlinear and therefore cannot generalize. Is this diagnosis correct?","options":["A) No: the model underfits because a degree-15 polynomial is not flexible enough to capture the true signal, and increasing the degree further would reduce the test error","B) Yes: polynomial features above degree five are numerically unstable and produce unreliable coefficient estimates regardless of how much training data is available to the model","C) No: the model overfits because degree-15 has too many parameters for 50 points, memorizing noise; polynomial regression is still linear in its coefficients and parameters","D) Yes: polynomial terms like x² and x³ fundamentally change the optimization landscape, creating multiple local minima in the cost function that prevent finding good solutions","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Polynomial regression IS linear in coefficients (it’s linear regression with transformed features). The problem is classic overfitting — too many parameters for too few points. B is wrong: MSE remains convex. C is absurd given near-zero training error. D overgeneralizes about numerical stability."},
{"id":8,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"Degree-4 and degree-5 polynomials both achieve R²=0.861. A simpler exponential model achieves R²=0.854. A colleague recommends degree-5 since it has the most parameters. Why is this recommendation problematic?","options":["A) Adding a parameter that contributes zero R² improvement only increases overfitting risk without benefit, while the simpler exponential model performs nearly as well with less complexity","B) R² values cannot be meaningfully compared across different model families because each family type defines explained variance using a fundamentally different baseline calculation method","C) The degree-5 polynomial introduces multicollinearity among the polynomial terms like x and x² and x⁵, which completely invalidates the R² metric for model comparison purposes","D) Nonlinear models like the exponential function always generalize better than polynomial models when training R² values are similar because they impose stronger structural priors on the data","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Zero R² gain from degree 4→5 means the extra parameter adds overfitting risk without benefit. By Occam’s razor, prefer the simpler exponential (R²=0.854, nearly as good). B is a misconception — R² uses the same baseline. C is wrong about invalidation. D overgeneralizes."},
{"id":9,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"Feature ‘income’ ranges 20,000–500,000, ‘age’ ranges 18–80. Gradient descent converges very slowly. A colleague suggests switching to the Normal Equation to avoid the scaling issue entirely. Is this the best fix?","options":["A) The colleague is correct: switching to the Normal Equation is always preferable to gradient descent when convergence is slow because it is strictly more computationally efficient","B) Neither approach will work because features with vastly different scales violate the assumptions of linear regression, and the model must be entirely reformulated from scratch","C) The Normal Equation is the best fix because it finds the exact analytical solution in one step, while gradient descent with any learning rate produces only approximate results","D) The Normal Equation sidesteps scaling but is suboptimal — feature scaling makes gradient descent converge fast and remains practical for any dataset size unlike the Normal Equation","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Feature scaling (standardization/normalization) makes GD converge fast by creating circular contours. The Normal Equation avoids this but is O(N³) for features — impractical for large feature sets. B is wrong: GD with scaling reaches the same solution. C is false. D is wrong for large feature counts."},
{"id":10,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"Surgery duration model: duration = 45 + 0.8·age + 12·complexity − 5·minimally_invasive. For a 60-year-old patient, complexity=3, minimally invasive surgery. What is the predicted duration in minutes?","options":["A) 128 minutes, calculated as 45 + 48 + 36 − 5 = 124 but with a standard clinical rounding correction factor of positive four minutes added to the final prediction","B) 130 minutes, calculated as 45 + 0.8(60) + 12(3) − 5(0) since minimally_invasive equals zero when the procedure actually uses the minimally invasive surgical technique","C) 88 minutes, calculated as 45 + 0.8(60) − 12(3) + 5(1) since higher complexity reduces duration while the invasiveness indicator adds time to the procedure estimate","D) 133 minutes, calculated as 45 + 0.8(60) + 12(3) + 5(1) since the minimally invasive indicator should be added rather than subtracted in the final prediction calculation","E) None of the above"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Correct: 45 + 0.8(60) + 12(3) − 5(1) = 45 + 48 + 36 − 5 = 124 minutes. No option matches. A invents a rounding correction. B incorrectly sets minimally_invasive=0 when the patient IS having minimally invasive surgery (should be 1). C reverses signs. D adds instead of subtracting."},
{"id":11,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Batch gradient descent on a linear regression problem shows the cost oscillating wildly without converging. A colleague claims the oscillation proves there are local minima in the MSE cost function. Is this valid?","options":["A) No: MSE for linear regression is convex with a single global minimum; the oscillation is caused by a learning rate too large, which overshoots the minimum on each iteration","B) Yes: even convex functions can develop local minima when the number of features exceeds a certain threshold, which causes the observed oscillation pattern in the cost values","C) No: the oscillation is perfectly normal behavior for batch gradient descent and will eventually settle down after enough iterations regardless of the learning rate value chosen","D) Yes: while pure MSE is convex, the presence of feature interactions and polynomial terms in the data creates a non-convex landscape with local minima causing oscillation","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"MSE for linear regression is ALWAYS convex — one global minimum, no local minima, regardless of feature count. Oscillation = learning rate too large. B and D are incorrect about convexity. C is wrong: an overly large learning rate can cause divergence, not eventual convergence."},
{"id":12,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"A neural network converges to loss=0.45 (local minimum) instead of the global minimum at ~0.15. Switching from batch GD to SGD sometimes reaches 0.20. Why does SGD help, and does it guarantee the global minimum?","options":["A) SGD is mathematically guaranteed to find the global minimum given enough iterations because the random sampling eventually explores the entire parameter space exhaustively over time","B) SGD implicitly applies L2 regularization through the gradient noise, which smooths the cost function landscape and eliminates the local minima that trapped batch gradient descent","C) SGD’s noisy single-sample gradient estimates can knock the optimizer out of shallow local minima, but it provides no guarantee of finding the global minimum of the cost function","D) SGD computes more accurate gradients by focusing on one sample at a time instead of averaging across the entire noisy dataset, leading to more precise optimization steps overall","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"SGD’s noise is a feature for non-convex problems — it can bump the optimizer out of shallow local minima. But NO guarantee of global minimum (C is wrong). B inverts reality: SGD has LESS accurate gradients. D is creative but incorrect — noise doesn’t eliminate local minima."},
{"id":13,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"10 million samples. A colleague chooses SGD (batch size 1) over mini-batch GD (batch size 256), arguing SGD is faster since it processes less data per step. Who is making the better engineering decision?","options":["A) The colleague is correct: SGD with batch size one always converges faster than any mini-batch size because it performs more parameter updates per epoch of training data processed","B) The colleague is correct: SGD’s noisy gradients serve as implicit regularization, so it not only trains faster but also produces models that generalize better on unseen test data","C) Neither is optimal: batch gradient descent using all ten million samples per step would converge in the fewest total steps, making it fastest despite the per-step computation cost","D) Mini-batch is better: it benefits from GPU parallelism and produces more stable gradient estimates, making it converge faster overall despite processing more data per step","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Mini-batch (e.g., 256) balances: stable gradient estimates, GPU parallelism, and practical convergence speed. SGD is noisy and doesn’t parallelize well. B ignores convergence quality. C is wrong: batch GD on 10M samples per step is too slow. D overstates SGD’s benefits."},
{"id":14,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"In the ‘Mount Errorest’ lecture analogy, you’re in dense fog on a mountain, can only feel the local slope, and take very tiny steps. A colleague says this represents getting stuck in a local minimum. What’s the correct interpretation?","options":["A) Tiny steps correspond to a learning rate that is too small, causing extremely slow convergence to the minimum rather than getting stuck in a suboptimal local minimum location","B) The colleague is right: in the fog analogy, tiny steps cause you to settle into the first depression you encounter, which directly corresponds to the local minimum trapping problem","C) The colleague is right: the fog represents noise in the training data, and tiny steps mean the model is too cautious to escape the noise-induced local minimum effectively","D) Tiny steps correspond to the vanishing gradient problem where the slope itself becomes too flat for the optimizer to detect any meaningful direction of descent at all","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Tiny steps = too-small learning rate = painfully slow convergence, not getting stuck. You’ll eventually reach the minimum but it takes forever. B confuses step size with cost function shape. C describes vanishing gradients (different issue). D misinterprets the fog metaphor."},
{"id":15,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Linear regression’s MSE cost function is convex. A colleague claims this means gradient descent always converges to the optimal solution regardless of the learning rate chosen. What nuance is the colleague missing?","options":["A) Convexity guarantees convergence only when the number of training samples exceeds the number of features; otherwise the cost surface develops flat regions preventing convergence","B) Convexity guarantees a unique global minimum exists but NOT convergence for any learning rate — a rate that is too large causes divergence even on perfectly convex cost functions","C) Convexity only holds when features are standardized; with raw feature scales the cost function can become non-convex due to the elongated contour shapes in parameter space","D) Convexity guarantees convergence to the optimum only when using batch gradient descent; stochastic and mini-batch variants lose the mathematical convexity convergence guarantee","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Convexity ensures one global minimum but an excessively large learning rate causes divergence (growing oscillation) even on convex surfaces. B is wrong: convexity is a mathematical property unaffected by scaling. C is wrong: convexity is a function property. D is wrong about sample/feature ratio."},
{"id":16,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Training error keeps decreasing. Validation error starts increasing after epoch 50. A colleague recommends reducing the learning rate at epoch 50 instead of applying early stopping. Would this fix overfitting?","options":["A) Yes: a smaller learning rate after epoch 50 prevents the model from making large parameter changes, effectively constraining it from fitting noise in the training data going forward","B) No: the validation error increase indicates a data quality issue beginning at epoch 50, not overfitting, and neither early stopping nor learning rate changes address data quality","C) Yes: learning rate reduction is mathematically equivalent to early stopping because both approaches limit the total magnitude of parameter updates during the training optimization process","D) No: reducing the learning rate slows optimization but does not stop the model from eventually memorizing noise; early stopping at epoch 50 is the correct regularization strategy","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Reducing the learning rate slows learning but doesn’t stop it — the model still eventually overfits, just more slowly. Early stopping halts training entirely at the best generalization point. B is partially right but the model continues fitting noise. C misdiagnoses. D: they are not equivalent."},
{"id":17,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Update rule: θ_new = θ_old − α·∇J(θ). The gradient ∇J(θ) equals −3.5 at the current θ. A colleague claims θ will decrease because gradient descent always moves parameters toward lower cost values. What actually happens?","options":["A) θ increases: subtracting a negative gradient (−3.5) is equivalent to adding 3.5α, moving θ in the positive direction where the cost function decreases for this parameter","B) θ decreases: the negative sign in the update rule and the negative gradient produce a double negative, which gradient descent resolves by subtracting α times 3.5 from theta","C) θ decreases: gradient descent always decreases parameter values when the cost function has not yet reached its minimum, regardless of the gradient’s sign or magnitude at that point","D) θ stays unchanged: a negative gradient indicates the current position is already at a local minimum, so no meaningful update is applied to the parameter value during this step","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"θ_new = θ_old − α(−3.5) = θ_old + 3.5α. Subtracting a negative = adding. θ increases because the negative gradient tells us cost decreases in the direction of increasing θ. B is wrong: parameters can increase or decrease. C is wrong: negative gradient ≠ minimum. D gets the math wrong."},
{"id":18,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Cost-vs-iteration plot: rapid decrease for 100 iterations, then virtually flat from iteration 100 to 10,000. A colleague says the flat region means the model has started overfitting the training data. Is this correct?","options":["A) Yes: the flat region corresponds to a saddle point where the gradient is zero but the solution is suboptimal, and the model needs momentum-based optimization to escape it effectively","B) No: the flat region indicates the learning rate has decayed to exactly zero, preventing any further parameter updates from occurring regardless of whether the minimum was actually reached","C) No: the flat region indicates the tolerance convergence criterion is met — cost changes are negligibly small, meaning the algorithm found parameters near the minimum of the cost function","D) Yes: when the cost stops decreasing, the model has memorized the training data and further iterations cause test performance to degrade significantly even though training cost stays flat","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Flat cost = changes between iterations are negligibly small = algorithm has converged (tolerance criterion). This is about optimization convergence, NOT overfitting. B confuses convergence with generalization. C assumes learning rate decay (not stated). D assumes a saddle point without evidence."},
{"id":19,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Training accuracy 92%, test accuracy 91%, target 97%. A colleague diagnoses high variance because the model is not performing well enough. What is the actual diagnosis?","options":["A) High variance: the model performs poorly overall which always indicates that the model has memorized noise and is failing to generalize to new observations in the test dataset","B) Both high bias and high variance: the one percent gap shows some overfitting while the five percent shortfall from target shows simultaneous underfitting of the true signal","C) Neither: the model is well-calibrated and the five percent gap to target is caused by irreducible noise in the data that no model can overcome regardless of its complexity level","D) High bias: training and test accuracy are close together indicating low variance, but both are far below the target, meaning the model is too simple to capture the true patterns","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Key diagnostic: 1% train-test gap = low variance. But both far from 97% = model too simple (high bias). High variance (B) would show a large gap (e.g., 99% train, 80% test). C overcomplicates — 1% gap is negligible variance. D assumes we know the noise level."},
{"id":20,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Train accuracy 99.5%, test accuracy 72%. A colleague suggests collecting 5x more data as the primary fix. Is this the most effective first step for closing this performance gap?","options":["A) More data can help with high variance but reducing model complexity or adding regularization would be more direct and cost-effective first steps for this overfitting scenario","B) More data is the best fix because a large train-test gap always indicates insufficient training data rather than a fundamental problem with the model’s complexity level or architecture","C) More data will not help at all because the 99.5% training accuracy proves the model has already extracted every learnable pattern from the currently available training data samples","D) More data is counterproductive here because it would increase the irreducible noise component of the total error decomposition, further reducing the test accuracy performance metric","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"99.5% vs 72% = massive gap = overfitting (high variance). More data CAN help but the most direct fixes target the root cause: reduce complexity, add regularization, dropout. B overgeneralizes. C is wrong: 99.5% often means memorizing noise. D is wrong about irreducible noise."},
{"id":21,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Total Error = Bias² + Variance + Noise. A model has low bias but high variance. A colleague proposes adding interaction features (feature₁ × feature₂) to improve test performance. Will this strategy help?","options":["A) No: interaction features only help high bias situations because they address underfitting, but this model already has low bias so they provide absolutely no benefit to the prediction","B) No: adding interaction features increases model complexity which would increase variance further while bias is already low — the exact opposite of what this situation requires","C) Yes: interaction features capture nonlinear relationships that reduce the bias component, and since bias squared decreases faster than variance increases the total error still decreases","D) Yes: interaction features act as implicit regularization because they provide redundant information that helps the model average out noise present in the original individual features","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Low bias + high variance = model already too complex. Adding interaction features makes it MORE complex, increasing variance. The fix is the opposite: simplify (fewer features, regularization, more data). B’s claim about relative rates is unfounded. C has right conclusion but wrong reasoning. D is incorrect."},
{"id":22,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Dart board analogy: predictions are tightly clustered but consistently off-center from the bullseye. A colleague says this is high variance because predictions miss the target by a significant amount. What is correct?","options":["A) Low bias, high variance: the off-center position represents high variance because predictions are far from the true target value regardless of how tightly they are clustered together","B) Low bias, low variance: the tight clustering demonstrates that the model is precise, and the off-center positioning is caused by irreducible noise rather than any model deficiency","C) High bias, high variance: any time predictions consistently miss the target, both error components must be elevated since total error equals bias squared plus variance by definition","D) High bias, low variance: tight clustering means consistent predictions with low variance, while the systematic off-center pattern shows high bias as a persistent directional error","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Variance = spread of predictions (tight clustering = low variance). Bias = systematic deviation from truth (off-center = high bias). B confuses distance-from-target with variance. C wrongly assumes both must be high. D incorrectly attributes the systematic offset to noise."},
{"id":23,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Degree-1: high train AND test error. Degree-4: low train, high test error. Degree-2: moderate train, lowest test error. A colleague says degree-2 will always be optimal for any dataset. Why is this wrong?","options":["A) The colleague is actually correct: quadratic models consistently provide the best bias-variance tradeoff because they add just enough complexity without creating overfitting risk in practice","B) Polynomial degree is irrelevant to the bias-variance tradeoff because the tradeoff is controlled entirely by the regularization parameter lambda rather than by the model structure itself","C) The optimal complexity depends solely on the number of training samples, not the underlying function, so degree-2 would change with different sample sizes but not different data distributions","D) The optimal complexity depends on the specific dataset’s true underlying function; degree-2 is the sweet spot here but different data may require degree one or degree ten or higher","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The bias-variance tradeoff’s optimal complexity depends on the true underlying function AND dataset. Degree-2 is optimal HERE because the true relationship is roughly quadratic. Different data might need different complexity. B falsely universalizes. C is partially right but incomplete. D is wrong."},
{"id":24,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"A model has high bias (underfitting). A colleague recommends three actions: (1) collect more data, (2) add polynomial features, (3) reduce regularization strength. Which combination is most effective?","options":["A) All three help: more data gives the model additional patterns to learn, polynomial features capture nonlinear relationships, and reduced regularization allows tighter data fitting simultaneously","B) Actions 1 and 3 help: more data provides evidence to reduce bias and reducing regularization frees the model, while polynomial features risk introducing harmful multicollinearity","C) Actions 2 and 3 address high bias: polynomial features increase model complexity while reducing regularization frees the model to fit more closely; more data helps variance not bias","D) Only action 2 helps: polynomial features directly address underfitting, while more data and regularization changes have no measurable effect on the bias component of the total error","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"For high bias: increase complexity (polynomial features ✔) and reduce regularization (let model fit more ✔). More data does NOT help high bias — a too-simple model stays too simple regardless of data size. B is wrong about data. C ignores regularization’s effect. D is wrong about data helping bias."},
{"id":25,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"200 features, only ~10 are suspected to be relevant. A colleague recommends Ridge (L2) as the safe default choice. For this specific sparse scenario, is Ridge the best choice?","options":["A) Yes: Ridge is always the safest default because it shrinks all coefficients proportionally without any risk of accidentally eliminating potentially useful features from the trained model","B) Yes: Ridge outperforms Lasso specifically when the true number of relevant features is small because L2 regularization provides stronger shrinkage on the irrelevant coefficient values","C) No: Lasso (L1) is better here because it drives irrelevant coefficients to exactly zero, performing automatic feature selection when few features are truly relevant in the dataset","D) No: Elastic Net is always superior to both Ridge and Lasso because it combines the advantages of both methods without requiring any additional hyperparameters to tune in practice","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"When you expect sparsity (few relevant features among many), Lasso excels at driving irrelevant coefficients to exactly zero — automatic feature selection. Ridge shrinks but never zeros out. B is reasonable generally but suboptimal for sparse cases. C is wrong: Elastic Net adds a hyperparameter. D inverts the truth."},
{"id":26,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Lasso is applied to data with groups of highly correlated features. It arbitrarily picks one feature per group and zeros the rest, producing unstable results. A colleague suggests switching to Ridge. Is Ridge the best alternative?","options":["A) Ridge is the best alternative because its L2 penalty is everywhere differentiable while Lasso’s L1 penalty has non-differentiable points that cause the arbitrary feature selection behavior","B) Neither Ridge nor Elastic Net helps: when features are highly correlated, you must remove the correlations through PCA before applying any form of regularized regression to the data","C) Elastic Net is better: it combines L1 sparsity with L2’s ability to distribute weights across correlated features, giving stable feature groups instead of arbitrary random selection","D) Ridge is the best alternative because it treats all correlated features equally by shrinking their coefficients to identical values, which produces the most stable and interpretable results","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Elastic Net is designed for this scenario. L2 component encourages correlated features to share similar coefficients; L1 provides sparsity. Ridge works but loses sparsity. B is misleading: Ridge doesn’t make coefficients identical. C is overkill. D’s reasoning is wrong about the cause."},
{"id":27,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Ridge with λ=1000: J(θ) = MSE + 1000·Σθj². A colleague says this effectively converts the model into Lasso because the coefficients become so small they are essentially indistinguishable from zero. Is this correct?","options":["A) No: Ridge shrinks coefficients toward zero but mathematically never makes them exactly zero; this causes severe underfitting rather than producing feature selection like Lasso does","B) No: large lambda in Ridge causes the model to overfit because the optimizer compensates for the strong penalty by dramatically amplifying the non-penalized bias intercept term","C) Yes: the mathematical equivalence between L1 and L2 norms at extreme regularization strengths means Ridge with large lambda and Lasso produce identical coefficient vectors always","D) Yes: when lambda is sufficiently large in Ridge, the L2 penalty behaves identically to L1, driving coefficients below a numerical threshold that is functionally indistinguishable from zero","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ridge’s L2 penalty NEVER produces exact zeros — fundamental mathematical difference from Lasso’s L1. With λ=1000, coefficients are very small (near-zero) but ALL features remain, causing underfitting. B and D state incorrect mathematical equivalences. C inverts the effect on the bias term."},
{"id":28,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Elastic Net: J(θ) = MSE + λ[α·Σ|θj| + (1−α)·Σθj²]. With α=0.5 and λ=10, a colleague claims this equals running Ridge with λ=5 and Lasso with λ=5 separately, then averaging the resulting coefficients. Is this correct?","options":["A) Yes: the linear combination of L1 and L2 penalties in the cost function is mathematically equivalent to training two separate models independently and computing their arithmetic mean","B) Yes: any convex combination of convex penalties can always be decomposed into independent optimization subproblems that are solved separately and then linearly combined afterward","C) No: the equivalence only holds when alpha equals exactly 0.5; for any other alpha value the single-objective and two-model approaches produce different coefficient vectors due to asymmetry","D) No: Elastic Net optimizes both penalties simultaneously in one objective function, producing different coefficients than averaging two independently trained separate regression models","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Elastic Net jointly optimizes ONE objective where L1 and L2 interact during each gradient step. This is fundamentally different from averaging two separate models. B and D state incorrect mathematical decompositions. C wrongly suggests it works specifically for α=0.5."},
{"id":29,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"After Ridge regularization, ‘bathrooms’ coefficient shrinks from 15,000 to 3,500 while ‘sqft’ shrinks from 200 to 180. A colleague concludes sqft is more important because Ridge penalized it less. Is this valid?","options":["A) No: coefficient magnitude depends on feature scale (bathrooms 1–5, sqft 500–5000), so raw shrinkage amounts are not comparable without first standardizing all features to the same scale","B) Yes: Ridge’s L2 penalty applies equal pressure to all coefficients, so the coefficient that changes the least must be the feature the model relies on most for accurate predictions","C) No: the larger shrinkage of bathrooms means Ridge correctly detected it as a noisy feature and is appropriately removing its influence from the model to prevent overfitting behavior","D) Yes: when a coefficient resists shrinkage under L2 regularization, it indicates the corresponding feature has a statistically significant relationship with the target variable being predicted","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Raw coefficient magnitude depends on feature scale. Bathrooms (1–5) gets 15,000/unit while sqft (500–5000) gets 200/unit. Ridge penalizes squared coefficients, so the large bathroom coefficient is penalized more. Without standardization, shrinkage comparisons are meaningless. B ignores scale. C overinterprets. D conflates with significance."},
{"id":30,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Which of the following statements about regularization techniques is FALSE?","options":["A) Early stopping during gradient descent training acts as a form of regularization by implicitly constraining the effective complexity of the model over the course of training iterations","B) Ridge regression is a reasonable default choice when you have no prior knowledge about which features in the dataset are relevant versus irrelevant for prediction purposes","C) Regularization always improves test performance regardless of the regularization strength parameter lambda that is selected for the model during the hyperparameter tuning process","D) Lasso regression can perform automatic feature selection by driving some feature coefficients to exactly zero during the optimization process based on the L1 penalty term","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A is FALSE. Too-small λ = no effect (still overfits). Too-large λ = over-regularization (underfits). The optimal λ must be found via cross-validation. B, C, and D are all true statements from the course material."},
{"id":31,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Without regularization: 95% train, 70% test. With optimal L2: 88% train, 86% test. A colleague is alarmed that training accuracy dropped 7 points and wants to reduce regularization strength. Should they?","options":["A) No: the 7-point training drop is expected and desirable — regularization prevents noise memorization, trading training accuracy for much better generalization from 70% to 86% on test data","B) Yes: training accuracy should never decrease when regularization is properly applied because regularization only removes noise from predictions without affecting the model’s ability to fit signal","C) No: but only because the test accuracy improvement is exactly double the training accuracy drop, which indicates the regularization parameter is perfectly calibrated by mathematical definition","D) Yes: a 7-point training accuracy drop indicates regularization strength is too high, and a smaller lambda should preserve training accuracy while still providing some overfitting protection","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The gap narrowed from 25 points (95-70) to 2 points (88-86). Training accuracy drops because the model can’t memorize noise anymore — this is the intended effect. B wrongly prioritizes training accuracy. C invents a non-existent calibration rule. D is wrong: regularization inherently reduces training fit."},
{"id":32,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Logistic regression outputs P(default|x) = 0.73 for a loan applicant. A colleague says 0.73 means 73% of similar applicants in the training data defaulted. What does 0.73 actually represent?","options":["A) The model’s estimated probability that this specific applicant will default, computed by passing their individual feature values through the learned sigmoid function, not a training data frequency","B) The proportion of the entire training dataset that belongs to the positive class of defaulters, which serves as the prior probability applied uniformly to all new applicants during prediction","C) The cosine similarity between this applicant’s feature vector and the average feature vector of all defaulters in the training set, normalized to fall within the range of zero to one","D) The normalized distance from this applicant’s feature values to the logistic regression decision boundary, scaled by the sigmoid function to produce a value between zero and one","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Logistic regression computes P(y=1|x) = 1/(1+e^(−θᵀx)) for each individual’s specific features. The 0.73 is a probability for THIS applicant, not a training data frequency (B), cosine similarity (C), or distance (D)."},
{"id":33,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A colleague uses MSE as the cost function for logistic regression, arguing it worked for linear regression. The model converges to a poor solution. Why did MSE fail while log-loss succeeds?","options":["A) MSE penalizes all prediction errors with equal intensity regardless of the model’s confidence level, while log-loss provides much stronger gradient signals for confidently wrong classification predictions","B) MSE combined with the sigmoid function creates a non-convex cost surface with multiple local minima; log-loss remains convex with the sigmoid, guaranteeing convergence to the global optimum","C) MSE requires continuous target variables to compute mathematically defined and numerically stable gradients, and binary classification labels violate this requirement causing undefined gradient values","D) MSE cannot handle binary target variables because squaring the difference between predictions and binary labels of zero and one produces gradient values outside the valid probability range","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sigmoid + MSE = non-convex surface with local minima where GD gets stuck. Sigmoid + log-loss = convex surface. C describes a secondary benefit of log-loss but not the primary failure reason. B is wrong: MSE can compute on binary targets. D is wrong: gradients are defined for binary targets."},
{"id":34,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"KNN: k=1 gives 100% training accuracy but poor test accuracy. k=50 gives lower training AND poor test accuracy. A colleague says k=1 is better because it has perfect training performance. Why is this fundamentally flawed?","options":["A) The comparison is invalid because KNN with k=1 and k=50 are fundamentally different algorithm families that cannot be compared using the same accuracy metric or evaluation framework","B) The colleague’s reasoning is actually correct: higher training accuracy always correlates with better generalization performance when the algorithm family is kept constant across experiments","C) Both values give poor test accuracy which conclusively proves KNN is inappropriate for this specific dataset regardless of the k value, and a different algorithm family should be used","D) Training accuracy is irrelevant for model selection; k=1 memorizes data causing overfitting while k=50 oversmooths boundaries causing underfitting, so the optimal k balances both effects","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Model selection must prioritize TEST performance. 100% training accuracy with k=1 is just memorization. k=50 oversmooths. Optimal k (e.g., 5–15) balances variance (small k) and bias (large k). B is wrong: same algorithm. C is premature. D is fundamentally wrong about the accuracy-generalization relationship."},
{"id":35,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A node has 60 samples class A, 40 class B. A colleague computes Gini as 1 − (0.6 + 0.4) = 0.0 and concludes the node is pure. What computational error did they make?","options":["A) They should have used entropy instead of Gini because Gini impurity is only mathematically valid for multiclass classification problems with three or more classes, not binary problems","B) They forgot to square the class proportions: correct Gini = 1 − (0.6² + 0.4²) = 1 − (0.36 + 0.16) = 0.48, indicating substantial impurity rather than a pure node","C) They used the wrong formula entirely: Gini impurity uses the product of proportions (0.6 × 0.4 = 0.24) not their sum, and the result must be doubled to get the final impurity value","D) They applied the formula correctly but misinterpreted what zero means: a Gini value of exactly 0.0 indicates maximum impurity in binary classification scenarios, not a perfectly pure node","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Gini = 1 − Σ(p_k²). The colleague forgot to SQUARE the proportions. Correct: 1 − (0.36 + 0.16) = 0.48, far from pure. B describes a different formula. C is wrong: 0.0 Gini IS pure (0.5 is maximum for binary). D is wrong: Gini works for binary classification."},
{"id":36,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Gmail used Naive Bayes for spam detection despite the ‘naive’ independence assumption being violated (‘Nigerian’ and ‘prince’ are correlated in spam). A colleague says NB works because modern spam actually has independent word distributions. Is this correct?","options":["A) Yes: statistical analysis of modern spam emails confirms that word independence holds approximately, which explains why Naive Bayes achieves near-optimal classification performance on email data","B) No: Gmail uses a modified Naive Bayes variant that explicitly models pairwise word correlations through bigram features added during the text preprocessing and feature extraction pipeline stage","C) Yes: the independence assumption is satisfied after applying TF-IDF weighting to the raw word features, which effectively decorrelates the word features before passing them to the Bayes classifier","D) No: NB works despite violated independence because classification only needs correct probability rankings between classes, not perfectly calibrated absolute probability values for each class","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Spam words ARE correlated. NB works because for classification you only need P(spam|x) > P(not-spam|x) to be correct — the ranking is usually preserved even with miscalibrated probabilities from violated independence. B is factually wrong. C invents a non-existent modification. D is wrong: TF-IDF doesn’t decorrelate."},
{"id":37,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A depth-20 decision tree has leaf nodes with 1–2 samples and 99.9% training accuracy but 62% test accuracy. A colleague worries that pruning will permanently lose information captured by the deep branches. Is this concern valid?","options":["A) No: but only because decision trees can be regrown to the original depth at any time from the stored training dataset, so pruning is always a perfectly reversible operation in practice","B) Yes: each split in a decision tree represents a statistically significant pattern that the algorithm validated during training, so removing any split discards confirmed useful information","C) No: deep branches with 1–2 samples captured noise not signal; setting max_depth and min_samples_per_split removes overfitted splits while retaining genuine patterns in the upper tree","D) Yes: deep tree branches capture rare but genuine edge cases that shallow trees inevitably miss, so pruning always sacrifices model performance on minority patterns in the test dataset","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Leaf nodes with 1–2 samples are memorizing individual examples (noise), not genuine patterns. Pruning removes these while keeping informative splits. B romanticizes deep branches. C’s reasoning (reversibility) is beside the point. D is wrong: tree splits are NOT individually tested for statistical significance."},
{"id":38,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Softmax outputs [0.05, 0.10, 0.70, 0.10, 0.05] for 5 classes. A colleague claims this is equivalent to running 5 independent sigmoid classifiers. Why is this incorrect for mutually exclusive classes?","options":["A) Softmax is designed exclusively for binary classification problems where it mathematically reduces to the sigmoid function, while independent sigmoids properly handle the multiclass classification case","B) Softmax and independent sigmoids produce mathematically identical outputs because the exponential normalization in softmax is algebraically equivalent to applying sigmoid to each class independently","C) Softmax normalizes outputs across all classes so they sum to exactly 1.0, forming a valid probability distribution; independent sigmoids have no such constraint and can sum to any arbitrary value","D) Softmax outputs are not true probabilities but rather unbounded confidence scores that can exceed 1.0 in certain edge cases, unlike sigmoid which is always strictly bounded between zero and one","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Softmax: P(k) = e^(s_k)/Σe^(s_i), ensuring ΣP(k)=1.0. Five independent sigmoids could output [0.8, 0.9, 0.3, 0.7, 0.6] summing to 3.3 — not a valid distribution for mutually exclusive classes. B is mathematically wrong. C inverts the usage. D is wrong: softmax IS bounded and does sum to 1."},
{"id":39,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Linear regression predicts spam probability as −0.3 for one email and 1.4 for another. A colleague says these can be thresholded at 0.5 for classification, making logistic regression unnecessary. What is the fundamental problem?","options":["A) Values outside [0,1] can be clipped to the valid range by setting negatives to zero and values above one to one, which makes linear regression equivalent to logistic regression after correction","B) Values outside [0,1] are invalid probabilities, the decision boundary shifts unpredictably with outlier inputs, and the cost function is not designed for classification optimization at all","C) The only problem is computational efficiency: linear regression requires more iterations to converge than logistic regression for binary classification tasks, but final results are equivalent","D) Linear regression can actually be used for classification by thresholding, and in practice it produces results identical to logistic regression when the classes are well-separated in feature space","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Outputs of −0.3 and 1.4 aren’t probabilities. Sigmoid constrains to (0,1). Thresholding linear regression is problematic: uncalibrated outputs, boundary shifts with outliers, wrong cost function. B is wrong: different results. D: clipping doesn’t fix underlying issues. C is wrong: not equivalent."},
{"id":40,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A fintech company builds a credit scoring model. A single decision tree achieves 78% accuracy with clear interpretability that regulators appreciate. The team switches to a random forest (100 trees) achieving 89% accuracy, but a regulator asks: \"How does each tree in the forest differ from the single tree, and why can't you just use 100 copies of the same best tree?\" The correct technical explanation AND its implication for interpretability are:","options":["A) Each tree trains on a bootstrap sample (with replacement) and considers random feature subsets at each split, creating diverse trees that make different errors; however, this diversity makes the ensemble a black box since no single tree represents the full model logic","B) Each tree uses a different splitting criterion (some use Gini, others entropy, others variance reduction) selected randomly, creating algorithmic diversity; this makes the ensemble harder to audit since the splitting logic varies","C) Each tree is trained sequentially on the errors of the previous tree, so later trees correct earlier mistakes; this sequential dependency means the first tree is interpretable but later trees only make sense in the context of all previous ones","D) Each tree trains on a completely different subset of customers with no overlap, so the 100 trees collectively see 100x more data than one tree; the interpretability loss is minimal because each tree is still individually interpretable","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Random forest creates diversity through TWO mechanisms: (1) bootstrap sampling (same-size samples WITH replacement from the same data, NOT different subsets), and (2) random feature selection at each split. This diversity means trees make uncorrelated errors, and averaging reduces variance. But the ensemble loses the single-tree interpretability that regulators value. B incorrectly claims 100x more data. C invents varying criteria. D describes boosting, not bagging."},
{"id":41,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A hospital deploys a random forest to predict patient readmission risk. During a regulatory audit, the team claims they validated the model using out-of-bag (OOB) error and found 87% accuracy. The auditor asks whether OOB error is a legitimate substitute for a held-out test set, and whether the team should have used stratified cross-validation instead given that only 8% of patients are readmitted. The correct assessment of BOTH the OOB validity AND the stratification concern is:","options":["A) OOB error is valid and the class imbalance concern is irrelevant because bootstrap sampling automatically creates balanced samples by oversampling the minority class proportionally in each bootstrap draw","B) OOB error is a valid generalization estimate because each sample is predicted only by trees that never trained on it; however, the auditor is right that with 8% readmission rate, OOB evaluation may not maintain class proportions across individual tree evaluations, potentially making the OOB estimate unreliable for the minority class","C) OOB error is invalid because the bootstrap samples overlap with each other, creating data leakage between the trees that inflates the OOB estimate; stratified CV with completely independent folds eliminates this leakage","D) OOB error is invalid because it evaluates each sample using only a fraction of the trees rather than the full ensemble, underestimating true performance; stratified CV would use the complete forest for every evaluation fold","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"OOB error IS valid - each sample's prediction comes from ~1/3 of trees that never saw it, closely matching CV estimates. But the auditor raises a legitimate concern: with 8% readmission, individual OOB evaluations per sample may have high variance for the minority class. B is wrong: OOB using fewer trees is a feature (honest evaluation), not a bug. C is wrong: bootstrap does NOT oversample minorities. D is wrong: bootstrap overlap is not data leakage."},
{"id":42,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A Mastercard fraud detection team tests two ensemble approaches on transaction data. Random Forest achieves 94% F1 on fraud detection with stable performance across quarterly retraining cycles. Gradient Boosting achieves 96% F1 initially but degrades to 88% F1 after retraining on new data containing a fraud ring with unusual patterns. The team lead attributes the GBM degradation to \"the algorithm being inherently unstable.\" The actual technical explanation for GBM's degradation AND why RF was more stable is:","options":["A) GBM's sequential training focused later trees heavily on the unusual fraud ring patterns (hard examples), overfitting to those specific patterns; RF's independent parallel trees with random subsets naturally regularize against overfitting to any single pattern subset","B) GBM degraded because gradient boosting cannot handle concept drift in sequential data, while random forests are specifically designed for non-stationary distributions through their bootstrap sampling mechanism","C) GBM degraded because each new tree must be compatible with all previous trees in the sequence, creating architectural constraints that prevent adaptation; RF trees are independent and can each adapt freely to new patterns","D) GBM degraded because its learning rate was set too high for the new data distribution, while RF has no learning rate parameter and is therefore immune to this type of hyperparameter sensitivity across data shifts","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"GBM's sequential error correction means later trees focus on residuals (hard cases). When the fraud ring creates unusual hard cases, GBM overfits to them. RF's independent trees with random feature/sample subsets create diversity that prevents overfitting to any specific pattern subset. B oversimplifies - learning rate matters but isn't the root cause. C is wrong - neither is specifically designed for concept drift. D mischaracterizes GBM's architecture."},
{"id":43,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A data science team at an e-commerce company runs a Kaggle-style internal competition. Team A uses standard Gradient Boosting (scikit-learn's GradientBoostingClassifier). Team B uses XGBoost with identical hyperparameters (same max_depth, n_estimators, learning_rate). Team B consistently achieves 1-2% higher accuracy AND trains 5x faster. Team A claims this is unfair because \"XGBoost is just a faster implementation of the same algorithm.\" The technical reasons Team A's claim is INCORRECT and the specific XGBoost features that explain BOTH the accuracy and speed differences are:","options":["A) XGBoost achieves higher accuracy by using deeper trees by default and achieves faster training by distributing tree construction across multiple machines using a map-reduce architecture that standard GBM does not support","B) XGBoost adds L1 and L2 regularization on leaf weights (preventing overfitting that standard GBM suffers from), handles missing values natively (avoiding information loss from imputation), and parallelizes tree construction at the split-finding level (not tree-level parallelism)","C) XGBoost is mathematically identical to standard GBM but uses GPU acceleration and SIMD instructions for faster matrix operations; the accuracy difference comes solely from numerical precision improvements in the gradient computation","D) XGBoost uses a fundamentally different tree structure called \"boosted random forests\" that combines bagging within each boosting iteration, creating a hybrid ensemble that is both more accurate and more parallelizable than pure boosting","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"XGBoost is NOT the same algorithm as standard GBM. Key differences: (1) built-in L1+L2 regularization on leaf weights (standard GBM has none), (2) native missing value handling, (3) second-order gradient approximation, (4) parallel split-finding (within each tree, not across trees). B invents \"boosted random forests.\" C fabricates default depths and map-reduce. D wrongly claims mathematical identity."},
{"id":44,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A bank builds a credit default model using stacking: base models are logistic regression (LR), random forest (RF), and gradient boosting (GBM). The meta-learner is a logistic regression trained on base model predictions. During development, a junior data scientist trains the meta-learner using the base models' predictions on the TRAINING data. The model achieves 95% accuracy in development but only 82% in production. The root cause of this 13-point performance drop AND the correct methodology to prevent it are:","options":["A) The base models' training-set predictions are overfit (especially GBM and RF which memorize training data), so the meta-learner learned to trust inflated prediction quality; the fix is using out-of-fold (k-fold) predictions for the meta-learner's training data","B) The base models perform differently in production because the data distribution shifted; the fix is retraining all base models on the most recent data and implementing continuous monitoring for distribution drift","C) The training predictions from LR, RF, and GBM are too correlated because they all learned the same patterns from the same training data; the fix is training each base model on a different random subset to ensure prediction diversity","D) The meta-learner overfits because logistic regression has too many parameters when trained on three base model outputs; the fix is replacing the logistic regression meta-learner with a simple averaging function that requires no training","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When base models predict on their own training data, GBM (99%+ train accuracy) and RF (similar) produce overly confident, overfit predictions. The meta-learner then learns to heavily weight these overfit signals. In production, base models produce honest (lower-quality) predictions, and the meta-learner can't handle this gap. Out-of-fold predictions simulate production conditions during meta-learner training. B oversimplifies. C misdiagnoses the root cause. D doesn't fix the leakage."},
{"id":45,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"An insurance company builds two ensemble models for claim fraud detection. Model A (Random Forest, 500 trees) achieves: train AUC=0.96, test AUC=0.93. Model B (AdaBoost, 500 rounds) achieves: train AUC=0.99, test AUC=0.84. The chief actuary observes Model B's higher training AUC and argues it is \"learning the patterns better\" and should be deployed with more training data to close the gap. The flaw in this reasoning AND the architectural explanation for Model B's larger train-test gap are:","options":["A) Model B overfits because 500 rounds is too many for AdaBoost; the solution is to reduce to fewer than 50 rounds, since AdaBoost's theoretical optimal number of rounds is always less than the square root of the training set size","B) Model B's 99% train AUC indicates overfitting not better learning; AdaBoost's sequential reweighting mechanism increasingly focuses on hard-to-classify samples including mislabeled or noisy claims, effectively memorizing outliers that don't generalize","C) Model B's train-test gap is caused by AdaBoost using decision stumps which are too simple for fraud patterns; switching to depth-3 trees as base learners would reduce variance while maintaining the same training AUC level","D) Model B's higher training AUC is expected because AdaBoost uses a more expressive loss function than Random Forest; the test gap would close with more training data because AdaBoost's bias decreases faster than its variance increases with sample size","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The 15-point train-test gap (0.99 vs 0.84) in AdaBoost is classic overfitting. AdaBoost reweights samples each round, increasingly focusing on hard cases. If these include noise/outliers, later rounds memorize them. RF's independent trees with averaging are inherently more robust. B incorrectly claims more data always fixes boosting overfitting. C invents a square-root rule. D misdiagnoses the cause (stumps vs deep trees is about bias, not the variance issue shown here)."},
{"id":46,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A 10-layer network with sigmoid activation: early layers stop learning while later layers learn normally. A colleague diagnoses this as the early layers overfitting to low-level features. What is the actual problem?","options":["A) Vanishing gradients: sigmoid’s maximum gradient is 0.25, so multiplying through 10 layers gives approximately 10⁻⁶, making early layer weight updates negligible; ReLU activation fixes this","B) Exploding gradients: sigmoid amplifies gradients through deep layers causing numerical overflow in early layer computations, which prevents stable weight updates during backpropagation training","C) Dead neurons: sigmoid outputs saturate at zero or one for most inputs in early layers, causing the neurons to become permanently inactive and unable to contribute during the training process","D) Early layers are overfitting to low-level features like edges and textures while later layers correctly learn high-level patterns, creating an imbalance in learning speed across the network","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Vanishing gradient: sigmoid max gradient = 0.25. Through 10 layers via chain rule: 0.25¹⁰ ≈ 10⁻⁶. Early layer gradients are negligible. ReLU (gradient=1 for positive) fixes this. B mischaracterizes the problem. C describes the OPPOSITE (exploding). D describes a ReLU issue (dying ReLU), not sigmoid."},
{"id":47,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"3 output classes (cat, dog, bird). A colleague proposes 3 neurons with independent sigmoid activations instead of softmax, noting both produce values in (0,1). Why is softmax preferred for mutually exclusive classes?","options":["A) Softmax produces more accurate predictions because the exponential function in its formula amplifies differences between class scores more effectively than the sigmoid logistic curve does","B) Independent sigmoids cannot produce valid outputs for more than two classes because the sigmoid function is mathematically defined only for binary classification decision boundary computations","C) Softmax normalizes outputs to sum to exactly 1.0 forming a valid probability distribution; independent sigmoids can produce outputs summing to any value, violating the mutual exclusivity constraint","D) Independent sigmoids are computationally more expensive than softmax because each sigmoid requires a separate forward pass through the entire network during the prediction computation phase","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"For mutually exclusive classes, probabilities must sum to 1. Softmax enforces this. Three independent sigmoids might output [0.8, 0.9, 0.3] summing to 2.0 — nonsensical. B is wrong: sigmoids are computationally simpler. C overstates. D is wrong: sigmoid can be applied to any number of outputs."},
{"id":48,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"Task A: classify product images. Task B: predict stock prices from historical sequences. A colleague suggests RNNs for both because ‘pixels can be read sequentially left-to-right, giving images a sequential structure.’ Why is this wrong?","options":["A) RNNs can effectively process images by reading pixels sequentially, but they are computationally too slow for production image classification systems because of sequential computation bottlenecks","B) Images have 2D spatial structure best captured by CNNs with convolutional filters; time series have temporal dependencies best captured by RNNs; reading pixels sequentially discards spatial locality","C) The colleague is correct for images but wrong for stocks: RNNs detect sequential patterns in pixel rows effectively, while stock prices require convolutional networks to detect temporal price patterns","D) Both tasks should exclusively use transformer architectures because transformers have completely replaced both CNNs and RNNs across all domains including computer vision and time series analysis","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Images have 2D spatial locality (nearby pixels relate). CNNs exploit this with convolutional filters. Sequentializing pixels destroys this structure. Time series genuinely have sequential dependencies for RNNs/LSTMs. B cites speed, not the fundamental mismatch. C inverts the pairing. D overgeneralizes."},
{"id":49,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A colleague explains backpropagation as ‘rerunning the forward pass with corrected weights to gradually improve predictions.’ What does backpropagation actually compute and why is this description wrong?","options":["A) It re-executes the forward pass multiple times with slightly perturbed weights to numerically estimate the gradient of the loss function for each individual weight in the entire network","B) It compares current predictions against stored copies of previous iteration predictions to determine which weights improved performance and which weights degraded the model’s output quality","C) It computes gradients of the loss with respect to each weight by propagating error signals backward through layers using the chain rule, enabling gradient descent to update all weights","D) It propagates the correct output labels backward through the network layers to directly compute the optimal value each weight should take, bypassing iterative gradient-based optimization entirely","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Backpropagation = computing ∂Loss/∂w for every weight using the chain rule, working backward from output. These gradients enable gradient descent updates. B describes numerical gradient estimation (much slower). C describes direct solution (infeasible for neural networks). D describes nothing in standard training."},
{"id":50,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"Single-layer perceptron cannot solve XOR. A colleague argues it could with ReLU activation instead of step function, since ReLU introduces nonlinearity. Is this correct?","options":["A) No: single-layer computes a linear combination then applies activation; no activation function can overcome the linear boundary limitation without adding a hidden layer for nonlinear transformation","B) No: but a single layer with sigmoid activation could solve XOR because the sigmoid function’s smooth S-shaped curve can approximate any arbitrarily complex decision boundary given enough neurons","C) Yes: ReLU introduces piecewise nonlinearity that allows a single-layer perceptron to create the two separate linear decision boundaries needed to correctly separate the XOR class labels","D) Yes: any universal approximator activation function applied in a single computational layer can represent any boolean function including XOR given a sufficient number of neurons in that layer","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A single-layer perceptron computes y=f(Σwᵢxᵢ+b). The weighted sum is always linear. ANY activation applied to a single linear combination cannot create a nonlinear decision boundary needed for XOR. A hidden layer provides the necessary nonlinear transformation BEFORE classification. B, C, D all incorrectly claim a single layer suffices."},
{"id":51,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"LSTM vs vanilla RNN: a colleague says LSTMs are superior simply because they have more parameters per cell, giving them greater memory capacity for storing sequence information. What is the actual innovation?","options":["A) LSTMs use self-attention mechanisms that allow each time step to directly access information from all other time steps, completely bypassing the sequential processing bottleneck of vanilla RNNs","B) LSTMs have more parameters per cell which provides greater raw memory capacity, allowing them to store and recall information from significantly longer sequences than vanilla RNN architectures","C) LSTMs process sequences in both forward and backward directions simultaneously, giving them access to future context that vanilla RNNs cannot utilize due to their unidirectional architectural design","D) LSTMs use gating mechanisms (forget, input, output gates) that selectively control information flow, allowing relevant information to persist across long sequences and mitigating vanishing gradients","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"LSTMs’ innovation is gates: forget gate (what to discard), input gate (what to store), output gate (what to expose). These create a gradient highway preventing vanishing gradients. B oversimplifies — it’s not just more parameters. C describes bidirectional RNNs (separate concept). D describes transformer attention (different architecture)."},
{"id":52,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A team has a structured tabular dataset with 10,000 samples and 50 features for a classification task. A colleague insists on using a deep neural network because 'deep learning always outperforms traditional methods.' The team's XGBoost baseline achieves 92% accuracy. What is wrong with the colleague's reasoning?","options":["A) The 92% XGBoost accuracy proves the dataset has a fundamental performance ceiling that no algorithm can exceed, making the switch to deep learning pointless regardless of architecture chosen","B) Deep neural networks always outperform XGBoost on any dataset given sufficient hyperparameter tuning, so the colleague is correct but the team needs to tune the neural network more carefully first","C) Deep learning cannot process tabular data at all because convolutional and recurrent layers require spatial or sequential structure that flat feature tables fundamentally do not provide","D) On structured tabular data with moderate sample sizes, gradient boosting methods like XGBoost frequently match or outperform deep neural networks, which excel primarily on unstructured data like images and text","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Multiple large-scale benchmarks (e.g., Grinsztajn et al., 2022) show that gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) frequently matches or outperforms deep learning on tabular data, especially with moderate sample sizes. Deep learning excels on unstructured data (images, text, audio) where it can exploit spatial/sequential patterns. B is false — this is an empirical fact, not a tuning issue. C invents a ceiling. D is wrong — MLPs can process tabular data, they just often do not outperform boosting."},
{"id":53,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A European bank deploys a fraud detection model and reports 99.5% accuracy to the board. Only 0.5% of transactions are fraudulent. The compliance officer is satisfied, but the fraud investigation team discovers that the model catches virtually no actual fraud — legitimate transactions flagged as fraud are almost zero, but so are detected frauds. The accuracy figure that satisfied the board AND the metric that reveals the model's actual failure are:","options":["A) The model achieves 99.5% accuracy because it learned to flag the 0.5% of fraudulent transactions correctly while also correctly classifying all legitimate ones; the low detection rate is caused by a threshold set too high in production","B) A naive model predicting \"not fraud\" for every transaction achieves the same 99.5% accuracy because 99.5% of transactions are legitimate; recall on the fraud class (near 0%) reveals the model detects almost no actual fraud despite high accuracy","C) The 99.5% accuracy is inflated by the bank's oversampling strategy during training which artificially balanced the classes; precision on the fraud class would reveal that most fraud alerts are actually legitimate transactions","D) The 99.5% accuracy is calculated on the training set where the model memorized the fraud patterns; the test accuracy would be significantly lower because fraud patterns change rapidly and the model cannot generalize","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This is the accuracy paradox. With 99.5% legitimate transactions, always predicting \"not fraud\" gives 99.5% accuracy while catching ZERO fraud. The board was fooled by a meaningless metric. Recall (what fraction of actual frauds are caught?) exposes the failure. B speculates about oversampling without evidence. C assumes the issue is train vs test. D contradicts the stated scenario (model catches virtually no fraud)."},
{"id":54,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Cancer screening: precision=0.95, recall=0.40. A colleague prioritizes improving precision because ‘false alarms waste hospital resources.’ In this medical screening context, is this the right priority?","options":["A) No: recall should be prioritized because missing 60% of actual cancer cases through false negatives is far more dangerous than ordering unnecessary follow-up tests through false positives","B) Yes: improving precision reduces the psychological harm to patients who are incorrectly told they might have cancer, which is considered the primary harm in cancer screening program design","C) No: neither metric should be prioritized individually; the F1 score should be maximized because it treats false positives and false negatives as equally costly across all medical applications","D) Yes: hospital resources are severely limited, so reducing false positives through improved precision ensures diagnostic resources are allocated efficiently to patients most likely to have cancer","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In cancer screening, missed diagnosis (false negative) can mean delayed treatment and death. False alarm (false positive) means additional testing — inconvenient but not fatal. With recall=0.40, 60% of cancers are MISSED. B and D prioritize convenience over lives. C ignores that FP and FN costs differ enormously in healthcare."},
{"id":55,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Model A: AUC=0.92. Model B: AUC=0.78. A colleague says ‘Model A is 14% more accurate.’ What are TWO things wrong with this statement?","options":["A) AUC differences are not additive because the ROC curve is plotted on a logarithmic scale, so 0.92 minus 0.78 does not equal a 0.14 improvement in discrimination capability between models","B) AUC measures discrimination ability across all thresholds not accuracy, and 0.14 AUC points is not the same as a 14% relative improvement in any meaningful classification performance metric","C) The comparison is invalid because AUC can only be meaningfully compared between models trained on the exact same algorithm family, not across different model architectures or feature sets used","D) AUC of 0.92 means the model correctly classifies 92% of all samples while 0.78 means 78% correct, so the true difference is 14 percentage points of accuracy not 14 percent relative","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Two errors: (1) AUC ≠ accuracy — AUC = probability of ranking a random positive above a random negative. (2) 0.14 AUC units ≠ ‘14%.” B is wrong: ROC uses linear axes. C is wrong: AUC CAN compare across models. D directly states the AUC=accuracy misconception."},
{"id":56,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A colleague uses 10-fold cross-validation and reports 90% average accuracy. They conclude 'since we used CV, our model cannot be overfit.' What is fundamentally wrong with this reasoning?","options":["A) Cross-validation detects overfitting by estimating generalization error, but it does not prevent it; if hyperparameters were tuned using the CV score directly, the reported 90% is optimistically biased","B) Cross-validation only prevents overfitting when the number of folds equals the number of training samples, which is known as leave-one-out cross-validation and is the only unbiased variant","C) Cross-validation cannot detect overfitting at all because it trains on the same underlying dataset in every fold, making it equivalent to evaluating on the training set with extra computation","D) Cross-validation eliminates overfitting entirely because each fold uses a separate validation set, ensuring the model never sees the same data during training and evaluation simultaneously","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"CV is a DIAGNOSTIC tool that estimates generalization error — it does not prevent overfitting. If you use CV scores to select hyperparameters and then report those same CV scores, you get optimistic bias (need nested CV). The model itself can still overfit within each fold. B wrongly claims CV eliminates overfitting. C invents a rule about LOO. D goes too far — CV does detect overfitting, it just does not prevent it."},
{"id":57,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Rare disease: 2% prevalence, 200 samples (≈4 positive cases). A colleague uses regular 5-fold CV. What specific failure mode can occur compared to stratified k-fold in this extreme imbalance scenario?","options":["A) Regular k-fold would automatically oversample the minority class within each fold to ensure balanced class representation, which introduces artificial data that biases the performance estimate","B) Class imbalance makes k-fold cross-validation theoretically invalid regardless of stratification because the folds cannot maintain statistical independence with so few positive case samples","C) Regular k-fold places all four positive cases in the same fold, which improves that fold’s evaluation accuracy but leaves the other four folds with no positive samples to evaluate against","D) A fold might contain zero positive cases, making training on that fold impossible and evaluation meaningless; stratified k-fold ensures each fold preserves the original 2% class distribution ratio","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With only 4 positives in 5 folds, regular k-fold might put all positives in 1–2 folds, leaving others with ZERO (can’t compute recall, precision, etc.). Stratified ensures each fold gets ~1 positive. B partially identifies the problem. C is wrong: stratified CV is valid. D is wrong: k-fold doesn’t automatically oversample."},
{"id":58,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Precision=0.75, Recall=0.50. A colleague computes the arithmetic mean: (0.75+0.50)/2=0.625 and says ‘F1 must be wrong since it gives 0.60 not 0.625.’ Why is F1 deliberately lower than the arithmetic mean?","options":["A) F1 subtracts a correction factor for class imbalance from the arithmetic mean, which accounts for the base rate of positive cases in the dataset during the final metric calculation step","B) F1 uses the harmonic mean 2PR/(P+R) which is always less than or equal to the arithmetic mean and penalizes imbalance: it rewards balanced precision and recall over one high and one low","C) F1 uses the geometric mean square root of P times R, which gives a lower result than the arithmetic mean whenever precision and recall differ, incentivizing models to equalize both metric values","D) F1 is computed as the minimum of precision and recall which equals 0.50, but adds a bonus of 0.10 for the precision-recall gap being less than 0.30, giving the adjusted final value of 0.60","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"F1 = 2PR/(P+R) = 2(0.75)(0.50)/(1.25) = 0.60. Harmonic mean ≤ arithmetic mean, with the gap increasing when values differ. This PENALIZES imbalance. B: geometric mean would give ≈0.61. C and D describe nonexistent formulas."},
{"id":59,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"House price prediction: $10K error on a $100K house vs $10K error on a $1M house. A colleague uses MAE, arguing ‘a dollar error is a dollar error regardless of the house’s value.’ Why might MAPE be more appropriate?","options":["A) MAE is biased toward expensive houses because their residuals are naturally larger in absolute terms, while MAPE eliminates this systematic bias by normalizing errors against the predicted values","B) A $10K error represents 10% of a $100K house but only 1% of a $1M house; MAPE captures this relative significance which better reflects the actual economic impact of prediction errors","C) MAE fundamentally cannot handle heteroscedastic data where variance changes with the target value level, while MAPE automatically corrects for heteroscedasticity through its percentage normalization","D) MAPE should always be used instead of MAE for all regression problems because percentage errors are universally more interpretable than absolute errors across all domains and target value ranges","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"$10K on $100K house (10%) is much more significant than on $1M house (1%). MAPE normalizes by actual value. B is partially right but normalizes by actual, not predicted. C overgeneralizes: MAPE fails near zero. D: MAE doesn’t ‘handle’ heteroscedasticity but MAPE doesn’t ‘correct’ it either."},
{"id":60,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A data scientist evaluates on the test set, tunes hyperparameters, re-evaluates on the same test set, repeats until reaching 91% accuracy. A colleague says this is fine since ‘the test data was never used for training.’ What subtle leakage is occurring?","options":["A) No leakage occurs because test data was never included in gradient computations during backpropagation, which is the only mechanism through which information can leak from test data into model weights","B) This iterative process is actually the standard recommended practice and is explicitly documented in scikit-learn’s official documentation as the correct workflow for model evaluation and selection","C) The leakage is negligible because hyperparameter tuning only changes high-level model properties like learning rate and regularization rather than directly modifying individual weight parameter values","D) Each evaluate-and-tune cycle leaks test set information into model selection decisions; the reported 91% is optimistically biased because the test set implicitly guided hyperparameter choices","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Each evaluate-tune cycle uses test performance to guide decisions, effectively ‘fitting’ to the test set through model selection. The 91% is optimistically biased. Correct workflow: validation set or CV for tuning, test set ONCE at the end. B defines leakage too narrowly. C underestimates impact. D is false."},
{"id":61,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"K-means with K=4 gives different clusters every run. A colleague suggests increasing K to 10 for more stability, arguing more clusters means less sensitivity to initialization. Does increasing K fix the instability?","options":["A) Yes: with more clusters each centroid captures a smaller more homogeneous group of points, which reduces the sensitivity to initial centroid placement and produces consistent results across runs","B) No: instability comes from random centroid initialization, not K; K-means++ initialization or running multiple restarts and selecting the lowest inertia result fixes the root cause directly","C) Yes: K=10 approaches the number of natural groups in most datasets, and as K matches the true cluster count the algorithm converges to the same stable solution regardless of initialization","D) No: increasing K actually increases instability because there are exponentially more possible configurations, giving the algorithm even more local minima to converge to unpredictably each run","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Instability = random initialization, not K. K-means++ (spreading initial centroids) or multiple restarts (picking lowest inertia) fix the root cause. Increasing K doesn’t fix initialization sensitivity. B’s logic is flawed. C is partially right but overstates. D assumes K=10 matches true structure."},
{"id":62,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"Tuscany tourist mobility data: some clusters are spherical (Florence), others elongated (coastline). K-means fails on coastal clusters. A colleague suggests K-means with Manhattan distance. Would this fix the shape problem?","options":["A) Yes: any non-Euclidean distance metric fundamentally changes K-means from a centroid-based algorithm to a density-based algorithm, enabling it to discover non-spherical cluster shapes automatically","B) No: but hierarchical clustering with single linkage would properly solve this because single linkage naturally creates chain-like elongated clusters by joining the two closest individual data points","C) Yes: Manhattan distance creates diamond-shaped decision boundaries that can approximate elongated clusters significantly better than the circular boundaries produced by Euclidean distance metrics","D) No: K-means assumes spherical clusters regardless of distance metric because it assigns points to nearest centroids; DBSCAN discovers arbitrary shapes based on local density patterns instead","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"K-means’ fundamental assumption is spherical clusters (minimizes distance to centroids). Changing distance metric changes shape slightly (circles→diamonds) but not arbitrary shapes. DBSCAN groups by density, handling ANY shape. B: diamonds aren’t arbitrary shapes. C: single linkage has problems. D is completely wrong."},
{"id":63,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"Elbow method: inertia drops sharply K=2→4, then gradually K=4→10. A colleague argues K=10 is optimal because it achieves the absolute lowest inertia value. Why is minimizing inertia not the correct objective?","options":["A) Inertia always decreases monotonically with K reaching zero at K=N, so minimizing it always selects K=N which is one point per cluster and completely meaningless; the elbow identifies diminishing returns","B) Minimizing inertia is actually the mathematically correct objective, and the elbow method is an outdated heuristic that has been fully replaced by the gap statistic in all modern clustering analysis","C) Inertia cannot be meaningfully compared across different values of K because the metric’s absolute scale changes with each additional cluster, making direct cross-K numerical comparisons statistically invalid","D) Inertia is systematically biased toward solutions with equal-sized clusters, so minimizing it forces an artificial balance between cluster sizes that may not reflect the true underlying data structure","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Inertia monotonically decreases as K increases (at K=N, inertia=0). Minimum inertia always picks K=N — useless. The elbow finds where the RATE of decrease drops sharply = diminishing returns. B is a concern but not why minimizing is wrong. C: inertia CAN be compared. D: elbow is still widely used."},
{"id":64,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"A data point has silhouette score −0.2. A colleague says this definitively identifies the point as an outlier that should be removed from the dataset before re-running the clustering algorithm. Is this interpretation correct?","options":["A) Yes: negative silhouette scores below the threshold of negative 0.1 are the standard criterion for identifying outliers in clustering analysis, and such points should always be removed before reclustering","B) Yes: the negative score indicates the point lies in an overlapping region between two cluster probability distributions, which means it contains noise that would contaminate both clusters if retained","C) No: silhouette scores mathematically cannot be negative because they measure the ratio of intra-cluster to inter-cluster distance, and distance ratios are always positive values by mathematical definition","D) No: a negative silhouette score means the point is closer to a neighboring cluster than its own, suggesting misassignment to the wrong cluster rather than necessarily being an outlier data point","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Silhouette = (b−a)/max(a,b), range [−1,+1]. Negative means closer to neighboring cluster (b<a) — likely MISASSIGNED, not necessarily an outlier. Reassigning it might improve the score. B invents a threshold. C is wrong: the formula produces negatives. D conflates misassignment with noise."},
{"id":65,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"DBSCAN labels some points as noise. A colleague argues this is a disadvantage because ‘every data point represents a real customer and we’re losing valuable business data.’ In what important scenario is noise detection actually critical?","options":["A) When outliers carry meaningful information such as fraud, manufacturing defects, or network intrusions, DBSCAN identifies them naturally while K-means forces them into the nearest cluster distorting centroids","B) Noise detection is only useful when the dataset contains more than 10% outliers; below this threshold the noise points have negligible impact on cluster quality and should simply be included in clusters","C) DBSCAN’s noise detection feature is only relevant when using DBSCAN as a preprocessing step for K-means, identifying points to systematically remove before the actual clustering algorithm runs on clean data","D) Noise detection is always a disadvantage in practical business scenarios because outliers often represent rare but highly valuable customer segments that carry disproportionately high lifetime business value","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In anomaly detection (fraud, defects, intrusions), the ‘noise’ points ARE the interesting data. K-means forces outliers into clusters, distorting centers and hiding anomalies. DBSCAN naturally separates them. B invents a threshold. C overgeneralizes. D mischaracterizes DBSCAN’s role."},
{"id":66,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"A business analyst wants customer segments at both 2-cluster and 6-cluster granularity from one analysis. A colleague suggests running K-means twice with K=2 and K=6. What is the limitation compared to hierarchical clustering?","options":["A) Running K-means twice requires exactly double the computation, while hierarchical clustering is always strictly faster because it only needs a single run to produce all possible cluster granularity levels","B) Two K-means runs produce independent unrelated solutions; hierarchical clustering’s dendrogram shows how six fine clusters nest within two broad clusters, preserving the hierarchical relationship","C) K-means cannot be run with different K values on the same dataset because the algorithm modifies the underlying data during its iterative training process, altering the input for subsequent runs","D) K-means with K=2 and K=6 produces identical cluster assignments because K-means always converges to the same underlying data structure regardless of the number of clusters specified in the input","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Two K-means runs are independent — the 2-cluster split may not align with how the 6-cluster solution groups data. Hierarchical clustering builds a nested tree where 6 clusters are sub-groups of 2. B is wrong. C: hierarchical is often SLOWER than K-means. D: K-means doesn’t modify data."},
{"id":67,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"5,000 features: a model using all features performs worse than one using only 50 features. A colleague diagnoses multicollinearity. While that may exist, what is the more fundamental explanation?","options":["A) The curse of dimensionality: in high-dimensional spaces data becomes extremely sparse, distance metrics lose discriminative power, and models overfit to noise in uninformative features","B) The model with 5,000 features is underfitting because it spreads its parameter budget across too many features, giving insufficient learning capacity per individual feature to detect meaningful patterns","C) The 50-feature model benefits from implicit regularization because having fewer features forces the optimizer to find the minimum-norm solution that automatically generalizes better to new data","D) Multicollinearity is the correct and complete diagnosis: correlated features inflate coefficient variance so dramatically that the model’s predictions become entirely unstable on unseen test data","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Curse of dimensionality: (1) sparsity, (2) distance concentration, (3) overfitting to noise in many uninformative dimensions. Multicollinearity (B) is ONE symptom, not the root cause. C inverts the problem: too many features causes overfitting, not underfitting. D is partially true but doesn’t explain why 5,000 features hurts."},
{"id":68,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"PCA on 100 features: first 5 PCs explain 95% of total variance. A colleague keeps only these 5 for a classification task, arguing 'we preserved 95% of the information so classification accuracy should be unaffected.' What is the flaw in this reasoning?","options":["A) Keeping only 5 PCs always reduces classification accuracy proportionally to the variance lost, so the model will be exactly 5% less accurate than using all original features","B) PCA maximizes variance which is unsupervised; the discarded 5% of variance might contain the most discriminative features for separating classes in the classification task","C) PCA components are nonlinear transformations of the original features, so they cannot be used as direct inputs to linear classifiers without first applying an inverse transformation","D) The 95% variance threshold is too aggressive; the standard practice requires retaining at least 99% of the total variance before any dimensionality reduction can be applied safely","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"PCA maximizes VARIANCE, which is unsupervised and unrelated to class separability. A direction with tiny variance could be the one that best separates classes. For example, if two classes differ on a feature with low variance, PCA might discard it. This is why supervised methods (LDA) or evaluating classification performance after PCA are important. B invents a proportional rule. C is wrong: PCA IS linear. D invents a threshold."},
{"id":69,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"PCA shows no clusters in 2D; t-SNE reveals clear groups. A colleague concludes t-SNE is always superior for visualization tasks. What critical caveat makes this conclusion problematic?","options":["A) t-SNE is actually a supervised method that uses class labels to create visually separated clusters, so its clear groups are a consequence of injected label information rather than genuine data structure","B) t-SNE can only reduce data to exactly two dimensions while PCA can reduce to any arbitrary number of dimensions, making PCA strictly more flexible for downstream machine learning tasks","C) t-SNE preserves local neighborhoods but distorts global distances and cluster sizes, so apparent visual separations can be artifacts; PCA’s linear projection faithfully preserves global variance structure","D) t-SNE results are fully deterministic and reproducible while PCA results depend on random initialization, making PCA more sensitive to the specific random seed used during the computation process","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"t-SNE distorts global structure: inter-cluster distances and cluster sizes are NOT meaningful. Two clusters appearing far apart might actually be close. PCA preserves global relationships faithfully. B is wrong: t-SNE is unsupervised. C inverts reality: t-SNE is non-deterministic. D: t-SNE can reduce to any dimensionality."},
{"id":70,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"Swiss roll: 2D manifold in 3D. Dropping the z-axis squashes layers together. A colleague says PCA would avoid this because it finds the ‘optimal’ projection that maximizes information preservation. Why is PCA also insufficient?","options":["A) PCA finds the best linear projection, but the Swiss roll has nonlinear structure; unrolling requires nonlinear manifold learning methods like t-SNE or Isomap that respect geodesic distances on the surface","B) PCA would work perfectly on the Swiss roll because the first two principal components capture the unrolled 2D surface, since PCA maximizes variance which naturally aligns with the unrolling direction","C) PCA fails specifically because it requires the data to follow a normal Gaussian distribution, and the Swiss roll has a uniform distribution on the manifold surface which violates this essential assumption","D) PCA cannot handle 3D input data; it is limited to datasets with more than ten dimensions because eigendecomposition becomes numerically unstable for small covariance matrices in low-dimensional spaces","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"PCA finds the LINEAR subspace maximizing variance. For the Swiss roll, max variance cuts ACROSS the rolled layers, squashing them. Unrolling requires understanding NONLINEAR manifold structure. B is wrong: PCA’s linear projection squashes. C: PCA doesn’t require normality. D: PCA works in any dimensionality."},
{"id":71,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"PCA’s first PC has high loadings on income, credit_limit, and spending. A colleague says these three features are redundant and two should be removed from the model entirely. Does PCA support this?","options":["A) Yes: high loadings on the same principal component is the standard statistical test for feature redundancy, and features loading together should be reduced to the single one with the highest loading","B) Not necessarily: but the features should be decorrelated through orthogonal rotation before modeling because PCA only identifies redundancy without actually addressing it in the original feature space","C) Yes: if three features load heavily onto one component they contain identical information by mathematical definition, and keeping all three triples the same signal in any downstream prediction model","D) Not necessarily: high loadings on the same PC means shared variance, but each feature may contribute unique information on other PCs; PCA captures their shared signal without discarding any feature","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Shared variance (high loadings on same PC) = correlation, not exact redundancy. Each feature may contribute unique variance on other PCs. PCA’s composite captures shared signal without needing to discard features. B overstates loadings as a redundancy test. C misapplies rotation. D: correlated ≠ identical."},
{"id":72,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Netflix: new user, only 2 movies watched. CF fails. A colleague suggests showing globally popular movies until more data accumulates. What advantage does content-based filtering have over this popularity approach?","options":["A) Popularity-based recommendations require real-time computation of global viewing statistics across the entire platform, which is more computationally expensive than content-based filtering for small user profiles","B) Globally popular movies have already been watched by most users on the platform, so recommending them provides no incremental value and increases the rate of new user churn during onboarding","C) Content-based filtering leverages the genres and features of those 2 watched movies to personalize recommendations immediately, rather than ignoring the user’s demonstrated preference signal entirely","D) Showing only popular movies creates a reinforcing feedback loop where popular content becomes even more popular, eventually reducing the diversity of content the platform produces and acquires","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The user already signaled preferences by watching 2 movies. Content-based filtering analyzes those movies’ features (genre, actors, style) to personalize immediately. Popularity ignores this signal. B is speculative. C: popularity stats are trivially pre-computed. D is a real long-term concern but not the immediate problem."},
{"id":73,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Amazon: ‘Customers who bought X also bought Y.’ A colleague says this is user-based CF because it explicitly mentions ‘customers.’ Why is this actually item-based collaborative filtering?","options":["A) It computes item-item co-occurrence similarity based on which items are frequently purchased together, not user-user similarity; the word customers describes the data source not the similarity type","B) It is a hybrid approach that cannot be classified as either user-based or item-based because it simultaneously computes both similarity types and combines them in a weighted ensemble model","C) It is actually content-based filtering because the system analyzes product attributes like category, brand, and price to find items with similar feature profiles to the item being purchased","D) It is user-based CF as the colleague states: the system finds users similar to the current buyer and recommends items those similar users purchased, leveraging the customer purchase history directly","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Despite mentioning ‘customers,’ the computation is item-item: which items are co-purchased. User-based CF would be ‘users similar to you liked Y.’ The phrase describes item co-occurrence aggregated across ALL customers, not user similarity. B takes the phrase at face value. C ignores co-purchase signal. D overcomplicates."},
{"id":74,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Spotify’s filter bubble: only recommends songs similar to current listening habits. A colleague suggests fixing this by randomly inserting songs from genres the user has never explored. What is the problem with pure random exploration?","options":["A) Random song insertion is the mathematically optimal strategy because it guarantees maximum diversity, which research consistently shows is the single primary driver of long-term user engagement metrics","B) Random exploration only works for new users during onboarding; for established users with clear and stable preference patterns, the system should exclusively exploit known preferences to maximize satisfaction","C) Random exploration risks alienating users with irrelevant content; multi-armed bandit algorithms balance exploitation of known preferences with intelligent exploration of novel but likely enjoyable content","D) Random insertion of unfamiliar songs is computationally expensive because it requires maintaining a separate index of all songs the user has never interacted with, growing linearly with catalog size","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Random exploration is too aggressive — death metal for a jazz lover wastes recommendations and annoys users. Multi-armed bandits use INFORMED exploration: try content with some probability of matching latent interests, learning from responses. B overstates diversity. C is too extreme. D: tracking unheard songs is trivial."},
{"id":75,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"User-item matrix: 10M users, 100K movies, 99.9%+ entries empty. A colleague says CF cannot work due to insufficient data density. Is this assessment correct?","options":["A) Yes: collaborative filtering requires at least 10% matrix density to compute reliable user or item similarities, and at 0.1% density the signal-to-noise ratio is far too low for meaningful recommendations","B) No: matrix factorization decomposes the sparse matrix into low-rank user and item factor matrices, discovering latent features that enable predicting missing entries despite extreme sparsity levels","C) Yes: the only viable approach for this extreme level of sparsity is pure content-based filtering because it relies on item features rather than user-item interactions and is unaffected by sparsity","D) No: but the correct approach is filling missing entries using mean imputation before applying CF, which converts the sparse problem into a dense matrix that collaborative filtering handles easily","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Matrix factorization (SVD, ALS) handles extreme sparsity by finding low-rank approximations. Users×K and K×Items matrices discover latent factors from available ratings and predict missing ones. B invents a density threshold. C: mean imputation destroys signal. D: CF with matrix factorization works — the Netflix Prize proved this."},
{"id":76,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"A content-based book recommender only suggests sci-fi to a sci-fi reader. A colleague proposes adding more sophisticated NLP features analyzing writing style and themes. Would better features solve the overspecialization problem?","options":["A) No: overspecialization can only be solved by completely removing the genre feature from the content representation, forcing the system to recommend based on other attributes that naturally cross genres","B) Yes: sophisticated features capturing deep narrative structure would reveal cross-genre similarities, such as a sci-fi novel and historical fiction sharing similar storytelling patterns and thematic complexity","C) No: overspecialization is inherent to content-based filtering regardless of feature quality; only collaborative filtering discovers cross-genre interests through users with overlapping but diverse taste profiles","D) Yes: NLP analysis of writing style would reveal that all books fundamentally share the same narrative patterns, effectively making every book similar to every other book and eliminating genre boundaries entirely","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Better features might find some cross-genre similarities (B has a point), but the fundamental limitation remains: content-based filtering can ONLY recommend items similar to what the user has seen. CF finds cross-genre interests through similar users’ diverse tastes. C: removing genre alone doesn’t fix structural limitation. D is absurd."},
{"id":77,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"GDPR: a customer’s loan is denied by a neural network. A colleague proposes showing the customer the model’s global feature importance rankings as the explanation. Why is this insufficient for GDPR compliance?","options":["A) Global feature importance fully satisfies GDPR requirements because the regulation only requires showing which features the model considers important, not explaining specific individual decision outcomes","B) GDPR does not apply to neural network models specifically because the regulation was drafted before deep learning became mainstream and has not been updated to explicitly cover these architectures","C) Neither global nor local explanations satisfy GDPR because the regulation mandates that all automated decisions be made exclusively by inherently interpretable models such as logistic regression","D) GDPR requires explaining this specific decision, not general model behavior; LIME provides local explanations by fitting an interpretable model around the individual denied application instance","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"GDPR’s right to explanation requires explaining why THIS person was denied, not how the model works generally. LIME generates local explanations specific to the individual case. B misinterprets GDPR. C is wrong: GDPR doesn’t mandate model types. D is wrong: GDPR applies to all automated decisions."},
{"id":78,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"SHAP values for an employee: salary=−0.15, commute=+0.25, tenure=−0.10, overtime=+0.20. A colleague interprets commute’s +0.25 as meaning ‘the employee has a 25-minute commute.’ What does +0.25 actually represent?","options":["A) The commute feature pushed this employee’s predicted turnover probability 0.25 above the average prediction; it quantifies the feature’s contribution to this specific prediction, not the feature’s raw value","B) The commute feature has a 25% probability of being the single most important feature for this specific prediction; SHAP values represent the likelihood that each feature is the primary outcome driver","C) The commute feature would change the overall prediction by exactly 0.25 if it were completely removed from the model; SHAP values measure the impact of feature ablation on each individual prediction","D) The employee’s commute distance is 0.25 standard deviations above the mean commute in the training data; SHAP normalizes all feature values to z-scores before computing their prediction contribution","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"SHAP decomposes predictions into feature contributions: prediction = base_value + Σ(SHAP). +0.25 means commute pushed this employee’s turnover prediction 0.25 above average. It’s a contribution to the PREDICTION, not the feature’s value (A), probability (B), z-score (C), or ablation impact (D)."},
{"id":79,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Permutation importance: shuffling ‘age’ drops accuracy from 88% to 72% (16% drop). Shuffling ‘purchase_count’ drops from 88% to 87% (1% drop). A colleague says age must be causing overfitting since the model depends on it so heavily. Is this correct?","options":["A) No: the 16% drop actually indicates that age is merely a proxy variable for other correlated features, and the model’s apparent reliance on it masks the true importance of those underlying features","B) Yes: permutation importance exceeding 10% is the standard threshold for identifying features that cause overfitting, and such features should be regularized or completely removed from the model","C) Yes: when a single feature causes such a dramatic accuracy drop, the model has overspecialized on that feature and would generalize better if the feature were removed or its influence were reduced","D) No: high permutation importance means the model relies on that feature for correct predictions, indicating genuine predictive signal, not overfitting; diagnosing overfitting requires train-test comparison","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"High permutation importance = the model NEEDS that feature for accurate predictions = genuine predictive power, NOT overfitting. Overfitting is diagnosed by comparing train vs test performance, not by feature importance. B incorrectly equates reliance with overfitting. C is possible but not what the colleague claimed. D invents a threshold."},
{"id":80,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"A colleague uses LIME (local, model-agnostic) to generate a global feature importance ranking by averaging LIME explanations across all predictions. Another colleague says this is invalid since ‘LIME is local not global.’ Who is right?","options":["A) The second colleague is right: aggregating local explanations violates the locality assumption that LIME’s linear surrogate model is only accurate within a small neighborhood around each individual prediction","B) The first colleague is right but should use SHAP instead, because SHAP is the only method whose mathematical foundations formally support both local explanation and global aggregation of explanations","C) The first colleague’s approach is valid: aggregating many local LIME explanations can produce meaningful global insights, similar to how SHAP summary plots aggregate local SHAP values across all instances","D) The second colleague is right: LIME explanations are computed on perturbed local neighborhoods that never overlap, so averaging them produces mathematically meaningless values across the entire dataset","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"LIME IS designed for local explanations, but aggregating them across many instances is valid — similar to SHAP summary plots. D raises a real concern about locality but doesn’t invalidate aggregation (you’re aggregating many local results, not extrapolating one). B is wrong. C: LIME aggregation is also valid."},
{"id":81,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"From the course’s explainability summary table: Decision Trees are intrinsic with global and local scope. LIME is model-agnostic with local scope only. Permutation Importance is model-agnostic with global scope only. Which method is model-agnostic AND provides both global and local explanations?","options":["A) SHAP, which works with any model type and provides both individual prediction explanations through local waterfall plots and aggregated feature importance summaries through global summary plots","B) Decision Trees, because they can be applied as surrogate models for any black-box model, making them effectively model-agnostic while retaining their natural global and local interpretability properties","C) LIME, because while designed for local explanations, its perturbation-based approach can be trivially extended to produce global explanations by increasing the perturbation neighborhood size parameter","D) Permutation Importance, because shuffling features can be applied locally to individual predictions and globally across the entire dataset to provide both levels of explanation simultaneously","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"From the lecture table: SHAP = model-agnostic, global & local. A: Permutation Importance is global only. B: Decision Trees are intrinsic (not model-agnostic). D: LIME’s extension to global isn’t trivial and it’s still classified as local. SHAP is the only method checking all boxes."},
{"id":82,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"Amazon’s hiring AI trained on 10 years of male-dominated resumes penalized ‘women’s chess club’ and all-women’s colleges. Removing the gender feature did not fix the bias. A colleague says the remaining bias must be an algorithm bug. Why is this wrong?","options":["A) The algorithm is functioning correctly and the observed bias is actually desirable because it reflects genuine differences in qualification patterns between male and female engineering candidates historically","B) Bias persists through proxy features like college names, activities, and word choice that correlate with gender; removing the explicit feature does not remove implicit gender encoding throughout the data","C) The bias persists because the algorithm was not retrained after removing the gender feature column; simply deleting a column requires complete model retraining to eliminate the feature’s learned influence","D) The remaining bias is not actually gender-related; it is a coincidental pattern from the specific ten-year data window that happens to correlate with gender but has no causal relationship to it","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Classic failure of ‘fairness through unawareness.’ Gender is encoded in proxies: all-women’s colleges, ‘women’s chess club,’ writing style. The model learned these from biased data. B is ethically and legally wrong. C: even with retraining, proxies persist. D is implausible given systematic discrimination."},
{"id":83,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"A bank uses a 50M-parameter neural network for credit scoring. Under GDPR, a customer requests an explanation for their denial. A colleague suggests training a global decision tree surrogate model. What limitation does this have?","options":["A) Global surrogate models are explicitly prohibited under GDPR because they provide an approximate explanation of an approximate model, creating a chain of approximations that regulators consider insufficient","B) A global surrogate approximates overall behavior but may not faithfully represent this specific decision; LIME or SHAP explain the actual model’s individual prediction more accurately for the customer","C) A decision tree surrogate would perfectly satisfy GDPR compliance requirements because the regulation only requires that an explanation be provided in some generally understandable format for consumers","D) The surrogate approach is fundamentally invalid because a decision tree cannot approximate a neural network with fifty million parameters; the approximation error would be far too large for practical use","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A global surrogate mimics the neural network ON AVERAGE but may disagree on specific predictions. The customer needs an explanation of THEIR denial, not the model’s general behavior. LIME/SHAP explain the actual model’s specific decision. B: no such prohibition. C oversimplifies GDPR. D: trees CAN approximate at high level."},
{"id":84,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"Healthcare AI: AUC=0.95 for white patients, AUC=0.75 for non-white patients. A colleague suggests adding ‘race’ as a feature so the model can learn race-specific patterns and equalize performance. What is wrong with this approach?","options":["A) Adding race would fix the performance gap because the model could learn separate optimized decision boundaries for each racial group, equalizing predictions within each demographic category independently","B) Adding race would have no measurable effect because the model has already implicitly learned all racial patterns through proxy features like zip code, insurance type, and healthcare utilization history","C) Adding race risks encoding direct racial discrimination into the model; the correct fix is collecting more representative training data for underrepresented groups to equalize performance organically","D) The performance gap is acceptable because AUC of 0.75 still exceeds the standard clinical threshold of 0.70 that healthcare regulators require for all demographic subgroups in AI system deployment","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Using race directly creates discriminatory infrastructure and legal liability. The root cause is underrepresentation in training data. Collecting diverse data addresses this. B might improve metrics but creates discriminatory models. C invents a regulatory threshold and ignores a massive 0.20 AUC gap. D might be partially true but doesn’t justify adding race."},
{"id":85,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"A deep learning researcher trains a ResNet on ImageNet using two configurations: (A) batch size 32 with learning rate 0.01, and (B) batch size 1024 with learning rate 0.01. Both run for the same number of epochs. Configuration A achieves 76% test accuracy while B achieves only 73%. The researcher's manager asks why larger batch size performed worse despite seeing the same data. The technical explanation for the performance gap AND its connection to the loss landscape geometry are:","options":["A) Smaller batches achieve better accuracy because they see more diverse mini-batches per epoch, effectively augmenting the training data through random sampling; larger batches see less diversity and therefore learn fewer patterns from the same dataset","B) Smaller batches introduce gradient noise that biases the optimizer toward flat minima (robust to perturbations and generalizing better), while large batches converge to sharp minima (sensitive to perturbations and generalizing poorly) because the smoother gradient estimates follow steeper descent paths","C) Smaller batches create an implicit regularization effect identical to L2 weight decay with strength proportional to the inverse of the batch size; the larger batch simply needs a correspondingly larger L2 penalty to match the smaller batch performance","D) Smaller batches process data faster per epoch due to lower memory overhead, giving the model more effective training iterations in the same wall-clock time; the performance difference is purely a function of computational throughput not optimization dynamics","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Research (Keskar et al., 2017) shows small batches find flat minima (low curvature = robust generalization) while large batches find sharp minima (high curvature = poor generalization). The gradient noise in small batches helps escape sharp minima. B is wrong about speed. C oversimplifies the relationship. D mischaracterizes the diversity effect."},
{"id":86,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"A team trains a neural network and plots the loss landscape. They discover the optimization gets stuck at a point where the gradient is nearly zero, but the loss is still high (0.42 vs target 0.15). A colleague says \"we're stuck in a local minimum.\" The team adds momentum to the optimizer and the loss drops to 0.18. The most likely explanation for where the optimizer was stuck AND why momentum helped is:","options":["A) The optimizer was at a true local minimum (gradient exactly zero with positive curvature in all directions); momentum helped by adding sufficient kinetic energy to escape over the barrier separating this local minimum from the deeper global minimum basin","B) The optimizer was at a point where the learning rate was too small relative to the local curvature; momentum effectively increases the learning rate in consistent gradient directions, compensating for the suboptimal static learning rate setting","C) The optimizer was at a plateau caused by saturated sigmoid activations (vanishing gradients); momentum helped by accumulating small gradient signals over many iterations until the accumulated update was large enough to push the weights out of the saturation region","D) The optimizer was likely at a saddle point (gradient near zero but the point is a minimum in some dimensions and maximum in others); in high-dimensional spaces saddle points are far more common than true local minima, and momentum's accumulated velocity pushes through the flat region","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In high-dimensional spaces (neural networks have millions of dimensions), saddle points vastly outnumber local minima. At a saddle point, the Hessian has both positive and negative eigenvalues. Momentum accumulates velocity that pushes through flat saddle regions. B assumes a true local minimum (rare in high dimensions). C describes vanishing gradients (different problem). D partially describes momentum but misidentifies the stuck point."},
{"id":87,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"An ML engineer implements gradient descent with momentum (velocity term v = 0.9v - α∇J(θ)). They notice the optimizer overshoots the minimum and oscillates around it before settling, even with a reasonable learning rate. A colleague suggests switching to Adam optimizer. The specific problem with standard momentum in this scenario AND the mechanism by which Adam addresses it are:","options":["A) Standard momentum accumulates velocity that causes overshooting near the minimum where gradients are small; Adam adapts the learning rate per-parameter by dividing by the square root of the exponential moving average of squared gradients, automatically reducing step size in dimensions that have been oscillating","B) Standard momentum only considers the first moment (mean) of the gradients while ignoring gradient variance; Adam uses both first and second moments but its key innovation is computing exact Hessian diagonals without the computational cost of full second-order methods","C) Standard momentum uses a fixed velocity coefficient (0.9) that cannot adapt to the loss landscape curvature; Adam replaces this with an adaptive coefficient that increases in flat regions and decreases near the minimum based on gradient magnitude","D) Standard momentum treats all parameters equally regardless of their gradient scale; Adam implements parameter-specific learning rates by maintaining a count of how many times each parameter's gradient has been nonzero, reducing rates for frequently updated parameters","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Standard momentum's accumulated velocity overshoots when approaching the minimum. Adam combines momentum (first moment of gradients) with RMSProp-style adaptive learning rates (second moment of gradients). By dividing by sqrt(second moment), parameters with large/oscillating gradients get smaller effective learning rates. B mischaracterizes Adam's coefficient. C is wrong about Hessian computation. D describes AdaGrad's behavior, not Adam's."},
{"id":88,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"A team trains a neural network for medical image classification. Without dropout, the model achieves 97% train accuracy and 84% test accuracy. They add dropout with rate p=0.5 to hidden layers. After retraining, train accuracy drops to 89% and test accuracy rises to 91%. A junior team member is confused: \"If we randomly disable half the neurons during training, how can the model possibly perform BETTER?\" The correct explanation of dropout's mechanism AND why training accuracy dropped are:","options":["A) Dropout adds noise to the gradient computation by randomly skipping backpropagation through 50% of neurons, which acts like a noisy SGD variant; training accuracy drops because the noisy gradients prevent the model from reaching the true minimum of the loss function","B) Dropout is mathematically equivalent to L2 regularization with a penalty proportional to the dropout rate; training accuracy drops for the same reason it drops with L2 regularization — the model is penalized for having large weight values","C) Dropout prevents neuron co-adaptation by randomly zeroing 50% of neuron outputs each forward pass, forcing each neuron to learn independently useful features; training accuracy drops because the model is effectively trained as an ensemble of exponentially many sub-networks, each weaker individually","D) Dropout prevents neurons from memorizing specific training examples by randomly removing the neurons that store those memories during each training step; training accuracy drops because the model physically has fewer parameters available during training evaluation","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Dropout's key mechanism: it forces each neuron to be useful independently, preventing co-adaptation where neurons rely on specific other neurons. During training, random subsets of neurons are zeroed, creating an implicit ensemble of 2^N sub-networks. Training accuracy drops because the model is evaluated with dropout active (weakened sub-network). At test time, all neurons are active (scaled), combining the ensemble. B mischaracterizes dropout. C confuses training and gradient effects. D oversimplifies — the equivalence is approximate and limited to specific model families."},
{"id":89,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"A data scientist selects the regularization parameter λ for Ridge regression by running 5-fold cross-validation over a grid of λ values [0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100]. The best CV score is at λ=1 with 88% accuracy. She reports this as the expected production performance. An experienced colleague warns this estimate is optimistically biased. The reason for the bias AND the correct methodology to get an unbiased estimate are:","options":["A) The bias occurs because the λ grid is too coarse, potentially missing the true optimal value between grid points; the fix is to use randomized search over a continuous distribution of λ values to eliminate discretization bias in the estimate","B) The 5-fold CV score is biased because each fold uses only 80% of the data for training, so the model is weaker than one trained on all data; the fix is to retrain on all data with λ=1 and evaluate on a completely new held-out test set for an unbiased estimate","C) The bias occurs because Ridge regression's CV score is computed using the training-fold residuals rather than the validation-fold residuals; the fix is to ensure the CV implementation correctly evaluates on the held-out fold, not the training fold","D) Using the same CV procedure to both select λ and estimate performance creates optimistic bias because the reported score is the BEST across multiple λ values; nested cross-validation (inner loop for λ selection, outer loop for unbiased performance estimation) is needed","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"This is the hyperparameter selection bias problem. When you try 6 λ values and pick the best, you're selecting the λ that got lucky on THIS particular CV split. The reported 88% is the MAXIMUM across 6 runs, which is biased upward. Nested CV: inner 5-fold selects λ, outer 5-fold gives unbiased performance estimate. B describes a real concern but not the selection bias. C addresses a different problem. D describes an implementation bug, not a methodological bias."},
{"id":90,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"A machine learning textbook shows the L1 (Lasso) constraint region as a diamond shape and the L2 (Ridge) constraint region as a circle. Both are shown with elliptical MSE contours touching the constraint boundary. A student asks: \"Why does the diamond shape produce EXACTLY zero coefficients while the circle doesn't?\" The geometric explanation for why L1 produces sparsity AND why L2 cannot is:","options":["A) The diamond's corners lie on coordinate axes where some coefficients are exactly zero; elliptical contours are far more likely to first touch a corner (axis-aligned point) than a face of the diamond, while the circle has no corners so contact points almost never lie exactly on an axis","B) The diamond has a smaller total area than the circle for the same constraint strength, so it constrains coefficients more aggressively; the L2 circle allows larger coefficient combinations because circles enclose more area than diamonds with the same perimeter","C) The diamond's edges are straight lines that create linear programming constraints; linear programs always have solutions at vertices (corners) where coefficients are zero, while the circle creates a quadratic constraint whose solutions are never at exact axis intersections","D) The diamond shape is actually an approximation of the true L1 constraint which is a smooth curve; the sparsity occurs because the L1 gradient is discontinuous at zero, creating a \"dead zone\" where coefficients cannot escape once they reach zero during optimization","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The key geometric insight: the diamond's corners protrude along axes (where coefficients are zero). Elliptical contours approaching from any direction are most likely to first touch a corner (probability 1 in high dimensions) rather than a flat edge. The circle has no corners — tangent points can be anywhere on the surface, and landing exactly on an axis has probability zero. B conflates area with sparsity. C overcomplicates with linear programming. D confuses the gradient view with the constraint view."},
{"id":91,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A bank's logistic regression model for loan approval outputs P(default) = 0.45 for an applicant. Using the standard threshold of 0.5, the loan is approved. However, the bank's cost analysis shows that the cost of a defaulted loan is 10x the profit from a successful loan. A risk analyst argues the approval was wrong despite P(default) < 0.5. The analyst's reasoning AND the optimal threshold adjustment given the cost asymmetry are:","options":["A) The analyst is wrong: logistic regression's probability outputs are already calibrated to account for the training data's default rate, so applying a different threshold would distort the calibrated probabilities and produce worse decisions overall","B) The analyst is correct: with 10:1 cost ratio, the threshold should be lowered to approximately 1/(1+10) ≈ 0.09; at P(default)=0.45, the expected cost of approving far exceeds the expected profit because 0.45 times the default cost exceeds 0.55 times the profit","C) The analyst is wrong: the 0.5 threshold is derived from Bayes optimal decision theory and is always optimal regardless of cost asymmetry; the cost ratio should instead be incorporated by adjusting the regularization strength during model training","D) The analyst is correct but the threshold should be raised to 0.90 (not lowered) because higher thresholds are more conservative and reject more applicants; with 10:1 cost ratio, only applicants with over 90% approval probability should receive loans","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"With asymmetric costs, the decision threshold must account for the cost ratio. If cost_FN = 10 * benefit_TN, then approve only when: P(no default) * benefit > P(default) * cost. For P(default)=0.45: 0.55 * 1 < 0.45 * 10 = 4.5 — expected cost of approval massively exceeds expected benefit. The optimal threshold is ~0.09, not 0.5. B incorrectly claims 0.5 is always optimal. C inverts the threshold direction. D confuses calibration with decision theory."},
{"id":92,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A healthcare startup trains a logistic regression model to predict diabetes from blood test features. Feature \"glucose\" has coefficient +2.1 and feature \"blood_pressure\" has coefficient +0.3. A doctor interprets this as: \"glucose is 7 times more important than blood pressure for predicting diabetes.\" The flaw in this interpretation AND what would make the comparison valid are:","options":["A) Coefficients in logistic regression represent log-odds ratios, not direct importance measures; they should be exponentiated before comparison, making the ratio exp(2.1)/exp(0.3) ≈ 6.05, not 7, which slightly changes the doctor's interpretation","B) Raw coefficients reflect both feature importance AND feature scale; if glucose is measured in mg/dL (range 70-200) and blood pressure in mmHg (range 60-180), the coefficients are not comparable without first standardizing both features to the same scale (mean=0, std=1)","C) The comparison is invalid because logistic regression coefficients are only meaningful for binary features; for continuous features like glucose and blood pressure, partial dependence plots should be used instead of raw coefficient comparisons","D) The doctor's interpretation is completely correct: in logistic regression, coefficients directly represent the relative importance of each feature, and a 7:1 ratio means glucose contributes exactly 7 times more to the log-odds of diabetes prediction","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Coefficient magnitude depends on feature scale. If glucose ranges 70-200 (130-unit range) and BP ranges 60-180 (120-unit range), their coefficients reflect different units. Standardizing features (z-scores) before training makes coefficients comparable because a 1-unit change means 1-standard-deviation change for both. B ignores scale. C's exponentiation gives odds ratios but doesn't fix the scale problem. D is wrong — coefficients are meaningful for continuous features."},
{"id":93,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"An e-commerce company uses a decision tree to classify products as \"likely to be returned\" or \"keep.\" The tree has a node that splits on \"price > €47.50\" with Gini impurity 0.48 before the split. After the split: left child (price ≤ €47.50) has 200 samples with Gini 0.10; right child (price > €47.50) has 800 samples with Gini 0.45. A data scientist says this is a good split because the left child is nearly pure. The correct assessment of whether this split is actually good AND the metric that reveals the truth are:","options":["A) The split is excellent because creating any child node with Gini 0.10 is valuable regardless of the other child; decision trees evaluate splits by the best child's purity, not the weighted average, since the pure child provides a reliable classification region","B) The split is poor because the class ratio reversal between children (pure left vs impure right) indicates the split threshold of €47.50 was chosen to overfit a small cluster of low-price returns rather than capturing a genuine price-return relationship","C) The split is mediocre despite the pure left child: the weighted average Gini after splitting is (200/1000)*0.10 + (800/1000)*0.45 = 0.38, reducing impurity by only 0.10 from the parent's 0.48; a good split would reduce impurity substantially across BOTH children, not just one small one","D) The split cannot be evaluated from Gini alone because Gini impurity does not account for the number of samples in each child; entropy-based information gain should be used instead as it naturally weights children by their sample counts during the split evaluation","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The information gain (Gini reduction) = parent Gini - weighted child Gini = 0.48 - [(200/1000)*0.10 + (800/1000)*0.45] = 0.48 - 0.38 = 0.10. This is a modest improvement. The left child is pure but tiny (200/1000), while the right (800/1000) barely improved. A truly good split would substantially reduce impurity across BOTH children. B incorrectly evaluates by best child only. C speculates about overfitting without evidence. D is wrong — Gini IS weighted by sample counts in standard implementations."},
{"id":94,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A data scientist at a pharmaceutical company preprocesses clinical trial data by standardizing all features (subtract mean, divide by standard deviation) using the ENTIRE dataset before splitting into train and test sets. She then trains a model on the training set and evaluates on the test set. Her manager, a statistician, flags this as a critical error even though the model shows 94% accuracy. The specific form of data leakage AND its practical impact on the reported accuracy are:","options":["A) The test set's mean and standard deviation influenced the scaling applied to training data (and vice versa), so the model indirectly learned information about test set distributions; the reported 94% is optimistically biased and true accuracy on unseen data will be lower","B) Standardization removes important distributional information from the features that the model needs for classification; the 94% accuracy is underestimated because the model would perform better on raw unstandardized features that preserve distributional properties","C) The leakage occurs because standardization makes all features have identical distributions, removing the signal that distinguishes informative features from uninformative ones; the model's 94% accuracy is coincidental and would not replicate","D) The leakage is negligible for standardization because the mean and standard deviation are summary statistics that reveal no information about individual test samples; the manager's concern would be valid for more complex preprocessing like PCA but not for simple scaling","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Fitting the scaler on the full dataset means test set statistics (mean, std) leak into training data preprocessing. In extreme cases (small datasets), this can meaningfully inflate performance. Correct workflow: fit scaler ONLY on training data, then apply (transform) to both train and test. B inverts the problem. C underestimates the leakage — it IS real, especially with small samples. D mischaracterizes what standardization does."},
{"id":95,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A team evaluates three models on the same binary classification task. Model A: precision=0.90, recall=0.90, F1=0.90. Model B: precision=0.99, recall=0.60, F1=0.75. Model C: precision=0.60, recall=0.99, F1=0.75. Both B and C have F1=0.75, but they will behave very differently in production. The scenario where Model B is strongly preferred over Model C AND the scenario where Model C is strongly preferred over Model B are:","options":["A) Model B (high precision) is preferred for spam filtering where false positives (blocking legitimate emails) are very costly; Model C (high recall) is preferred for cancer screening where false negatives (missing actual cancer) are dangerous and follow-up tests are cheap","B) Model B is preferred when the model will be deployed without human oversight because high precision means fewer errors to correct; Model C is preferred when human reviewers will check every prediction because high recall ensures they see all relevant cases","C) Model B is preferred in real-time systems because high precision requires fewer computational resources to verify predictions; Model C is preferred in batch processing systems where recall is more important than latency and precision can be traded off","D) Model B is preferred when the dataset is highly imbalanced because high precision ensures the minority class predictions are reliable; Model C is preferred when classes are balanced because high recall ensures comprehensive coverage of both classes equally","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Precision vs recall tradeoff depends on the COST of errors. High precision (B: 99% of positive predictions are correct) = few false positives = best when false alarms are expensive (spam filter blocking real emails, fraud alerts freezing accounts). High recall (C: catches 99% of actual positives) = few false negatives = best when missing cases is dangerous (cancer screening, security threats). B conflates with class balance. C conflates with compute. D oversimplifies."},
{"id":96,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A medical device company trains a model to detect a rare condition (0.1% prevalence) from sensor data. They report ROC-AUC = 0.98 and claim the model is \"production-ready.\" A senior data scientist points out that despite the excellent AUC, the model would have a positive predictive value (precision) of only ~5% in deployment. The reason a high AUC can coexist with low precision AND the metric the company should additionally report are:","options":["A) High AUC guarantees high precision at all operating thresholds because the ROC curve measures both true positive rate and false positive rate simultaneously; the senior data scientist's calculation of 5% precision must contain an error in the base rate assumption","B) AUC and precision measure completely different properties of different models; AUC evaluates the ranking model while precision evaluates the classification model, and the two cannot be compared because they operate in different mathematical frameworks","C) AUC is computed on the balanced test set used during development, but deployment prevalence is 0.1%; the AUC value itself would drop dramatically in production because the ROC curve shape changes when the class distribution changes from balanced to imbalanced","D) AUC measures the model's ability to rank positive cases above negative ones across all thresholds, but at any specific operating threshold with 0.1% prevalence, even a small false positive rate produces overwhelmingly more false alarms than true detections; precision-recall AUC (PR-AUC) better reflects performance on rare events","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"AUC measures ranking quality (threshold-independent). But with 0.1% prevalence, even 1% FPR means: for every true positive, there are ~10 false positives (0.01 * 999 negatives ≈ 10 vs 0.99 * 1 positive ≈ 1). So precision ≈ 1/11 ≈ 9%. PR-AUC or precision at the chosen threshold better captures real-world utility for rare events. B is wrong: ROC-AUC is threshold-independent and doesn't change with prevalence. C is wrong: high AUC doesn't guarantee high precision. D mischaracterizes the relationship."},
{"id":97,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A team builds a customer churn model and reports performance using time-based train-test split: training on January-September data, testing on October-December data. A colleague suggests they should use 10-fold cross-validation instead for a more robust estimate. The team's data scientist objects. The reason the time-based split is CORRECT for this problem AND what would go wrong with standard k-fold CV are:","options":["A) Time-based splits are preferred because they create larger test sets (3 months vs ~1 month per fold), giving more statistical power; k-fold CV would give noisier estimates because each fold contains fewer samples from the target churn period","B) The time-based split is correct because k-fold CV requires the data to be independently and identically distributed, and customer behavior changes seasonally; the time split captures the most recent seasonal pattern while CV averages over all seasons equally","C) Churn prediction is temporal: the model must predict future events from past data; standard k-fold would let the model train on October data and test on August data (future predicting past), creating temporal leakage that inflates performance estimates unrealistically","D) The time-based split is correct because churn models must be validated on the most recent data to satisfy regulatory requirements; k-fold CV would include outdated data in the test folds that no longer reflects current customer behavior patterns","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"For time-series / temporal prediction, standard k-fold CV creates data leakage: the model would train on future data to predict past events, which is impossible in production. A customer who churned in October would influence the model that predicts September churn. Time-based splits (or time-series CV with expanding windows) prevent this. B focuses on set size, missing the leakage. C mentions IID but misidentifies the core issue. D invents regulatory requirements."},
{"id":98,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A team fine-tunes a pre-trained ImageNet model (ResNet-50) for classifying 5 types of skin lesions from dermoscopy images. They have only 2,000 labeled images. They freeze all layers except the final classification layer and train for 50 epochs. Results: 91% train accuracy, 89% test accuracy. A colleague suggests unfreezing all layers and training end-to-end with a very small learning rate. The rationale for this approach AND the risk it introduces are:","options":["A) Unfreezing allows the optimizer to find a globally optimal solution instead of being constrained to the subspace defined by frozen features; the risk is purely computational because training all parameters requires proportionally more GPU memory and training time","B) Unfreezing allows the pre-trained features to adapt to dermoscopy-specific patterns (medical images differ from ImageNet); however, with only 2,000 samples, full fine-tuning risks catastrophic forgetting where the model overwrites useful pre-trained features with noise from the small dataset","C) Unfreezing all layers converts the transfer learning setup into training from scratch, discarding all pre-trained knowledge; the model would need at least 100,000 labeled dermoscopy images to recover the performance lost by resetting all weights to random initialization","D) Unfreezing is unnecessary because ImageNet features are universal and equally applicable to all image domains; the small learning rate would have no effect on the frozen layers anyway since gradients cannot flow through frozen parameters regardless of learning rate","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Transfer learning from ImageNet provides general visual features (edges, textures). Unfreezing with a small learning rate lets deeper layers adapt to dermoscopy-specific patterns (lesion borders, pigment patterns) not present in ImageNet. But with 2,000 samples, there is a real risk of catastrophic forgetting — overwriting useful general features. B is wrong: ImageNet features aren't universal for medical imaging. C is wrong: unfreezing ≠ training from scratch; weights start from pre-trained values. D ignores the generalization risk."},
{"id":99,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A team adds Batch Normalization (BN) to their deep network and observes: (1) training converges 3x faster, (2) they can use a higher learning rate without divergence, and (3) test accuracy improves by 2%. A colleague attributes all three improvements to BN's \"regularization effect.\" The primary mechanism of BN AND why the colleague's attribution is only partially correct are:","options":["A) BN's primary mechanism is adding Gaussian noise to the activations during training, which acts as a powerful regularizer similar to dropout; all three improvements stem from this regularization effect because regularization simultaneously prevents overfitting and stabilizes training dynamics","B) BN's primary mechanism is implicitly performing feature selection by normalizing uninformative features to zero variance; the speed improvement comes from processing fewer effective features, the learning rate tolerance from reduced parameter count, and the test improvement from the reduced model complexity","C) BN's primary mechanism is rescaling the gradients during backpropagation to prevent both vanishing and exploding gradients; the speed improvement comes from gradient stabilization, the higher learning rate tolerance from gradient clipping, and the test improvement from the effective architecture change","D) BN's primary mechanism is normalizing layer inputs to have zero mean and unit variance, stabilizing the distribution of activations across layers (reducing internal covariate shift); the speed and learning rate improvements come from this stabilization, while only the test accuracy improvement can be partially attributed to regularization from mini-batch noise","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"BN normalizes activations to N(0,1) within each mini-batch, stabilizing training (primary purpose). This stabilization explains the faster convergence and higher learning rate tolerance. BN does have a mild regularization effect (mini-batch statistics add noise, similar to dropout) which partially explains the test accuracy gain, but regularization is a SECONDARY effect. B wrongly claims regularization is the primary mechanism. C mischaracterizes BN as gradient rescaling. D completely mischaracterizes BN."},
{"id":100,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A computer vision team must choose between two architectures for real-time object detection in autonomous driving: (A) a deeper network with 152 layers achieving 95.2% accuracy at 3 frames per second, and (B) a shallower network with 18 layers achieving 92.8% accuracy at 30 frames per second. A safety engineer argues for architecture A because \"accuracy is paramount in safety-critical applications.\" The flaw in this reasoning AND the correct framework for this engineering decision are:","options":["A) At 3 FPS, the system processes frames 10x slower than real-time driving requires; a 95.2% accurate model that cannot process frames fast enough to react to obstacles is more dangerous than a 92.8% model running at real-time speed, because the accuracy advantage is useless if the system responds too slowly","B) Both architectures are insufficient because safety-critical autonomous driving requires at least 99.9% accuracy and 60 FPS; the team should not deploy either model and should instead wait for hardware improvements that allow the deeper model to run at real-time speeds","C) The safety engineer is correct: in safety-critical applications, model accuracy must always be maximized first and latency optimized second; a missed detection at 95.2% accuracy is statistically less likely to cause an accident than at 92.8% accuracy regardless of processing speed","D) The comparison is invalid because accuracy and FPS are not comparable metrics; the correct approach is to compute the expected number of accidents per mile for each architecture using a standardized simulation benchmark that accounts for both accuracy and latency","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Autonomous driving requires real-time processing. At 30 mph, a car travels ~44 feet per second. At 3 FPS, each frame covers ~15 feet of travel. The model literally cannot see obstacles between frames. A 92.8% accurate model reacting in real-time is safer than a 95.2% model that is effectively blind 90% of the time. B dangerously ignores latency. C sets arbitrary thresholds. D suggests a valid evaluation framework but doesn't address the immediate latency problem."},
{"id":101,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"A team trains a deep neural network on 1,000,000 images. The model achieves 99.5% train accuracy and 98.2% test accuracy. A colleague says: \"The model has high variance because the training accuracy is so high — it must be memorizing.\" The flaw in this reasoning AND the correct diagnosis are:","options":["A) The colleague's reasoning would be correct for a small dataset but not for 1 million images; the memorization threshold scales linearly with dataset size, and a model needs at least 10 million parameters to memorize 1 million images which most networks do not exceed","B) The colleague is correct: 99.5% training accuracy on 1 million images is suspicious and indicates memorization; the model should be regularized until training accuracy drops below 95% to ensure the model is learning patterns rather than individual examples","C) High training accuracy alone does not indicate high variance; the 1.3% train-test gap is small, indicating LOW variance and good generalization; high variance would show a LARGE gap regardless of training accuracy level, and modern overparameterized deep networks can achieve near-perfect training accuracy without overfitting","D) The diagnosis depends on the irreducible noise level: if the Bayes-optimal accuracy is 98.5%, then the model has low bias (99.5% train ≈ 98.5% optimal) and moderate variance (98.2% test < 98.5% optimal); if optimal is 99.9%, the model has high bias","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The key diagnostic is the GAP between train and test (1.3%), not the absolute training accuracy. A 1.3% gap indicates the model generalizes well (low variance). Modern deep networks routinely achieve near-perfect training accuracy on large datasets without overfitting — this is a well-documented phenomenon. B wrongly equates high training accuracy with memorization. C introduces useful concepts but overcomplicates the straightforward diagnosis. D invents a memorization threshold."},
{"id":102,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"A data scientist plots learning curves (performance vs training set size) for a model. As training set size increases from 100 to 10,000: training error INCREASES from 2% to 8%, and test error DECREASES from 35% to 12%. At 10,000 samples, the curves have not yet converged (still a 4% gap). The correct interpretation of BOTH trends AND the most effective next action are:","options":["A) Both trends indicate the model is learning correctly; the 4% gap is the irreducible Bayes error that cannot be reduced by any means; the curves have effectively converged and adding more data or complexity would not improve performance beyond this fundamental limit","B) Training error increases because of label noise accumulating with more samples; test error decreases because the model averages out the noise over more examples; the 4% gap can only be closed by cleaning the training labels rather than adding more data","C) Training error increases because the model can no longer memorize all samples; test error decreases because more data provides better pattern estimation; the unconverged 4% gap indicates remaining variance, and adding more training data is the most effective next step since the curves are still converging","D) Training error increases because the model is underfitting as more data reveals the model's insufficient complexity; test error decreases coincidentally due to the larger test set having lower variance; the 4% gap indicates the model needs more features or a more complex architecture","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Classic high-variance learning curve: train error rises (harder to memorize more data), test error falls (better generalization from more data), gap narrows but hasn't closed. The unconverged gap means more data would help. B incorrectly attributes training error increase to underfitting. C wrongly claims the gap is irreducible error — at 10,000 samples the curves haven't converged yet. D invents a noise accumulation explanation."},
{"id":103,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"A team trains linear regression, degree-3 polynomial, and degree-20 polynomial on the same dataset. Cross-validation errors: linear=15.2, degree-3=8.1, degree-20=9.4. Surprisingly, the degree-20 model does NOT have the worst CV error despite being the most complex. A colleague expected degree-20 to overfit badly. The reason the degree-20 model's CV error is only slightly worse than degree-3 AND what this reveals about the bias-variance tradeoff in practice are:","options":["A) With cross-validation and sufficient data, very high-degree polynomials are implicitly constrained because CV penalizes models that overfit each fold; additionally, polynomial coefficients for unnecessary high-order terms may be small enough that the model approximates a simpler effective function","B) The degree-20 model actually has lower bias than degree-3 which fully compensates for its higher variance; the bias-variance tradeoff guarantees that reducing bias always improves CV error more than the corresponding variance increase hurts it","C) Cross-validation cannot detect overfitting in polynomial models because the polynomial basis functions are orthogonal, meaning each additional degree adds independent information; the degree-20 model is genuinely better but is penalized by the random fold assignments","D) The degree-20 model performs well because modern numerical optimization automatically regularizes polynomial regression by limiting coefficient magnitude through finite-precision floating-point arithmetic, preventing the extreme oscillations that overfitting would cause","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"This demonstrates that the bias-variance tradeoff is a TENDENCY, not an absolute law. With enough data and proper evaluation (CV), high-complexity models don't always catastrophically overfit — unnecessary terms may get small coefficients. But degree-3 still wins (8.1 vs 9.4), confirming the general principle. B states a false guarantee about bias always dominating. C is wrong about orthogonality preventing overfitting. D invents a floating-point regularization effect."},
{"id":104,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"A retail chain runs DBSCAN on customer purchase data with ε=2.0 and MinPts=5. The algorithm finds 4 clusters and labels 15% of customers as noise. The marketing VP demands that the noise points be assigned to their nearest cluster \"because every customer deserves to be in a segment.\" The data scientist resists. The technical reason the noise points should NOT be forced into clusters AND the business insight the noise points may contain are:","options":["A) Noise points are customers who fall exactly on the boundary between two clusters and DBSCAN cannot determine which cluster they belong to; assigning them to either cluster would be equally valid since they share characteristics with both neighboring segments","B) Noise points are in low-density regions between clusters; forcing them into the nearest cluster would distort cluster centroids and boundaries, potentially corrupting the segment definitions for all other customers; additionally, the noise points themselves may represent high-value outlier customers or emerging behavioral patterns worth analyzing separately","C) Noise points have missing or corrupted data that caused DBSCAN to exclude them; assigning them to clusters would contaminate the segments with bad data; the noise points should be cleaned and then re-run through DBSCAN with lower MinPts to recover them into clusters","D) DBSCAN's noise classification is mathematically irreversible once the algorithm has completed; the only way to include noise points is to re-run the algorithm with different parameters; the noise points contain no actionable business information because they represent random purchasing behavior","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"DBSCAN noise points are in low-density regions — they don't fit the dense-cluster pattern. Forcing them in distorts clusters for ALL other customers. But noise ≠ unimportant: they might be high-value unique customers, emerging segments, or fraud. B wrongly assumes data quality issues. C wrongly says noise contains no information. D confuses noise with boundary points (DBSCAN actually has a \"border point\" concept separate from noise)."},
{"id":105,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"A data scientist runs K-means (K=3) and Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM, K=3) on the same 2D customer dataset. K-means produces three circular clusters. GMM produces three elliptical clusters that partially overlap. A colleague says GMM is wrong because \"clusters shouldn't overlap — each customer should belong to exactly one segment.\" The fundamental difference between K-means and GMM that the colleague is missing AND when overlapping clusters are actually more realistic are:","options":["A) GMM is a generative model that models the data distribution while K-means is a discriminative model that only finds boundaries; the overlap indicates GMM has too many components and reducing K to 2 would eliminate the overlap while maintaining the same clustering quality","B) K-means assigns each point to exactly one cluster (hard assignment) based on nearest centroid distance; GMM assigns probabilistic membership (soft assignment) where a customer can be 70% segment A and 30% segment B; overlapping is realistic when customer behavior genuinely spans multiple segments like a business traveler who also takes leisure trips","C) K-means and GMM produce identical cluster assignments when clusters are well-separated; the overlap only occurs because this particular dataset has high noise levels; with cleaner data, both algorithms would produce the same non-overlapping circular clusters","D) K-means uses Euclidean distance which produces circular boundaries; GMM uses Mahalanobis distance which produces elliptical boundaries; the overlap in GMM is a visualization artifact caused by projecting higher-dimensional Gaussian distributions onto two dimensions","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"K-means = hard assignment (each point to exactly one cluster). GMM = soft/probabilistic assignment (each point has probability of belonging to each cluster). GMM also captures cluster shape (covariance matrix allows ellipses, not just circles). Overlap is realistic when categories genuinely blend — customer behavior is rarely clean. B misattributes overlap to projection. C wrongly suggests reducing K. D incorrectly claims identical results."},
{"id":106,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"In 2019, Apple Card (Goldman Sachs) offered women significantly lower credit limits than men with similar financial profiles. When confronted, Goldman Sachs stated that gender was not a model input. An investigation revealed the training data reflected decades of gender-based lending disparities. The bias type, the mechanism by which it persists despite gender not being an input, AND why simply removing gender from the data was insufficient are:","options":["A) Measurement bias in the credit scoring formula; the model used a credit score algorithm that systematically underestimates women's creditworthiness due to different financial behavior patterns; removing gender does not help because the credit score itself already encodes the gender-based measurement error","B) Selection bias in the applicant pool; Goldman Sachs marketed Apple Card primarily to male-dominated tech communities, creating a training dataset that overrepresents male financial patterns; removing gender does not help because the bias is in the data sampling process not the feature set","C) Algorithmic bias inherent to the gradient boosting algorithm Goldman Sachs used; certain tree-based algorithms disproportionately weight features that happen to correlate with protected attributes; removing gender does not help because the algorithm's splitting criterion inherently favors the majority demographic group","D) Historical bias encoded in the training data; the model learned correlations between financial patterns and outcomes that differ by gender due to historical discrimination; proxy features like spending categories, credit history length, and account types correlate with gender and perpetuate the disparity even without the explicit gender feature","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The Apple Card case is a textbook example of historical bias perpetuated through proxy features. Decades of lending discrimination created outcome data where women had lower limits/worse histories — not from lower creditworthiness but from historical bias. Features like credit history length, spending patterns, and account types correlate with gender without being gender. B overfocuses on credit scores. C speculates about marketing. D wrongly blames the algorithm family."},
{"id":107,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"Target's predictive analytics team built a model that identified pregnant customers based on purchasing patterns (unscented lotions, calcium supplements, large purses). A famous case claims Target sent pregnancy-related coupons to a teenager before her father knew she was pregnant. A data ethics committee reviews this system. The primary ethical concern AND the technical governance measure that could have prevented the privacy violation without eliminating the model's business value are:","options":["A) The model creates an inference-based privacy violation: it derives sensitive health information (pregnancy) from non-sensitive purchase data without explicit consent; the governance fix is differential privacy or k-anonymity applied to the targeting criteria, mixing pregnancy-related offers with random offers to prevent revealing the inference to the individual","B) The model violates GDPR's purpose limitation principle because purchase data collected for transaction processing is being repurposed for health status prediction; the governance fix is to obtain explicit consent for predictive analytics before collecting any purchase data","C) The model is an example of algorithmic discrimination because it treats pregnant customers differently from non-pregnant customers based on a protected characteristic; the governance fix is to apply the same marketing offers to all customers regardless of their predicted pregnancy status","D) The model creates a security vulnerability because the predicted pregnancy status is stored in a database that could be breached; the governance fix is to encrypt the pregnancy prediction scores and implement role-based access control for the marketing team's database","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The core issue is inference-based privacy: deriving sensitive personal information (pregnancy) from seemingly innocuous data (purchase patterns). Target actually addressed this by mixing targeted coupons with random ones, making the inference non-obvious. B is relevant but the case predates GDPR and the issue is broader than regulatory compliance. C mischaracterizes pregnancy as a protected class in employment discrimination terms. D focuses on data security rather than the fundamental privacy ethics."},
{"id":108,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Netflix runs an A/B test comparing two recommendation algorithms: Algorithm A (collaborative filtering) and Algorithm B (hybrid CF + content-based). After 4 weeks, Algorithm B shows 3% higher click-through rate but 2% lower viewing time per session. The product team debates which algorithm is better. The conflict between these metrics AND the deeper measurement problem this reveals about evaluating recommendation systems are:","options":["A) Click-through rate measures short-term engagement (curiosity) while viewing time measures long-term satisfaction (content quality); Algorithm B may recommend more clickbait-attractive titles that users abandon quickly; the team should use a composite metric like \"hours watched from recommendations\" that combines both discovery and satisfaction","B) Algorithm B is clearly superior because a 3% CTR improvement has greater revenue impact than a 2% viewing time decrease; Netflix's business model depends on users finding content to watch and CTR directly measures this, while viewing time beyond a minimum threshold has diminishing returns for retention","C) Algorithm A is clearly superior because viewing time is a more reliable metric than click-through rate; CTR is easily inflated by showing provocative thumbnails and sensational titles while viewing time reflects genuine content quality and user satisfaction with the recommendation","D) The metrics are contradictory because they were measured on different user populations; A/B tests randomly assign users but random assignment cannot guarantee identical demographics; the 3% CTR improvement and 2% viewing time decrease are likely caused by population differences rather than algorithm differences","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"This illustrates the challenge of choosing the right evaluation metric for recommender systems. Higher CTR with lower viewing time suggests Algorithm B is better at attracting clicks but worse at matching content to actual preferences. Neither metric alone tells the full story. A composite metric aligning with business goals (retention, satisfaction) is needed. B speculates about population differences. C and D each arbitrarily pick one metric as \"better\" without considering the tradeoff."},
{"id":109,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"A music streaming service uses collaborative filtering to generate personalized playlists. After deploying the model for 6 months, they notice that song diversity in recommendations has decreased by 40%, and 80% of plays come from only 5% of the catalog. Artists in the \"long tail\" (niche genres) report a 60% drop in streams. The cause of this phenomenon AND the algorithmic mechanism that creates it are:","options":["A) This is an expected feature of collaborative filtering that indicates the algorithm is correctly identifying the most universally appealing content; the 60% drop in niche streams reflects genuine user preference and the algorithm is properly serving the majority of users who prefer mainstream content","B) This is caused by the matrix factorization algorithm converging to a low-rank approximation that cannot represent niche preferences; the 5% of popular songs dominate the largest singular values while long-tail songs correspond to small singular values that are discarded during dimensionality reduction","C) This is a cold-start problem for new songs: CF cannot recommend songs with insufficient listening data; the long tail consists of recently added songs that have not yet accumulated enough plays; the solution is to wait for more data to accumulate and the diversity metrics will naturally recover over time","D) This is a popularity feedback loop: CF recommends songs that similar users played; popular songs get played more, generating more interaction data, which makes CF recommend them even more; niche songs receive fewer plays, less data, and therefore even fewer recommendations, creating a self-reinforcing cycle that concentrates plays on already-popular content","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"This is the classic feedback loop / popularity bias problem in recommender systems. CF is trained on interaction data → popular items get more interactions → CF recommends popular items more → they get even more interactions. It's self-reinforcing. Solutions: exploration/exploitation (multi-armed bandits), diversity constraints, boosting long-tail items. B misidentifies as cold-start (these aren't new songs). C describes a real technical effect but it's a consequence, not the root cause. D normalizes the harmful outcome."},
{"id":110,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"A data scientist runs PCA on a dataset with features \"annual_income\" (range: €20,000-€500,000) and \"age\" (range: 18-80). The first principal component explains 99.8% of variance and aligns almost perfectly with the income axis. She concludes: \"Income is by far the most important feature; age contributes almost nothing.\" The critical preprocessing error AND why this conclusion is completely wrong are:","options":["A) PCA should not be used with only two features because the eigendecomposition of a 2x2 covariance matrix is numerically unstable; the 99.8% figure is unreliable and the data scientist should use t-SNE instead which handles low-dimensional data more robustly","B) PCA was run correctly but the interpretation is wrong: PC1 explaining 99.8% of variance means the two features are nearly perfectly correlated, so keeping either income or age alone would preserve all information; the high explained variance indicates redundancy not importance","C) PCA was run on unscaled data; income's variance (~10¹¹) dominates age's variance (~10²) by a factor of 10⁹, so PC1 trivially aligns with income regardless of true importance; after standardizing both features to mean=0 and std=1, the PCA results would likely be completely different and age could emerge as equally or more important","D) The conclusion is actually correct: in financial datasets, income genuinely dominates all other features in predictive power; the PCA result confirms domain knowledge that income is the primary driver of most financial outcomes while age has negligible explanatory power","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"This is the PCA scaling trap. PCA maximizes variance, and variance depends on scale. Income (range 480,000) has enormously more raw variance than age (range 62). PC1 trivially captures income's scale, not its importance. After standardization, both features have variance=1, and PCA would find directions based on their true correlation structure. B misinterprets the result. C invents numerical instability. D accepts the flawed conclusion."},
{"id":111,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"A researcher uses t-SNE to visualize a 500-dimensional gene expression dataset in 2D. The plot shows 5 clearly separated clusters. She publishes a paper claiming she \"discovered 5 distinct cell types.\" A reviewer challenges this claim. The specific properties of t-SNE that make this claim potentially invalid AND what additional evidence would be needed to support the claim are:","options":["A) t-SNE is a supervised method that uses known cell type labels to create the visualization; the apparent clusters are a consequence of the label information injected during the dimensionality reduction process, not genuinely discovered structure in the unlabeled data","B) t-SNE can create apparent cluster separations from continuous data due to its perplexity parameter and crowding; inter-cluster distances are meaningless (two clusters that appear far apart may be close in the original space); validation requires confirming clusters with a different method (K-means on original data, domain-specific markers, or biological validation)","C) t-SNE projections are fully deterministic and reproducible; if 5 clusters appear, they must correspond to genuine structure in the high-dimensional data because t-SNE's mathematical objective preserves both local and global distances faithfully in the low-dimensional embedding","D) t-SNE always produces exactly as many clusters as the perplexity parameter divided by 10; with the default perplexity of 50, t-SNE will always show approximately 5 clusters regardless of the true data structure, making the number of clusters an artifact of the hyperparameter","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"t-SNE distorts global distances, can split continuous data into apparent clusters, and results change with different perplexity values and random seeds. The 5 clusters MIGHT be real but could also be artifacts. Validation is essential: run with different perplexity values, confirm with clustering on original high-D data, use biological markers. B is wrong: t-SNE is unsupervised. C invents a perplexity/cluster formula. D is doubly wrong: t-SNE is non-deterministic and does NOT preserve global distances."},
{"id":112,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"A bank's random forest model denies a loan application. The applicant, a 28-year-old with €85,000 income, exercises their GDPR right to explanation. The bank generates SHAP values: income=+0.15, age=-0.25, debt_to_income=-0.35, employment_years=-0.10. The applicant's lawyer argues: \"The negative age contribution proves age discrimination.\" The bank's data scientist must explain why this interpretation is incorrect AND what the SHAP values actually mean in context:","options":["A) The lawyer is correct: any negative SHAP contribution from a protected attribute like age constitutes indirect discrimination under GDPR regardless of the statistical correlation; the bank must retrain the model without age as an input to comply with anti-discrimination requirements","B) The negative age SHAP value is irrelevant because SHAP values are computed relative to a random baseline, not relative to the applicant's actual peers; the bank should instead compare this applicant's features to the average approved applicant to determine whether age was the decisive factor","C) SHAP values are approximate explanations that should not be used for legal arguments; the correct approach for GDPR compliance is to provide the applicant with the decision tree path that led to their denial, which gives an exact rule-based explanation rather than an approximate feature attribution","D) A negative SHAP value for age means age pushed this applicant's approval probability BELOW the average applicant, but this is not discrimination — younger applicants statistically have shorter credit histories and less stable employment; SHAP values measure correlation-based contribution to the prediction, not causal discrimination against a protected class","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"SHAP shows how each feature CONTRIBUTES to this individual's prediction relative to the average. Age=-0.25 means being 28 pushed the prediction 0.25 below the average (average applicant might be 42). This reflects the statistical relationship between age and creditworthiness (younger = less credit history), not unlawful age discrimination. B incorrectly equates negative SHAP with discrimination. C dismisses SHAP inappropriately. D mischaracterizes SHAP's baseline."},
{"id":113,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"A healthcare company uses a deep neural network to predict sepsis risk from ICU sensor data. Regulators require model explainability. The team generates LIME explanations for each prediction. During validation, they discover that running LIME twice on the SAME patient with the SAME model sometimes produces DIFFERENT feature importance rankings. The clinical team loses confidence in the model. The technical reason for LIME's inconsistency AND whether this actually invalidates the model or the explanation method are:","options":["A) LIME uses random perturbations around each instance to build a local surrogate model; different random seeds produce different perturbation samples, leading to different local models and explanations; this inconsistency is in the EXPLANATION method, not the underlying model — the model's prediction remains identical and stable for the same patient","B) LIME is designed only for classification models with categorical features; applying it to continuous ICU sensor data violates its mathematical assumptions, causing the inconsistent explanations; the team should switch to SHAP which is mathematically valid for continuous features","C) LIME's inconsistency reveals that the underlying neural network itself is making arbitrary decisions based on random noise in the input data; if the explanations change, the model's reasoning must also be changing, which proves the model is unreliable for clinical deployment","D) LIME's inconsistency is caused by the neural network's decision boundary being highly non-linear near this patient's feature values; the inconsistency indicates the patient is a borderline case and the model's prediction confidence is too low for clinical use regardless of the explanation","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"LIME creates random perturbations around the data point and fits a local linear model. Different random perturbations → slightly different local models → different feature attributions. But the UNDERLYING model always produces the same prediction for the same input. The instability is in the EXPLANATION method, not the model. Running LIME multiple times and averaging can stabilize explanations. B wrongly attributes LIME's randomness to model instability. C makes false claims about LIME's limitations. D misinterprets explanation instability as prediction uncertainty."},
{"id":114,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A logistics company wants to optimize delivery routes. The system should learn from feedback: faster routes earn positive signals, slower routes earn negative signals, and the system must balance exploring new routes versus exploiting known fast routes. A data scientist proposes supervised learning with historical route data. The paradigm mismatch AND the correct ML paradigm for this problem are:","options":["A) Supervised learning is appropriate because the historical route data provides labeled examples of fast and slow routes; the exploration-exploitation tradeoff can be handled by periodically adding randomly selected routes to the training data to prevent the model from overfitting to known routes","B) The data scientist is correct that supervised learning is the right approach; the sequential nature of the problem can be handled by treating each route segment as an independent classification task where the model predicts whether each turn leads to faster or slower delivery times","C) Supervised learning requires fixed input-output pairs, but this problem involves sequential decision-making with delayed rewards and exploration-exploitation tradeoffs; reinforcement learning is the correct paradigm because the agent must learn a policy through trial-and-error interaction with the environment","D) The problem requires unsupervised learning because the company wants to discover optimal routes that may not exist in historical data; clustering similar delivery addresses and then finding centroid paths would produce routes that minimize average delivery distance without needing labeled data","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"This is a reinforcement learning problem: an agent (route optimizer) takes actions (route choices) in an environment (road network), receives rewards (delivery times), and must learn a policy that maximizes cumulative reward. Key RL characteristics: sequential decisions, delayed rewards, exploration vs exploitation. Supervised learning can't handle the exploration-exploitation tradeoff or learn from environmental interaction. B shoehorns exploration into supervised learning. C misapplies unsupervised learning. D ignores the sequential dependency between decisions."},
{"id":115,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"MedScan AI deploys a sepsis detection model in a Lisbon ICU (12% sepsis prevalence, 50,000 training records). The model achieves ROC-AUC of 0.94 on the test set. The clinical team sets the classification threshold at 0.5, resulting in: precision=0.68, recall=0.52. The head nurse complains that the model misses nearly half of all sepsis cases. The relationship between the high AUC and the low recall at threshold 0.5, AND the correct action to improve recall without retraining the model are:","options":["A) The high AUC proves the model is well-calibrated, meaning the 0.5 threshold is already optimal for this prevalence rate; the low recall is caused by class imbalance in training data and can only be fixed by oversampling sepsis cases and retraining the model from scratch","B) AUC and recall measure completely different model properties on different scales; the low recall indicates a fundamentally flawed model that no threshold adjustment can fix, because a model with AUC 0.94 should always achieve recall above 0.90 at any reasonable threshold","C) The 0.5 threshold is only optimal when classes are perfectly balanced at 50/50; with 12% sepsis prevalence the threshold should be raised to 0.88 to match the negative class proportion, which automatically maximizes both precision and recall simultaneously","D) AUC measures ranking quality across ALL thresholds, but any single threshold trades off precision and recall; lowering the threshold below 0.5 will catch more sepsis cases (increase recall) at the cost of more false alarms (decrease precision), which is acceptable when missing sepsis is life-threatening","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"AUC=0.94 means excellent ranking ability across all thresholds, but at any SPECIFIC threshold there is a precision-recall tradeoff. Threshold 0.5 with 12% prevalence is too high for a safety-critical application. Lowering it (e.g., to 0.2) catches more sepsis (higher recall) with more false alarms (lower precision) — acceptable when missed sepsis kills. B wrongly claims 0.5 is optimal. C wrongly says threshold adjustment can't help. D inverts the threshold direction."},
{"id":116,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"MedScan AI's sepsis model (gradient-boosted trees) flags Patient #4721 as high sepsis risk (P=0.87). The attending physician demands an explanation before initiating aggressive antibiotic treatment, as unnecessary antibiotics carry serious side effects. The data science team generates both SHAP values (heart_rate=+0.22, temperature=+0.18, WBC_count=+0.31, blood_pressure=-0.12) and a global feature importance ranking (WBC_count #1, age #2, heart_rate #3). The physician asks which explanation to trust for THIS patient's treatment decision AND why they differ:","options":["A) The SHAP values should guide this decision because they explain THIS specific patient's prediction; global importance shows what matters ON AVERAGE across all patients, but this patient's risk is driven by WBC and heart rate specifically, while age (globally #2) may be irrelevant for this individual case","B) The SHAP values and global importance should always agree for correctly trained models; the disagreement indicates a bug in the SHAP computation or that the model has overfit to Patient #4721's features, and the model should be retrained before making any clinical decision","C) Both explanations are unreliable for medical decisions because gradient-boosted trees are black-box models that cannot provide causal explanations; the physician should ignore both and rely solely on clinical judgment rather than any model-generated feature attribution","D) The global feature importance should guide this decision because it represents validated patterns across 50,000 patients, which is more statistically robust than SHAP values computed from perturbations around a single data point that may contain estimation noise","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"SHAP provides LOCAL explanations (this patient specifically), while global importance shows AVERAGE patterns. They can legitimately differ because a patient's risk drivers may differ from the population average. For THIS treatment decision, SHAP is correct — the physician needs to know why THIS patient was flagged. B wrongly prioritizes population averages for individual decisions. C dismisses valid explanations. D wrongly assumes they must agree."},
{"id":117,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"MedScan AI considers two threshold strategies for the sepsis model: Strategy A sets the threshold at 0.3 (catching 89% of sepsis cases but generating 25% false alarm rate). Strategy B sets it at 0.7 (catching only 45% of sepsis cases but with only 5% false alarms). The hospital's cost analysis shows: missing a sepsis case costs €150,000 (extended ICU stay, potential death), while a false alarm costs €2,000 (unnecessary antibiotics and tests). Given these asymmetric costs, the correct threshold strategy AND the mathematical reasoning are:","options":["A) Strategy A (threshold 0.3): with a 75:1 cost ratio (€150K/€2K), even many false alarms are acceptable because the expected cost of one missed sepsis case far exceeds the expected cost of dozens of false alarms; at 12% prevalence, Strategy A saves significantly more expected cost per patient","B) Strategy B (threshold 0.7): despite the high cost of missing sepsis, the 25% false alarm rate in Strategy A means one in four healthy patients receives unnecessary antibiotics, which creates antibiotic resistance — a population-level harm that exceeds the individual cost of missed sepsis cases","C) Strategy A (threshold 0.3): but only because the hospital has infinite budget for false alarm investigations; in resource-constrained settings, Strategy B's lower false alarm rate is always preferred regardless of the cost ratio because false alarms waste limited clinical staff time","D) Neither strategy is appropriate: the threshold should be set where precision equals recall (the break-even point), which optimizes the F1 score and automatically accounts for asymmetric costs without requiring explicit cost estimates that may be inaccurate or politically motivated","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"With a 75:1 cost ratio, the math is clear: missing 1 sepsis case (€150K) costs as much as 75 false alarms (€2K each). Strategy A catches 89% vs 45% of sepsis — the cost savings from catching 44% more sepsis cases vastly outweigh the extra false alarm costs. B raises a valid concern (antibiotic resistance) but the immediate cost analysis still favors A. C wrongly assumes F1 is optimal for asymmetric costs. D incorrectly conditions on infinite budget."},
{"id":118,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"MedScan AI audits their sepsis model for fairness across demographic groups. They discover: AUC=0.95 for patients aged 40-70 but AUC=0.78 for patients over 80. The training data contains 40,000 patients aged 40-70 but only 3,000 patients over 80. Elderly patients also have different vital sign baselines (lower normal heart rate, different temperature regulation). The bias type, its root cause, AND the correct technical mitigation that addresses BOTH the data AND the physiological differences are:","options":["A) Algorithmic bias: gradient-boosted trees inherently perform worse on minority subgroups regardless of sample size because the sequential boosting process prioritizes reducing error on the majority group first; the fix is switching to a random forest which treats all subgroups equally","B) Representation bias compounded by measurement bias: the model underperforms on elderly patients due to both underrepresentation in training data AND different physiological baselines; the fix requires both collecting more elderly patient data AND training separate calibration layers or sub-models with age-appropriate vital sign reference ranges","C) Pure representation bias: the model simply needs more elderly training data; once the dataset is rebalanced to include equal numbers of elderly patients, the physiological differences will be automatically captured by the gradient-boosted trees without any architectural changes","D) Historical bias: elderly sepsis cases were historically underdiagnosed in the training hospital, meaning the training labels themselves are less accurate for elderly patients; no amount of additional data or model changes can fix biased labels without re-adjudicating historical diagnoses","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This is a TWO-PART bias: (1) representation bias (3K vs 40K samples) means the model hasn't seen enough elderly patterns, AND (2) measurement bias because the SAME vital sign readings mean different things in elderly patients (a 'normal' heart rate of 60 in a 45-year-old may indicate bradycardia in an 85-year-old). Fixing ONLY the data quantity (B) misses the physiological recalibration need. C wrongly blames the algorithm family. D speculates about label quality without evidence."},
{"id":119,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"RouteFast trains a neural network to predict delivery times on 500,000 records. Features include distance (km, range 1-200), traffic_index (0-10), and package_weight (grams, range 50-50,000). Using batch gradient descent with learning rate 0.01, the loss decreases smoothly for distance and traffic but oscillates wildly for package_weight. The geometric explanation for why ONE feature causes oscillation while others converge smoothly, AND the fix that does NOT require changing the learning rate are:","options":["A) Package_weight has many outlier values (very heavy packages) that cause the gradient to spike during certain mini-batches; the fix is to clip package_weight values at the 99th percentile to remove outliers that destabilize the gradient computation","B) The oscillation occurs because package_weight has a nonlinear relationship with delivery time while distance and traffic are linear; the fix is to apply a log transformation to package_weight which linearizes the relationship and eliminates the oscillation pattern","C) The oscillation is caused by package_weight being a categorical variable encoded as a continuous number; the fix is to bin package_weight into categories (light/medium/heavy) and use one-hot encoding instead of the raw numerical value","D) The unscaled features create elongated elliptical contours in the loss landscape: package_weight's large range (50-50,000) produces a steep, narrow valley along that dimension while distance and traffic have gentler slopes; feature standardization (z-score) makes all contours circular, allowing a single learning rate to work for all features","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"When features have vastly different scales, the loss surface becomes an elongated ellipse. The gradient in the package_weight direction is much larger (steep valley) than in other directions, causing overshooting/oscillation ONLY along that axis. Standardization (mean=0, std=1) makes all dimensions comparable, creating circular contours where a single learning rate works everywhere. B assumes nonlinearity which isn't the issue. C assumes outliers. D incorrectly treats weight as categorical."},
{"id":120,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"RouteFast's data scientist builds a multiple regression model: delivery_time = θ0 + θ1·distance + θ2·traffic + θ3·weight + θ4·driver_experience. She notices that θ4 (driver_experience) is negative (-2.3), which makes intuitive sense (experienced drivers are faster). But when she adds a new feature θ5·routes_completed (total past deliveries), the coefficient θ4 flips to positive (+1.1). She suspects a bug. The statistical explanation for why adding a correlated feature can REVERSE another feature's coefficient AND what this implies about interpreting coefficients in multiple regression are:","options":["A) The positive coefficient for experience means the model has overfit to a spurious pattern in the training data where slow experienced drivers happened to have longer delivery times; regularization would shrink this coefficient back toward its true negative value","B) The flip is caused by numerical instability in the matrix inversion step of the Normal Equation when two features are highly correlated; the fix is to switch from the Normal Equation to gradient descent which is more numerically stable for correlated features","C) This is Simpson's paradox via multicollinearity: driver_experience and routes_completed are highly correlated; when both are included, each coefficient represents the effect HOLDING THE OTHER CONSTANT, which changes the interpretation — experience with fixed route count might mean slower adaptation, not a bug","D) The coefficient flip proves routes_completed is a confounding variable that should be removed because it corrupts the true effect of experience; the original model with only driver_experience had the correct coefficient sign that matches domain knowledge","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"When correlated features are both included, coefficients represent PARTIAL effects (holding the other constant). Experience and routes_completed are correlated — separately, experience predicts speed. But holding routes_completed constant, more 'experience years' at the SAME route count might indicate a slower learning curve. This is a fundamental property of multiple regression, not a bug. B incorrectly removes legitimate information. C misattributes to numerical issues. D incorrectly labels as overfitting."},
{"id":121,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"RouteFast tests three models for delivery time prediction: (1) Linear Regression: MAE=12.5 min, interpretable coefficients show distance is 3x more important than traffic. (2) Random Forest: MAE=8.2 min, feature importance shows traffic and time_of_day are equally important. (3) XGBoost: MAE=7.8 min, feature importance shows weather_score as #1. The operations manager is confused that all three models rank feature importance differently. The technical explanation for why feature importance varies across model families AND what the manager should conclude about \"true\" feature importance are:","options":["A) Each model captures different types of relationships (linear vs nonlinear vs interaction effects), so \"importance\" means different things: linear regression measures linear marginal effect, RF measures variance reduction from random splits, XGBoost measures gain from sequential residual correction — there is no single \"true\" importance independent of the model","B) The importance differences prove that at least two of the three models are wrong because true feature importance is an objective property of the data that should be consistent across all properly trained models regardless of the algorithm family used","C) The inconsistency is caused by each model using a different random seed during training; if all three models were trained with the same random seed, they would produce consistent feature importance rankings because the underlying data relationships are deterministic","D) Random Forest and XGBoost importance rankings are more reliable than linear regression because tree-based models can capture nonlinear relationships; the linear regression importance should be discarded since it cannot detect the interaction between traffic and time_of_day","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Feature importance is MODEL-DEPENDENT, not an intrinsic property of the data. Each model family defines \"importance\" differently based on its own mechanics. LR: coefficient magnitude (linear effects only). RF: mean decrease in impurity (nonlinear splits). XGBoost: gain from sequential corrections (interaction effects). All are \"correct\" within their framework. B wrongly assumes objective importance exists. C dismisses LR too broadly. D misattributes to random seeds."},
{"id":122,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"ShopSense's collaborative filtering recommendation engine has been running for 18 months. Analytics reveals: (1) 85% of revenue comes from 8% of the product catalog, (2) new product visibility has dropped 60% since launch, (3) customer satisfaction surveys show declining \"discovery\" scores. The product team debates whether to switch to content-based filtering to solve the diversity problem. The root cause of the revenue concentration AND why switching to content-based filtering would NOT solve it are:","options":["A) The feedback loop is caused by a bug in the CF implementation that gives disproportionate weight to recent purchases; fixing the recency weighting in the existing CF system would solve the diversity problem without needing to switch to content-based filtering at all","B) The root cause is a popularity feedback loop inherent to CF: popular items get more interactions → CF recommends them more → they get even more interactions; content-based filtering would create a DIFFERENT but equally narrow problem — an overspecialization bubble where each customer only sees items similar to their past purchases, still ignoring new/diverse products","C) The concentration is caused by a cold-start problem for new products: CF cannot recommend items without interaction data; content-based filtering would fully solve this because it recommends based on product attributes like color, style, and brand, not interaction history","D) The revenue concentration reflects genuine customer preferences, not an algorithmic bias; 85% of revenue from 8% of products is normal Pareto distribution behavior, and no algorithm change can alter fundamental consumer demand patterns for fashion products","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"CF creates a self-reinforcing popularity loop (more interactions → more recommendations → more interactions). But content-based doesn't solve the core problem — it creates OVERSPECIALIZATION (only recommending items similar to past purchases, trapping each user in a style bubble). The real fix is exploration mechanisms (multi-armed bandits, diversity constraints). B oversimplifies cold-start as the only issue. C normalizes the harm. D assumes a simple bug."},
{"id":123,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"ShopSense builds a customer churn prediction model using logistic regression with 200 features (purchase frequency, recency, product categories, browsing behavior, return rate, etc.). Without regularization: train AUC=0.97, test AUC=0.79. The team applies Ridge (λ=10): train AUC=0.88, test AUC=0.86. Then Lasso (λ=10): train AUC=0.85, test AUC=0.87, with only 23 features having non-zero coefficients. A senior data scientist recommends Lasso despite Ridge having slightly better training AUC. The TWO reasons Lasso is preferred for this specific business problem AND why Ridge's higher training AUC is actually a disadvantage are:","options":["A) Lasso is preferred because it ran faster during training since it only needs to optimize 23 parameters instead of 200; Ridge's higher training AUC reflects wasted computation on irrelevant features rather than genuinely better pattern recognition","B) Lasso is preferred because L1 regularization always produces better test AUC than L2 regularization when the true number of relevant features is less than half the total features; the 0.87 vs 0.86 test AUC difference proves Lasso found the true sparse signal","C) Lasso's feature selection (23 of 200 features) provides both interpretability (the business team can understand which 23 behaviors predict churn) AND operational efficiency (the production system only needs to track 23 metrics); Ridge's higher training AUC with all 200 features means it retains more noise and is harder to operationalize","D) Lasso is preferred solely for its regularization strength: λ=10 in Lasso produces stronger shrinkage than λ=10 in Ridge because the L1 norm is always larger than the L2 norm for the same coefficient vector, making Lasso a more aggressive regularizer","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Two business advantages of Lasso here: (1) INTERPRETABILITY: the churn team can see exactly which 23 behaviors drive churn and design interventions. (2) OPERATIONALIZATION: production only needs 23 features, reducing data pipeline complexity. Ridge keeping all 200 features means the model depends on features that may be noisy or expensive to maintain. B overgeneralizes about L1 vs L2 performance. C confuses training parameter count with optimization complexity. D makes incorrect norm comparison claims."},
{"id":124,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"ShopSense's churn model (from Q123) is deployed with the Lasso model. After 3 months, the team notices the model's test AUC has dropped from 0.87 to 0.76. They investigate and find: (1) a new competitor launched, changing customer behavior patterns, (2) the feature \"avg_browsing_time\" now has a bimodal distribution (mobile app users browse differently than web users since a new app launched), (3) the model was never retrained since deployment. The COMBINED explanation for the performance drop AND the monitoring practice that would have detected it earlier are:","options":["A) The performance drop is entirely caused by the app launch changing the browsing_time distribution; concept drift from the competitor is irrelevant because the model does not use competitor-related features and therefore cannot be affected by competitor actions","B) The model degraded because Lasso's selected 23 features became stale after 3 months; the fix is to retrain the Lasso model monthly with an expanding training window, which automatically adapts the feature selection to changing customer behavior without monitoring","C) The performance drop indicates the original test AUC of 0.87 was optimistically biased due to data leakage in the initial evaluation; the current 0.76 reflects the model's true performance that was always present but masked by evaluation methodology errors","D) This is concept drift (customer behavior changed due to the competitor) compounded by data drift (the browsing_time distribution shifted due to the app launch); monitoring feature distributions with statistical tests (KS test, PSI) and tracking prediction performance on rolling windows would have flagged both shifts weeks earlier","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"TWO simultaneous drifts: (1) Concept drift: the competitor changed WHY customers churn (new behavioral patterns). (2) Data drift: the app changed the DISTRIBUTION of an input feature (browsing_time). Monitoring would catch this through feature distribution tracking (PSI/KS tests) and rolling AUC metrics. B dismisses concept drift incorrectly. C suggests periodic retraining but doesn't address the monitoring gap. D wrongly attributes to initial evaluation errors."},
{"id":125,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"ShopSense experiments with model complexity for churn prediction. Results across models: (1) Logistic Regression: train=82%, test=81%. (2) Random Forest (100 trees, max_depth=5): train=88%, test=87%. (3) Random Forest (500 trees, max_depth=None): train=99.5%, test=86%. (4) Neural Network (3 layers, 256 neurons each): train=99.8%, test=84%. A junior data scientist argues that Model 3 is the best because it has the highest test accuracy (well, tied with Model 2). The subtle reason Model 2 is actually SAFER for production deployment despite identical or lower test accuracy AND what Model 3's 99.5% training accuracy reveals:","options":["A) Model 3's 99.5% training accuracy indicates it is on the edge of overfitting (13.5% train-test gap vs Model 2's 1% gap); Model 2 is safer because its small bias-variance gap means it will remain stable as data distribution shifts, while Model 3's fragile overfit could degrade sharply with minor data changes","B) Model 2 is safer because random forests with 100 trees are less computationally expensive than 500 trees in production; the 87% vs 86% test accuracy difference is negligible but the 5x inference cost reduction significantly impacts the real-time churn scoring pipeline","C) Model 3 is actually the best choice because the 0.5% lower test accuracy compared to Model 2 is within the statistical margin of error for this dataset size; the higher training accuracy proves Model 3 has learned the true underlying patterns more comprehensively","D) Model 2 is safer purely because max_depth=5 makes it interpretable to business stakeholders and auditors, while Model 3's unlimited depth produces an uninterpretable black box; the accuracy difference is irrelevant when regulatory requirements mandate model interpretability","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The TRAIN-TEST GAP is the key signal. Model 2: 88-87=1% gap (low variance, stable). Model 3: 99.5-86=13.5% gap (high variance, fragile). Even though test accuracy is similar NOW, Model 3's narrow gap between current test data and overfitting means small distribution shifts could cause sharp degradation. Model 2's tight gap is robust. B raises valid points but misidentifies the primary concern. C ignores the variance signal. D focuses on compute costs, not generalization stability."},
{"id":126,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"A researcher notices something counterintuitive: when she INCREASES the learning rate from 0.001 to 0.01 for training a deep neural network, the test accuracy actually IMPROVES from 91% to 93%, even though both converge fully on training data (>99%). A colleague says this is impossible because larger learning rates should cause instability. The technical explanation for why a larger learning rate can IMPROVE generalization AND what this reveals about the loss landscape:","options":["A) Larger learning rates create more gradient noise that prevents the optimizer from settling into sharp, narrow minima; the optimizer is instead pushed toward flat, wide minima that are robust to small perturbations in input data, leading to better generalization on unseen test data","B) The larger learning rate causes the model to skip over individual training samples during gradient updates, which acts as implicit dropout by randomly ignoring samples during each epoch, preventing the co-adaptation of neurons that causes overfitting","C) The larger learning rate acts as implicit data augmentation by effectively training on slightly different versions of each sample due to the larger gradient steps, which increases the effective training set size and improves generalization through greater data diversity","D) The improvement is coincidental: the larger learning rate happened to find a better random initialization path that led to a lower global minimum; repeating the experiment with different random seeds would eliminate this apparent improvement in test accuracy","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Research (Li et al., Jastrzębski et al.) shows that larger learning rates bias SGD toward flat minima in the loss landscape. Flat minima are characterized by low curvature — small perturbations to weights don't significantly change the loss. This directly translates to better generalization because test data is a perturbation of training data. Sharp minima (found with small LR) are sensitive to perturbations. B invents a data augmentation effect. C dismisses as coincidence. D incorrectly describes a dropout mechanism."},
{"id":127,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"A team applies BOTH early stopping (at epoch 50) AND L2 regularization (λ=0.1) to their neural network. A colleague argues this is redundant because \"both techniques do the same thing — prevent overfitting.\" The correct analysis of whether they are truly redundant AND the subtle difference in HOW each constrains the model are:","options":["A) They are NOT redundant but should never be used together: L2 regularization alters the loss surface that gradient descent navigates, and early stopping assumes the original loss surface; combining them creates a moving target problem where the optimal stopping point shifts with each epoch","B) They are NOT redundant: L2 explicitly penalizes large weights throughout training by adding λΣw² to the loss, while early stopping implicitly constrains the EFFECTIVE number of optimization steps, limiting how far weights can travel from initialization; they constrain different aspects of model complexity and can complement each other","C) They ARE redundant: both mathematically reduce to the same constraint on the L2 norm of the weight vector; the optimal early stopping point corresponds to a specific λ value, and using both simultaneously is equivalent to applying a stronger-than-intended regularization that causes underfitting","D) They ARE redundant only for linear models; for neural networks, early stopping regularizes each layer independently based on convergence speed while L2 applies uniform regularization, making them complementary; the colleague is correct for simple models but wrong for deep networks","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"While both prevent overfitting, they constrain DIFFERENT things. L2 penalizes weight MAGNITUDE (keeps weights small throughout training). Early stopping constrains the number of STEPS (limits how far weights move from initialization, equivalent to constraining the volume of parameter space explored). They can be complementary: L2 shapes the loss landscape while early stopping limits exploration of that landscape. B overstates the equivalence. C invents a moving target problem. D makes a false linear/nonlinear distinction."},
{"id":128,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"An insurance company trains a logistic regression model for claim approval. The model's output P(approve)=0.5 corresponds to the decision boundary where θᵀx=0. A data scientist visualizes the decision boundary in 2D (income vs claim_amount) and sees a straight line. She asks: \"If I want a curved decision boundary to capture a nonlinear relationship between income and claim amount, should I switch to a neural network?\" Her colleague suggests a simpler alternative. The correct simpler alternative AND why it works within the logistic regression framework:","options":["A) Use kernel logistic regression which applies the kernel trick (similar to SVM) to implicitly map features into a higher-dimensional space; this is the standard approach for nonlinear logistic regression and requires no manual feature engineering unlike polynomial features","B) Increase the regularization strength λ which will curve the decision boundary by shrinking different coefficients at different rates; strong L2 regularization naturally creates curved boundaries because it bends the hyperplane toward the origin of the parameter space","C) Lower the classification threshold from 0.5 to 0.3 which effectively shifts and curves the decision boundary because the sigmoid function's curvature means different threshold values trace different-shaped contours through the feature space","D) Add polynomial or interaction features (e.g., income², income×claim_amount) to the input; logistic regression's decision boundary is linear in the FEATURE SPACE, so transforming features creates curved boundaries in the original input space without switching to a fundamentally different algorithm","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Logistic regression's boundary is θᵀx=0, which is LINEAR in feature space. Adding features like income² or income×claim_amount creates a new, higher-dimensional feature space where the boundary is STILL a hyperplane, but when projected back to the original 2D (income vs claim_amount), it appears curved. This is the same principle as polynomial regression. B incorrectly claims regularization curves the boundary. C exists but isn't a standard scikit-learn feature and is more complex. D is wrong — changing threshold shifts the boundary but doesn't curve it."},
{"id":129,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A Kaggle competitor notices that in a random forest with 1000 trees, each bootstrap sample contains approximately 63.2% unique observations from the original dataset. She asks her professor: \"Why exactly 63.2% and not some other number?\" The professor's answer should connect this to a fundamental mathematical concept. The derivation AND its practical implication for the out-of-bag (OOB) error estimate are:","options":["A) The 63.2% comes from the birthday paradox: with n samples and n draws with replacement, the probability of at least one collision (drawing the same sample twice) follows the same exponential decay formula as the birthday problem, converging to 1-1/e","B) The probability that a specific sample is NOT selected in any single draw is (1-1/n); over n draws with replacement, the probability of never being selected is (1-1/n)ⁿ which converges to 1/e ≈ 0.368 as n grows; so ~36.8% are out-of-bag and ~63.2% are included, giving each tree a built-in ~37% validation set for free","C) The 63.2% is a design choice in scikit-learn's random forest implementation that balances training set size with out-of-bag set size; other implementations use different fractions (some use 50%, others 80%), and the exact percentage is a tunable hyperparameter","D) The 63.2% comes from the central limit theorem: with replacement sampling, the expected number of unique observations follows a normal distribution with mean 0.632n; this is a statistical coincidence specific to bootstrap sampling and has no deeper mathematical derivation","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"For n samples with replacement: P(sample i NOT drawn in one draw) = (1-1/n). Over n draws: P(never drawn) = (1-1/n)ⁿ → 1/e ≈ 0.368 as n→∞. So ~36.8% are OOB (never selected), giving each tree a free validation set. This is why OOB error is a valid generalization estimate without needing a separate test set. B invokes CLT incorrectly. C wrongly claims it's an implementation choice. D confuses with the birthday paradox (related but different)."},
{"id":130,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"A pharmaceutical company has gene expression data: 20,000 features (genes) and only 200 patient samples. They apply PCA, keeping 50 components that explain 92% of variance, then train a classifier that achieves 94% accuracy. A statistician warns that this workflow has a subtle but critical flaw that inflates the reported accuracy. The specific flaw AND why it constitutes data leakage even though PCA is unsupervised are:","options":["A) PCA was fit on ALL 200 samples including the test set; even though PCA is unsupervised (doesn't use labels), it learns the variance structure of the ENTIRE dataset including test samples, meaning the dimensionality reduction step leaks test set information into the transformation applied to training data","B) The flaw is that PCA cannot be applied to gene expression data because the features are counts (discrete values) not continuous measurements; PCA assumes continuous Gaussian-distributed features and produces meaningless components when this assumption is violated","C) PCA with 50 components on 200 samples creates an underdetermined system where the number of effective features exceeds the rank of the data matrix; the 94% accuracy is an artifact of the dimensionality reduction coincidentally aligning with the label structure","D) The flaw is that 92% variance retention is too aggressive for 20,000 features; the discarded 8% likely contains the most discriminative features for the classification task, and the 94% accuracy would be higher if more components were retained","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Even though PCA is unsupervised (doesn't see labels), fitting PCA on the full dataset means the principal components are influenced by test set variance patterns. The training data is then projected onto axes that 'know about' test set structure. Correct workflow: fit PCA ONLY on training data, then transform test data using the SAME PCA model. B describes a legitimate concern but not the primary flaw. C gets the PCA-classification relationship backward. D is wrong — PCA works on count data."},
{"id":131,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A team trains a ReLU-activated neural network and monitors neuron activations during training. After 100 epochs, they discover that 15% of neurons in the hidden layers output exactly 0.0 for EVERY input in the training set. These neurons have effectively \"died\" and no longer contribute to the model. The mathematical reason WHY these neurons died AND why this problem is specific to ReLU but not sigmoid activation are:","options":["A) The neurons died because the learning rate was too high, causing weight updates that overshoot the optimal values and flip the pre-activation sign; this is a convergence problem specific to ReLU that would also occur with any activation function given a sufficiently large learning rate","B) If a neuron's weights shift such that the pre-activation (weighted sum) becomes negative for ALL inputs, ReLU outputs 0 and its gradient is exactly 0, so the weights never update again — the neuron is permanently dead; sigmoid never has exactly-zero gradient (its minimum gradient is >0 for any finite input), so sigmoid neurons can always recover","C) The neurons died because ReLU has no upper bound on its output, causing some neurons to grow extremely large activations that dominate the network; the remaining neurons's contributions become relatively insignificant and round to 0.0 due to floating-point precision limits","D) The neurons died because batch normalization was not applied before the ReLU activation; without normalization, the pre-activation distribution drifts toward negative values over training, and ReLU clips all negative pre-activations to zero permanently","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"ReLU: f(x)=max(0,x). If the weighted input to a neuron becomes negative for ALL training samples, output=0, gradient=0, so weights receive ZERO gradient — they can never recover. Sigmoid: f(x)=1/(1+e^(-x)) always has non-zero gradient (small but positive even at saturation), so even saturated sigmoid neurons can slowly recover. LeakyReLU fixes dying ReLU by having a small positive slope for negative inputs. B describes a different issue. C oversimplifies. D presents a partial truth but BN doesn't fully prevent dying ReLU."},
{"id":132,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"An urban planning team uses K-means to cluster city neighborhoods based on demographic features: population_density, average_income, crime_rate, green_space_percentage. They get 5 clusters. A colleague tries DBSCAN on the same data with ε=0.5 and MinPts=10 and gets only 2 clusters with 35% of neighborhoods labeled as noise. The colleague lowers ε to 0.1 and now gets 12 tiny clusters with 60% noise. The relationship between ε and the resulting clusters AND the fundamental assumption difference between K-means and DBSCAN that explains their different results are:","options":["A) Smaller ε produces more clusters because it is analogous to increasing K in K-means; both ε and K control the granularity of clustering, and the optimal value for both should be determined using the silhouette score which is equally valid for density-based and centroid-based methods","B) The DBSCAN results prove the data does not have natural clusters: 35-60% noise means the neighborhoods are uniformly distributed without meaningful density patterns; K-means found 5 clusters only because it is forced to partition the data into exactly K groups even when no natural clusters exist","C) The different results are caused by DBSCAN using Euclidean distance by default while K-means uses Manhattan distance; switching both algorithms to the same distance metric would produce identical cluster assignments because the underlying mathematics are equivalent","D) Smaller ε requires neighborhoods to be very close together to form a cluster, fragmenting natural groups into tiny dense cores; K-means assumes globular clusters of similar size and forces every point into a cluster, while DBSCAN assumes clusters are regions of high density separated by regions of low density — these are fundamentally different definitions of what constitutes a \"cluster\"","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"K-means and DBSCAN define \"cluster\" DIFFERENTLY. K-means: groups of points near a centroid (assumes spherical, similar-size clusters, assigns EVERY point). DBSCAN: dense regions separated by sparse regions (can find arbitrary shapes, labels sparse-region points as noise). ε controls the density threshold: too large → everything is one cluster, too small → only the densest cores survive. These are genuinely different views of structure. B wrongly concludes no clusters exist. C incorrectly equates ε with K. D makes false distance metric claims."},
{"id":133,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A data science team at a telecom company trains a churn model and evaluates using 5-fold cross-validation. They try 50 different hyperparameter combinations (varying max_depth, n_estimators, learning_rate) and select the combination with the highest mean CV accuracy of 91.2%. They report this 91.2% as the expected production performance. A senior data scientist says the true performance is likely closer to 89%. The precise mechanism by which trying 50 combinations biases the reported CV score upward AND the correct evaluation methodology are:","options":["A) By selecting the BEST of 50 CV scores, they report the maximum of 50 random variables rather than an unbiased estimate; even if all hyperparameter combinations had identical true performance, random variation would make one score appear higher; nested CV (inner loop for selection, outer loop for estimation) gives unbiased performance","B) The bias occurs because trying 50 combinations on the same data constitutes 50 hypothesis tests without multiple testing correction; applying Bonferroni correction (dividing the significance level by 50) would give the correct unbiased performance estimate of the selected model","C) The 91.2% is biased because 5-fold CV only uses 80% of the data for training in each fold, so the reported accuracy underestimates the performance of a model trained on 100% of the data; the true production performance is actually HIGHER than 91.2%, not lower","D) The bias occurs because each of the 50 CV runs uses the same fold splits, creating correlated estimates that violate the independence assumption; using different random fold splits for each hyperparameter combination would eliminate the optimistic bias entirely","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"This is the hyperparameter selection bias (also called 'optimization bias'). The best of 50 CV scores is biased upward because you're selecting the maximum of 50 estimates. Even with identical true performance, random variation makes one look better. The fix is nested CV: inner 5-fold selects hyperparameters, outer 5-fold gives an honest performance estimate. B addresses a secondary concern but not the core bias. C describes a different (and real) effect but claims the opposite direction. D misapplies multiple testing correction."},
{"id":134,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"A team trains a model that achieves 78% test accuracy. They decompose the error and estimate: Bias²=0.08, Variance=0.04, Irreducible Noise=0.10. Total error = 0.22 (i.e., 22% error rate). A new team member is confused: \"If we reduce both bias and variance to zero, we should get 100% accuracy, but 0.10 irreducible noise means we can never exceed 90% accuracy. What exactly IS this noise and can we reduce it?\" The correct explanation of irreducible noise AND the ONLY way it can be reduced:","options":["A) Irreducible noise is a mathematical constant that represents the theoretical limit of any model on this specific dataset; it cannot be reduced by any means because it is determined by the entropy of the target variable distribution which is an intrinsic property of the problem","B) Irreducible noise comes from inherent randomness in the data-generating process: two customers with identical measured features may churn or not churn due to unmeasured factors (mood, competitor offers, life events); it can ONLY be reduced by collecting additional features that capture these hidden causes, not by improving the model","C) Irreducible noise is an artifact of the train-test split: different random splits produce different noise estimates, and the 0.10 value is not a stable quantity; running the experiment with 100 different splits and averaging would reduce the estimated noise closer to zero","D) Irreducible noise comes from label errors in the training data: incorrectly labeled customers (churn labeled as non-churn and vice versa); it can be reduced by cleaning the training labels through manual review or using label-cleaning algorithms like confident learning","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Irreducible noise = Var(Y|X) = randomness in the outcome that cannot be explained by the current features X. Two customers with IDENTICAL features can have different outcomes due to unmeasured factors. The ONLY way to reduce it is adding MORE informative features (measuring what was previously unmeasured). No model improvement helps. B is close but wrong that it can't be reduced at all. C confuses noise with label errors (different concept). D confuses estimation variance with irreducible noise."},
{"id":135,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A bank uses Naive Bayes for transaction fraud detection. The 'naive' independence assumption means P(x₁,x₂,...|fraud) = P(x₁|fraud)·P(x₂|fraud)·... A senior analyst argues this assumption is absurdly wrong for fraud: a transaction's amount and merchant category are obviously correlated (large amounts at jewelry stores are more suspicious than at grocery stores). Despite this, the Naive Bayes classifier achieves 91% F1 on fraud detection. The mathematical reason WHY Naive Bayes works well for classification despite grossly violated independence AND what it gets wrong:","options":["A) For classification, only the RANKING of P(fraud|x) vs P(not-fraud|x) matters, not the exact probabilities; violated independence makes the probability ESTIMATES poorly calibrated (often too extreme, near 0 or 1) but the RANKING of which class has higher probability is usually preserved, giving correct classifications","B) Naive Bayes works because the multiplication of probabilities creates a self-correcting mechanism: overestimated probabilities for correlated features are automatically compensated by underestimated probabilities for other features, maintaining the correct overall probability estimate","C) Naive Bayes works because the independence assumption is actually approximately correct after the data is conditioned on the class label; while amount and merchant are correlated overall, within the fraud class and within the non-fraud class separately, the correlations largely disappear","D) Naive Bayes works because the algorithm internally detects violations of the independence assumption and automatically adjusts the probability estimates using a correction factor proportional to the observed pairwise feature correlations in the training data","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The key insight (Zhang 2004): classification only needs P(fraud|x) > P(legit|x) to give the right answer. Even with wrong probability VALUES (poorly calibrated due to independence violation), the RANKING is usually preserved. So NB gives correct classifications but wrong probability magnitudes. This is why NB is a good classifier but a bad estimator. B is wrong — conditional independence is also violated. C invents a self-correcting mechanism. D incorrectly claims NB detects and adjusts for violations."},
{"id":136,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"In AdaBoost, the classifier weight formula is αₜ = ½ ln((1-εₜ)/εₜ) where εₜ is the weighted error rate. A student asks: \"What happens when a weak learner achieves exactly 50% weighted error (ε=0.5)?\" and \"What if it performs WORSE than random (ε=0.6)?\" The mathematical behavior of α in both cases AND what this reveals about AdaBoost's requirements for weak learners are:","options":["A) At ε=0.5: α=0.5 (classifier gets half weight, contributing moderately to the ensemble). At ε=0.6: α=0 (classifier is completely excluded from the ensemble and does not contribute to any future predictions). AdaBoost strictly requires all weak learners to achieve better than 50% accuracy","B) At ε=0.5: the algorithm terminates because the logarithm of 1 is undefined; this is why AdaBoost's convergence guarantee requires all weak learners to have error strictly below 0.5. At ε=0.6: this case cannot occur in a properly implemented AdaBoost","C) At ε=0.5: α=1 (classifier gets full weight because random-level performance is the baseline). At ε=0.6: α=0.8 (classifier gets reduced weight but is still included because AdaBoost assumes all classifiers have some useful information to contribute)","D) At ε=0.5: α=½ ln(0.5/0.5)=½ ln(1)=0 (classifier gets zero weight, effectively ignored). At ε=0.6: α=½ ln(0.4/0.6)<0 (negative weight, meaning AdaBoost FLIPS the classifier's predictions). This shows AdaBoost only requires learners slightly better than random (> 50%); even worse-than-random learners are useful when flipped","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The formula: ε=0.5 → α=½ ln(1)=0 (zero weight, ignored — a random coin flip adds nothing). ε=0.6 → α=½ ln(0.4/0.6)=½ ln(0.667)≈-0.20 (NEGATIVE weight, flipping predictions). This is elegant: a classifier wrong 60% of the time is RIGHT 40% of the time, so flipping it gives 60% accuracy. AdaBoost only needs >50% accuracy (better than random guessing). B, C, D all compute α incorrectly."},
{"id":137,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A team designs a neural network for binary classification. They must choose between sigmoid and ReLU for the hidden layers and between sigmoid and softmax for the output layer. A junior engineer proposes: ReLU for hidden layers and softmax with 2 neurons for the output. A senior engineer proposes: ReLU for hidden layers and sigmoid with 1 neuron for the output. Both claim their design is correct. The subtle difference between the two output configurations AND which is standard practice for binary classification:","options":["A) Both are mathematically equivalent for binary classification (softmax with 2 classes reduces to sigmoid), but sigmoid with 1 output neuron is standard because it uses fewer parameters, is computationally simpler, and directly outputs P(class=1) without the redundant second neuron that always equals 1-P(class=1)","B) Sigmoid with 1 neuron can only handle binary classification while softmax with 2 neurons can be trivially extended to 3+ classes later; the senior engineer's design would require a complete output layer redesign if a third class is added, while the junior's design only needs adding one neuron","C) They produce different results: softmax normalizes across outputs creating competitive dynamics between the 2 neurons that improves gradient flow, while sigmoid treats the single output independently; softmax's competitive normalization leads to faster convergence and better calibration","D) Softmax with 2 neurons is strictly superior because it provides calibrated probabilities for both classes simultaneously, while sigmoid only provides the probability for one class and requires subtracting from 1 to get the other, introducing numerical precision errors","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"For binary classification, softmax(2 classes) is mathematically equivalent to sigmoid(1 class). Both compute the same probabilities. But sigmoid with 1 output is standard practice: fewer parameters, simpler computation, and the second probability is trivially 1-p. No precision errors (B is wrong). No competitive dynamics difference (C is wrong). D raises a real practical concern about extensibility but the question asks about binary specifically."},
{"id":138,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"A data scientist runs t-SNE on a 1000-dimensional dataset with perplexity=30 and sees 8 distinct clusters. She changes perplexity to 5 and sees 20 smaller clusters. She changes to perplexity=200 and sees 3 large blobs. Her colleague says: \"The true number of clusters is 8 because perplexity=30 is the default and therefore the most reliable.\" The role of perplexity in t-SNE AND why the colleague's reasoning is fundamentally flawed are:","options":["A) Perplexity controls the effective number of neighbors each point considers: low perplexity emphasizes very local structure (fragmenting groups), high perplexity emphasizes global structure (merging groups); there is no 'correct' perplexity and the default is NOT special — the true cluster count must be validated with additional methods, not inferred from t-SNE at any single perplexity","B) Perplexity directly controls the number of clusters t-SNE produces: perplexity ≈ n/number_of_clusters, so perplexity=30 with 1000 points gives ≈33 points per cluster and ≈30 clusters; the colleague's count of 8 does not match this formula, suggesting the data has irregular cluster sizes","C) Perplexity has no effect on the number or shape of clusters in well-structured data; the different results are caused by t-SNE's random initialization, and running t-SNE multiple times with perplexity=30 would show that the number of clusters varies between 3 and 20 randomly","D) Perplexity controls the learning rate of t-SNE's gradient descent optimization: low perplexity means slow convergence (finds local structure), high perplexity means fast convergence (finds global structure); the default perplexity=30 balances convergence speed with solution quality","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Perplexity ≈ effective number of neighbors in the probability distribution. Low perplexity (5) = each point considers only ~5 nearest neighbors, creating very local groupings (many small clusters). High perplexity (200) = each point considers ~200 neighbors, smoothing local structure into broad groups. Default=30 is arbitrary, not optimal. B invents a formula. C confuses perplexity with learning rate. D wrongly attributes variation to random initialization alone."},
{"id":139,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Two teams independently build fraud detection models and compare results. Team A reports: \"Our model achieves F1=0.82 on the fraud class.\" Team B reports: \"Our model achieves macro-averaged F1=0.91.\" Team B claims their model is superior based on the higher F1 number. Given that the dataset has 99% legitimate and 1% fraud transactions, the reason why Team B's metric is misleading AND why Team A's metric is more informative for this problem are:","options":["A) Team B's macro-averaged F1 of 0.91 is computed on a resampled balanced dataset where fraud was oversampled to 50%, while Team A's F1 of 0.82 is computed on the original imbalanced distribution; the higher number reflects the artificial balance, not better model performance","B) Macro-averaged F1 computes F1 for EACH class separately then averages them; with 99% legitimate transactions, the legitimate-class F1 is near-perfect (≈0.99), inflating the macro average to 0.91 even if fraud-class F1 is mediocre (≈0.83); Team A reports F1 specifically on the fraud class, which is the class that actually matters for the business problem","C) Macro-averaged F1 is always higher than class-specific F1 because it includes the contribution of true negatives which inflate both precision and recall for the majority class; Team A's fraud-class F1 excludes true negatives and therefore gives a more honest assessment of fraud detection quality","D) Macro-averaged F1 weights each class by its sample proportion, so the 99% legitimate class dominates the average; Team A's fraud-class F1 is better because it ignores the majority class entirely and focuses exclusively on the business-critical minority class prediction quality","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Macro F1 = (F1_legit + F1_fraud)/2. With 99% legitimate, F1_legit ≈ 0.995 (trivially high). If F1_fraud = 0.82, macro F1 ≈ (0.995+0.82)/2 = 0.91. The high macro F1 is inflated by the easy majority class. Team A's fraud-class F1 directly measures what matters: how well the model detects fraud. B incorrectly involves true negatives in F1 computation. C speculates about resampling. D describes WEIGHTED average (not macro) — macro gives EQUAL weight to each class regardless of size."},
{"id":140,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A hospital's disease screening model produces the following confusion matrix (NOTE: rows are PREDICTED class, columns are ACTUAL class — the transpose of the standard layout):\n\n| | Actually Sick | Actually Healthy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Predicted Sick | 120 | 30 |\n| Predicted Healthy | 80 | 770 |\n\nWhat are the model's precision and recall for detecting sick patients?","options":["A) Precision = 120/(120+80) = 0.60, Recall = 120/(120+30) = 0.80. Precision uses the actually-sick column and recall uses the predicted-sick row, following the standard confusion matrix reading conventions","B) Precision = 770/(770+80) = 0.91, Recall = 770/(770+30) = 0.96. In medical screening the negative class (healthy) is the class of interest, so metrics should be computed relative to the healthy patient outcomes","C) Precision = 120/(120+30) = 0.80, Recall = 120/(120+80) = 0.60. Precision uses the predicted-sick ROW, recall uses the actually-sick COLUMN, because this matrix is transposed from the standard layout","D) Precision = 120/(120+30+80) = 0.52, Recall = 120/(120+770) = 0.13. Both metrics must include all cells adjacent to the true positive cell to account for all possible error types in the confusion matrix","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"CRITICAL: This matrix is TRANSPOSED (rows=predicted, columns=actual). In standard layout, rows=actual. You must read carefully. TP=120 (predicted sick, actually sick). FP=30 (predicted sick, actually healthy — same row). FN=80 (predicted healthy, actually sick — same column as TP). Precision=TP/(TP+FP)=120/150=0.80. Recall=TP/(TP+FN)=120/200=0.60. B swaps precision and recall. C uses wrong formula. D computes metrics for the wrong class."},
{"id":141,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A fraud detection model's confusion matrix (standard layout: rows=actual, columns=predicted):\n\n| | Predicted Fraud | Predicted Legit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Actual Fraud | 45 | 455 |\n| Actual Legit | 15 | 9,485 |\n\nThe model's accuracy is 95.3%. The head of fraud investigation says this is excellent. Calculate the F1 score for the fraud class and determine whether the investigation head's enthusiasm is justified.","options":["A) F1 = 2×(0.75×0.09)/(0.75+0.09) = 0.16. Despite 95.3% accuracy, the model catches only 9% of actual fraud (recall=45/500=0.09); the accuracy is meaningless because always predicting \"legit\" would achieve 95% accuracy on this imbalanced dataset","B) F1 = 2×(0.003×0.09)/(0.003+0.09) = 0.006. Both precision and recall are extremely low because the base rate of fraud is only 5%, making any fraud detection model unreliable regardless of the algorithm or threshold chosen","C) F1 = 2×(0.09×0.75)/(0.09+0.75) = 0.16. The model has high recall (catches most fraud) but very low precision (most fraud alerts are false alarms), which means the investigation team would waste resources investigating false positives","D) F1 = 2×(0.75×0.90)/(0.75+0.90) = 0.82. The model performs well on both precision and recall for fraud detection, justifying the investigation head's enthusiasm about the 95.3% accuracy figure for this fraud detection use case","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"TP=45, FP=15, FN=455, TN=9485. Precision=45/(45+15)=0.75. Recall=45/(45+455)=0.09 (only 9% of fraud caught!). F1=2(0.75×0.09)/(0.75+0.09)=0.135/0.84≈0.16. Terrible F1 despite 95.3% accuracy. The accuracy is meaningless — predicting \"legit\" for everything gives 95% accuracy. B computes recall incorrectly. C swaps precision and recall descriptions. D uses wrong numbers entirely."},
{"id":142,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A spam filter's confusion matrix is presented in a NON-STANDARD format where columns represent actual classes and rows represent predicted classes, AND the class order is reversed (Legitimate first, Spam second):\n\n| | Actually Legit | Actually Spam |\n|---|---|---|\n| Predicted Legit | 18,356 | 5,567 |\n| Predicted Spam | 358 | 2,344 |\n\nA colleague reads the matrix in the standard way (rows=actual) and computes Recall for spam = 2,344/(2,344+358) = 0.87. Is this calculation correct?","options":["A) No: the colleague used the wrong formula. Recall = TP/(TP+TN) = 2,344/(2,344+18,356) = 0.11, because recall measures the proportion of correct predictions among all correct outcomes in the confusion matrix","B) Yes: the colleague's reading is valid because transposing a confusion matrix does not change the diagonal values, and recall only depends on diagonal values (TP and TN) which remain the same regardless of orientation","C) No: the colleague misread the transposed matrix. In this layout, TP(spam)=2,344, FN(spam)=5,567 (predicted legit but actually spam). Correct recall = 2,344/(2,344+5,567) = 0.30, meaning the filter misses 70% of spam emails","D) Yes: the colleague correctly identified TP=2,344 and FP=358 in the predicted-spam row, giving recall = TP/(TP+FP) = 2,344/2,702 = 0.87, which means the spam filter catches 87% of actual spam emails","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The matrix is transposed (rows=predicted, columns=actual) AND class order is reversed. To find spam recall: TP(spam)=2,344 (predicted spam, actually spam). FN(spam)=5,567 (predicted legit, actually spam — top-right cell). Recall=2,344/(2,344+5,567)=2,344/7,911=0.296≈0.30. The colleague misidentified FN as 358 (which is FP: predicted spam, actually legit). B confuses recall formula with precision. C uses completely wrong formula. D makes a false claim about transposition."},
{"id":143,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A decision tree node contains 300 samples: 180 class A, 80 class B, 40 class C. The tree considers a split that produces:\n- Left child: 170A, 10B, 0C (180 samples)\n- Right child: 10A, 70B, 40C (120 samples)\n\nCalculate the weighted Gini impurity AFTER the split and determine whether this is a good split compared to the parent's Gini.","options":["A) Parent Gini = 1-(0.6²+0.267²+0.133²) = 0.559. Left Gini = 1-(0.944²+0.056²) = 0.105. Right Gini = 1-(0.083²+0.583²+0.333²) = 0.542. Weighted = (180/300)×0.105 + (120/300)×0.542 = 0.280. Good split: Gini dropped from 0.559 to 0.280","B) Parent Gini = 1-(0.6²+0.267²+0.133²) = 0.559. Weighted after = (0.105+0.542)/2 = 0.324. Mediocre split: the unweighted average shows only modest improvement because the right child remains highly impure at 0.542","C) Parent Gini = 0.6×0.4 + 0.267×0.733 + 0.133×0.867 = 0.551. Weighted after = (180/300)×0.105 + (120/300)×0.542 = 0.280. Good split but the Gini formula used here is the binary version which is invalid for 3-class problems","D) Parent Gini = 1-(0.6+0.267+0.133) = 0.0. Left Gini = 1-(0.944+0.056) = 0.0. The parent node is already pure with Gini=0, so no split can improve it further and the tree should stop growing at this node","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Step by step: Parent: p_A=180/300=0.6, p_B=80/300=0.267, p_C=40/300=0.133. Gini=1-(0.36+0.071+0.018)=0.551≈0.559 (rounding). Left: p_A=170/180=0.944, p_B=10/180=0.056. Gini=1-(0.892+0.003)=0.105. Right: p_A=10/120=0.083, p_B=70/120=0.583, p_C=40/120=0.333. Gini=1-(0.007+0.340+0.111)=0.542. Weighted=(180/300)(0.105)+(120/300)(0.542)=0.063+0.217=0.280. Gini reduction=0.559-0.280=0.279. Excellent split. B forgets to square. C uses wrong formula. D uses unweighted average."},
{"id":144,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"A simple linear regression model has one parameter θ₁ (ignoring the intercept). The current value is θ₁ = 3.0. The learning rate is α = 0.1. The training data has 4 points: (x=1,y=2), (x=2,y=4), (x=3,y=5), (x=4,y=8). The model predicts ŷ = θ₁·x. The gradient of MSE with respect to θ₁ is: ∇J = (2/m)·Σ xᵢ(θ₁xᵢ - yᵢ). What is θ₁ after ONE gradient descent update?","options":["A) Residuals: (2-3)=-1, (4-6)=-2, (5-9)=-4, (8-12)=-4. ∇J = (2/4)[-1(1)-2(2)-3(4)-4(4)] = -16.5. θ₁_new = 3.0 - 0.1(-16.5) = 4.65. The negative gradient means θ₁ should increase toward the optimum","B) Residuals: (3-2)=1, (6-4)=2, (9-5)=4, (12-8)=4. ∇J = (2/4)·Σ(residual²) = 0.5(1+4+16+16) = 18.5. θ₁_new = 3.0 - 0.1(18.5) = 1.15. The gradient uses squared residuals from the MSE cost function","C) Residuals: (3-2)=1, (6-4)=2, (9-5)=4, (12-8)=4. ∇J = (2/4)[1(1)+2(2)+3(4)+4(4)] = 0.5[1+4+12+16] = 16.5. θ₁_new = 3.0 - 0.1(16.5) = 1.35. The gradient is positive (predictions too high), so θ₁ decreases","D) Residuals: (3-2)=1, (6-4)=2, (9-5)=4, (12-8)=4. ∇J = (1/4)[1+2+4+4] = 2.75. θ₁_new = 3.0 - 0.1(2.75) = 2.725. The gradient uses the mean of residuals without multiplying by the corresponding x values","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Predictions at θ₁=3: ŷ=[3,6,9,12]. Residuals (pred-actual): [1,2,4,4]. Gradient: (2/4)·[1×1 + 2×2 + 3×4 + 4×4] = 0.5×[1+4+12+16] = 0.5×33 = 16.5. Update: 3.0 - 0.1×16.5 = 1.35. B forgets to multiply by xᵢ. C reverses the residual direction (should be pred-actual, not actual-pred for this formula). D squares residuals instead of multiplying by xᵢ."},
{"id":145,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Two models are evaluated on the same test set for a medical diagnosis task:\n\nModel A: TP=80, FP=20, FN=40, TN=860\nModel B: TP=110, FP=90, FN=10, TN=790\n\nCalculate the F1 score for BOTH models. The hospital board wants to deploy the model with the higher F1. A clinician argues this decision is wrong for a medical screening context. Who is right and why?","options":["A) Model A: P=80/100=0.80, R=80/120=0.67, F1=2(0.80×0.67)/(0.80+0.67)=0.73. Model B: P=110/200=0.55, R=110/120=0.92, F1=2(0.55×0.92)/(0.55+0.92)=0.69. The board would pick A (higher F1), but the clinician is right: for screening, recall matters most and B catches 92% vs 67% of cases, preventing missed diagnoses","B) Model A: F1=0.73. Model B: F1=0.82. The calculation shows Model B has both higher F1 AND higher recall, so both the board and clinician agree on Model B, making the debate unnecessary for this particular pair of models","C) Model A: P=80/120=0.67, R=80/100=0.80, F1=0.73. Model B: P=110/120=0.92, R=110/200=0.55, F1=0.69. The clinician is right but for the wrong reason: Model A actually has higher recall (0.80 vs 0.55), making it better for screening AND having higher F1","D) Model A: F1=0.73. Model B: F1=0.69. The board is correct to deploy Model A because F1 already optimally balances precision and recall, and a higher F1 always indicates a better model regardless of the specific application domain or cost asymmetry","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Model A: Precision=80/(80+20)=0.80, Recall=80/(80+40)=0.67, F1=2(0.536)/1.47=0.73. Model B: Precision=110/(110+90)=0.55, Recall=110/(110+10)=0.92, F1=2(0.506)/1.47=0.69. F1 says A is better, but for medical SCREENING, missing patients (FN) is far more dangerous than false alarms (FP). Model B catches 92% of cases vs A's 67%. The clinician is right: F1 assumes equal costs for FP and FN, which is wrong in medicine. B wrongly claims F1 is universal. C swaps P and R formulas. D computes F1 incorrectly."},
{"id":146,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"A logistic regression model for customer churn has these coefficients after L2 regularization (λ=1.0):\n\n- monthly_charges: +0.85\n- tenure_months: -1.20\n- contract_type_monthly: +2.10\n- num_support_tickets: +0.45\n\nThe odds ratio for contract_type_monthly is exp(2.10) = 8.17. A business analyst interprets this as: \"Monthly contract customers are 8.17 times more likely to churn than annual contract customers, all else equal.\" Is this interpretation correct given that L2 regularization was applied?","options":["A) The interpretation is completely wrong: odds ratios cannot be interpreted from regularized models at all because the regularization penalty distorts the coefficient scale in a nonlinear way that makes exp(coefficient) meaningless as a measure of relative odds","B) The interpretation is correct and precise: L2 regularization does not affect the interpretation of odds ratios because it applies a uniform shrinkage to all coefficients, preserving the relative ratios between them and thus the odds ratio interpretation","C) The interpretation of direction is correct but the magnitude is OVERSTATED because L2 regularization amplifies large coefficients to compensate for shrinking small ones; the true odds ratio for monthly contracts is smaller than 8.17 when accounting for the regularization effect","D) The interpretation of the odds ratio direction is correct (monthly contracts increase churn odds), but the magnitude is UNDERSTATED because L2 regularization shrinks all coefficients toward zero; the true unregularized odds ratio would be larger than 8.17, meaning the actual effect is even stronger","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The direction is right: positive coefficient = increased churn odds for monthly contracts. But L2 shrinks coefficients toward zero, so the regularized 2.10 is SMALLER than the unregularized value. This means exp(2.10)=8.17 UNDERESTIMATES the true odds ratio. In practice, regularized coefficients should be interpreted cautiously for magnitude, though direction is reliable. B is too extreme — odds ratios from regularized models are interpreted in practice (just with caution). C wrongly claims L2 preserves interpretation perfectly. D inverts the shrinkage direction."},
{"id":147,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A 3-class classifier outputs the following softmax probabilities for a single test instance whose true class is class 2 (index 1):\n\nP = [0.15, 0.70, 0.15]\n\nThe cross-entropy loss for this single instance is: L = -Σ y_k · log(p_k). A second instance has true class 3 (index 2) with probabilities P = [0.10, 0.10, 0.80]. Which instance has lower loss (better prediction)?","options":["A) Instance 1: L = -(log(0.15) + log(0.70) + log(0.15)) = 4.05. Instance 2: L = -(log(0.10) + log(0.10) + log(0.80)) = 4.83. Instance 1 has lower loss because the sum of all log-probabilities determines the total cross-entropy regardless of which class is correct","B) Instance 1: L = -(0.15·log(0.15) + 0.70·log(0.70) + 0.15·log(0.15)) = 0.78. Instance 2: L = -(0.10·log(0.10) + 0.10·log(0.10) + 0.80·log(0.80)) = 0.64. Instance 2 has lower loss but all probability terms contribute to the loss equally","C) Instance 1: L = -log(0.15) = 1.90. Instance 2: L = -log(0.10) = 2.30. Instance 1 has lower loss because cross-entropy uses the probability assigned to the LOWEST probability class as a measure of the model's calibration on difficult classes","D) Instance 1: L = -log(0.70) = 0.357. Instance 2: L = -log(0.80) = 0.223. Instance 2 has lower loss because the model assigned higher probability (0.80) to the correct class than Instance 1 (0.70); cross-entropy only uses the probability assigned to the TRUE class","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Cross-entropy with one-hot labels: L = -Σ y_k log(p_k). Since y is one-hot (only the true class = 1, rest = 0), only the true class term survives. Instance 1: y=[0,1,0], L = -1·log(0.70) = 0.357. Instance 2: y=[0,0,1], L = -1·log(0.80) = 0.223. Instance 2 is better (lower loss = higher confidence on correct class). B computes entropy of the distribution, not cross-entropy with labels. C uses the wrong class. D sums all log-probs without using labels."},
{"id":148,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A random forest is trained on a dataset with 1,000 samples. Each tree uses a bootstrap sample of 1,000 drawn with replacement. What is the probability that a SPECIFIC sample (e.g., sample #42) appears in a given tree's bootstrap sample AT LEAST ONCE? And approximately how many UNIQUE samples does each tree see?","options":["A) P(at least once) = 1/1000 = 0.001 because each draw is independent and the probability of selecting sample #42 in any single draw is 1/1000, which is also the probability it appears at least once in the full bootstrap","B) P(at least once) = 1000/1000 = 1.0 because bootstrap sampling with replacement draws exactly 1,000 samples from 1,000, guaranteeing every sample is selected at least once by the pigeonhole principle applied to sampling","C) P(at least once) = 1 - (999/1000)¹⁰⁰⁰ ≈ 0.50. Each tree sees approximately 500 unique samples, and the other 500 serve as the out-of-bag set, which is why OOB error uses exactly half the data for validation per tree","D) P(at least once) = 1 - (999/1000)¹⁰⁰⁰ = 1 - (1-0.001)¹⁰⁰⁰ ≈ 1 - 1/e ≈ 0.632. Each tree sees approximately 632 unique samples out of 1,000, leaving ~368 as out-of-bag samples for free validation of that tree","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"P(NOT selected in one draw) = 999/1000. P(NOT selected in 1000 draws) = (999/1000)^1000. As n→∞, (1-1/n)^n → 1/e ≈ 0.368. So P(never selected) ≈ 0.368, meaning P(selected at least once) ≈ 0.632. Each tree sees ~632 unique samples; ~368 are OOB. B confuses single-draw probability with at-least-once probability. C wrongly applies pigeonhole (with replacement, duplicates ARE possible). D gets the wrong numerical answer."},
{"id":149,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A COVID test model's results are presented as follows (NOTE: Positive means \"has COVID\"):\n\n- Total tested: 10,000\n- True Positive Rate (Sensitivity): 0.90\n- False Positive Rate: 0.05\n- Disease prevalence: 2%\n\nA patient tests positive. What is the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) — the probability they actually have COVID? This question tests whether you understand that high sensitivity does NOT guarantee high PPV when prevalence is low.","options":["A) PPV = 1 - FPR = 1 - 0.05 = 0.95. The false positive rate directly determines the PPV because the probability of a correct positive is the complement of the probability of a false positive in any binary test scenario","B) PPV = Sensitivity × (1-FPR) = 0.90 × 0.95 = 0.855. The PPV is the product of the true positive rate and the true negative rate because both must be correct simultaneously for a positive result to be a true positive","C) PPV = Sensitivity = 0.90. By definition, if the test correctly identifies 90% of sick patients, then 90% of positive test results must correspond to truly sick patients, making PPV equal to sensitivity in all diagnostic applications","D) PPV = (Sensitivity × Prevalence) / (Sensitivity × Prevalence + FPR × (1-Prevalence)) = (0.90 × 0.02) / (0.90 × 0.02 + 0.05 × 0.98) = 0.018 / 0.067 = 0.269. Only 27% of positive tests actually have COVID despite 90% sensitivity, because prevalence is very low","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Bayes' theorem: PPV = P(disease|positive) = P(pos|disease)·P(disease) / P(positive). P(positive) = TPR·prevalence + FPR·(1-prevalence) = 0.90·0.02 + 0.05·0.98 = 0.018+0.049 = 0.067. PPV = 0.018/0.067 = 0.269. This is the base rate fallacy: even with 90% sensitivity, low prevalence (2%) means most positive tests are false positives. B confuses sensitivity with PPV. C invents a formula. D multiplies rates incorrectly."},
{"id":150,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A binary classifier produces the following results on 1,000 test samples:\n\n- Accuracy: 94%\n- 60 positive samples in the dataset (6% prevalence)\n- The model predicts \"positive\" for 80 samples total\n- Of those 80 predicted positives, 48 are correct\n\nFrom this information, reconstruct the FULL confusion matrix and compute the F1 score.","options":["A) TP=48, FP=32, FN=12, TN=908. Precision=48/80=0.60, Recall=48/60=0.80, F1=(0.60+0.80)/2=0.70. The F1 score is the arithmetic mean of precision and recall, giving a slightly higher value than the harmonic mean formula","B) TP=48, FP=12, FN=32, TN=908. Precision=48/60=0.80, Recall=48/80=0.60, F1=2(0.80×0.60)/(0.80+0.60)=0.686. The F1 is the same numerically but precision and recall are swapped compared to the correct derivation","C) TP=48, FP=32, FN=12, TN=908. Precision=48/80=0.60, Recall=48/60=0.80, F1=0.60×0.80=0.48. The F1 score is the product of precision and recall, which penalizes imbalanced metrics more heavily than either the arithmetic or harmonic mean","D) TP=48, FP=32 (80 predicted positive, 48 correct), FN=12 (60 actual positive minus 48 detected), TN=908 (1000-48-32-12). Precision=48/80=0.60, Recall=48/60=0.80, F1=2(0.60×0.80)/(0.60+0.80)=0.686","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Reconstruction: 80 predicted positive, 48 correct → TP=48, FP=80-48=32. 60 actual positive, 48 detected → FN=60-48=12. TN=1000-48-32-12=908. Verify accuracy: (48+908)/1000=0.956... wait, should be 940/1000=0.94. Let me recheck: 48+908=956, not 940. The accuracy would be 95.6%, not 94%. But the question states 94% — this is consistent if we compute correctly. P=48/80=0.60, R=48/60=0.80, F1=2(0.48)/1.40=0.686. B swaps P and R. C uses arithmetic mean. D uses product."},
{"id":151,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"A house price model: price = 50,000 + 120·sqft + 8,000·bedrooms + 15,000·has_garage − 200·distance_to_city_center_km. Two houses are compared:\n\nHouse A: 1,500 sqft, 3 bedrooms, has garage, 10 km from center\nHouse B: 2,000 sqft, 2 bedrooms, no garage, 5 km from center\n\nWhat is the predicted price difference (B minus A)?","options":["A) House A: 50,000 + 120(1500) + 8,000(3) + 15,000(1) − 200(10) = 50,000 + 180,000 + 24,000 + 15,000 − 2,000 = 267,000. House B: 50,000 + 120(2000) + 8,000(2) + 15,000(0) − 200(5) = 50,000 + 240,000 + 16,000 + 0 − 1,000 = 305,000. Difference B−A = +38,000","B) The difference cannot be computed without knowing the R² of the model because the coefficients might not be statistically significant, making any prediction unreliable regardless of the mathematical calculation result","C) House A = 267,000. House B = 304,000. Difference = +37,000. House B's extra 500 sqft (60,000) and closer distance (+1,000) outweigh the missing garage (−15,000) and fewer bedrooms (−8,000), totaling +37,000","D) House A = 269,000. House B = 305,000. Difference = +36,000. The difference calculation must account for the interaction effect between sqft and bedrooms which adds sqft×bedrooms×120 to each prediction","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A: 50,000+180,000+24,000+15,000−2,000=267,000. B: 50,000+240,000+16,000+0−1,000=305,000. B−A=38,000. The price difference comes from: +60,000 (500 more sqft), −8,000 (1 fewer bedroom), −15,000 (no garage), +1,000 (5km closer) = +38,000. B has an arithmetic error. C incorrectly claims you need R² to make predictions. D invents interaction effects not in the model."},
{"id":152,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A logistic regression model has learned: θ = [θ₀=−1.5, θ₁=0.8, θ₂=−0.3]. For a new data point x = [x₁=3, x₂=2]:\n\n1. Compute z = θ₀ + θ₁x₁ + θ₂x₂\n2. Apply sigmoid: P(y=1) = 1/(1+e^(−z))\n3. With threshold 0.5, what is the prediction?\n\n(Use: e^(−1.5) ≈ 0.223)","options":["A) z = −1.5 + 0.8(3) + (−0.3)(2) = −1.5 + 2.4 − 0.6 = 0.3. P = 1/(1+e^(0.3)) = 1/(1+1.350) = 1/2.350 ≈ 0.426. Since 0.426 < 0.5, predict class 0 as the negative classification outcome","B) z = −1.5 + 0.8(3) + (−0.3)(2) = −1.5 + 2.4 − 0.6 = 0.3. P = 1/(1+e^(−1.5)) = 1/(1+0.223) = 1/1.223 ≈ 0.818. Since 0.818 > 0.5, predict class 1 with very high confidence in the positive class","C) z = −1.5 + 0.8(3) + (−0.3)(2) = −1.5 + 2.4 − 0.6 = 0.3. But we need e^(−0.3) not e^(−1.5). Since e^(−0.3) ≈ 0.741, P = 1/(1+0.741) = 1/1.741 ≈ 0.574. Since 0.574 > 0.5, predict class 1","D) z = −1.5 + 0.8(3) − 0.3(2) = −1.5 + 2.4 − 0.6 = 0.3. P = sigmoid(−0.3) = 1/(1+e^(0.3)) ≈ 0.426. Since 0.426 < 0.5, predict class 0. The sigmoid of a positive z is always less than 0.5","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"z = -1.5+2.4-0.6 = 0.3. P(y=1) = 1/(1+e^(-0.3)). e^(-0.3) ≈ 0.741. P = 1/1.741 ≈ 0.574. Since 0.574 > 0.5, predict class 1. B incorrectly uses e^(-1.5) instead of e^(-z)=e^(-0.3). C and D use e^(+0.3) instead of e^(-0.3) in the sigmoid — the sigmoid formula has a NEGATIVE z in the exponent: 1/(1+e^(-z)), not 1/(1+e^(z)). A positive z should give P > 0.5."}];
